      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES ANT) NEWS</p>
        <p>ELECTRIC LIGHT AND TIIE SKIN.</p>
        <p>The Academy of Medicine at the University vf Dresden has been conducting sonic .elabo- rate investigations into the charge that elec- tric light is ruinous to the skin. The conten- tion of electrical experts is that the human akin, if permanently emposed to electric 'light, deteriorates in whiteness and texture almost in the same degree as if exposed to X-rays. One of the experimenters maintains that the most beautiful skin subjected to the rays of an, arc light for a certain period will becomo rough and blotchy.</p>
        <p>NEW METHOD OF PRESERVING MILK. A German inventor has patented a process for preserving milk. It consists in removing all dissolved oxygen by means of the addition of a small quantity of ferrous carbonate. The process •is based on the fact that freshly-pre- cipitated ferrous carbonate in the presence of oxygen immediately a.ssiinilat,,n- oxygen and +evolves an equivalent quantity of carbon dioxide. One part of tarrous carbonate is sufficient for 50,000 parts, of milk, and .7 thc properties of the milk are not altered in any way- by the addition, which should be made -before the -milk is boiled.</p>
        <p>WHY PLEASURE REMOVES FATIGUE. Dr. Landone dwells on the value of culti- vating love, and cheerfulness, and good thoughts towards mankind. "For," he says, "the body is a substance composed .of chemi- cals and these chemicals are at the mercy of the emotions. Anger, hatred, and sorrow poison the fluids of the body, while love, cheerfulness, and happiness serve as elimina- tors of emotive, and fatigue poisons. Plea- sure stimulates, that is why When we are tired and worn an evening of gay dancing to music will remove all signs of fatigue and one will sleep better and be more rested than if iDile were to attempt to sleep fatigue off."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>CORDYCEPS</head>
        <p>THE CORDYCEPS</p>
        <p>A most remarkable life history is attri- buted to a fungus named cordyceps, found chiefly in .New Zealand. According to the Enirersity Correspondent. the minute spores of this fungus enter the bodies of large cater- pillars and germinate in the blood and diges- tive canal. EventL:ally the threads of the fungus destroy the Whole of the internal organs of the larva, which becomes a firm mass of fungus-tissue covered by the cater- pillar's skin. The dead larva is actually a, cast" in fungus-threads densely woven to- gether. The, fungus then sends up, usually from the head of the stuffed larva, its spore- producing branch; a rod-like structure often zbout Sin. lonz.</p>
        <p>NATURAL ROTATION IN FORESTS. Observations in India indicate that just as agricultural land, after mony repetitions of ,the same crops, becomes exhausted for that particular species, so in forest lands, after long periods, the seedlings may fail owing to changes in the soil, and new varieties may take -their place. Foresfs of blue pine in that country has e been observed od ar-tree tending to take the place of pine. In another case forests of pine and oak have been found, each invading and tending to take up the ground occupied by the other. Elsewhere in India spruce and silver fir have been found advancing iuto a failing oak forest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>SMOKE VERSUS SUNSHINE.</head>
        <p>SMOKE VERSUS SUNSHINE.</p>
        <p>That the smoke which rises from the chim- mays of a city in winter greatly diminishes the practical duration of sunlight, is fihewn by is comparison of observations made in the centre of London, at Kew, a few miles to the west, and at Gre2nwicli, a few miles to the east. The annual number of hours of sun- aline is at Kew 1,399, or 31 per cent, of the.. anaximum possible number; at the centre of London 1,027 hours,, or 23 per cent., and at Greenwich 1,227, or 27 per cent. Hence the centre of Lone.on enjoys 372 hours (8 per cent.) of sunshine less than Kew, and 200 (4 per cent.) less than Greenwich. In winter the deficiency of sunshine in the centre of London rises to 11 per cent. The disparity between Kew and Greenwich is due to the fact that the prevailing winds are wc.!Aerly, so that much of the City's smoke is blown over rean wich.. bui- very little over Kew. At</p>
        <p>Hamburg observations extenaing over eleven years shew an average annual numoer of 108 days without sunshine. At all seasons, but especially in winter, the air of Hamburg is filled with smaller or larger particles of soot. The result is that Hamburg annually enjoys only 1,236 hours of sunshine, or 28 per cent. of the maximum possible number, while Ber- lin enjoys 1,672 hours, or 37 per cent.</p>
        <p>NEW WAY TO IDENTIFY CRIMINALS. Dr. Tamassia has been studying the dorsal (back) veins of the howls as a means of per- sonal identification. These veins differ con- siderably not only in different people but also in the two hands of a single individual. The veins are rendered prominent either by let- ting the hand hang down for some time or by applying a ligature, and then the hands are photographed. The surface is a fairly large one, the differences considerable, easily photographed, and require no elaborate mea- surements or delicata apparatus to register; and even if one right hand may resemble an- other, it is pretty certain that a right and left hand of any given subject would</p>
        <p>DIFFER FROM ANY OTHER right and left hand. Dr. Tamassia does not disparage the merits of pertillon's method and Galton's finger-tip imprcFsions, but he believes insufficient attention has been directed to this simple method of identifica- tion by means of the dorsal veins of the hands. These veins, we are told in the Britieh Medical Journal, remain pretty much the same throughout life, and cannot be altered by disease or by intentional fraud, or at least cannot be altered in such a way as to impair their value as marks of identity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>MOVING BOGS.</head>
        <p>MOVING BOGS.</p>
        <p>The numerous peat•bogs of Ireland, occu- pying in all about one-seventh cf the entire country, are at once a source of wealth and of danger. A useful fuel under normal condi- tions, the peat may in certain circumstances become a devastating flood, engulfing in its course forme and houses, and spreading ruin over the labours of the farmer. To understand how such a disaster may happen it is neces- sary, remarks a Globe writer, to know some- thing of the nature of a peat bog. In many cases it is the last stage in the filling up of a lake. As the lake shallowed by thodeposition of mud, sand, &amp;c., vegetation. took possession of the edges and gradually spread over the surface. It became a marsh, and the accu- mulation of the growth of centuries of vege- tation formed the peat. The water which fed the lake finds its way between the imper- meable substratum and the layer of peat. Tho peat bog is but A LAKE WITH A SKIN OF PEAT.</p>
        <p>Or a peat bog may be formed in a depression rendered marshy by springs, or by the dam- ming back of a river, say by the falling of tree-trunks across its course. In any case, if th 3 water supply gets between the peat and the impermeable base the peat bog may be- come a source of danger. For it becomes, in fact, a peat bubble inflated by water. Any extra supply of water serves to inflate the bubble, and the bog rises in the centre. In Lithuania there are peat bogs rising as much as 50ft. in their- eeetral parts above the sur- rounding country. Under normal conditions the growing vegetation and evaporation are sufficient to dispose of the water without dan- gerous inflation of the bog. Excessive rain- fall and the sudden melting of snows may in- crease the tension to the breaking point, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>BOG MAY BURST.</head>
        <p>THE BOG MAY BURST.</p>
        <p>- The liquid peat escapes, and rolls down as a devastating flood. In some esszs a pent bog is provided with a natural safety-valve which renders such a disaster impossible. Tho streams which fed the old lake, instead of soaking in beneath the skin of peat,. have cut for themselves channels through it. Or the springs beneath come up through the peat instead of spreading beneath it. Thus, the surplus waters pass away without inflating the skin of peat. The question then arises, could not dangerous bogs be provided with artificial safety-valves? A cutting to carry off the sur- plus water without allowing it to soak be- neath the peat is all that Is required. •</p>
        <p>• In succession to Mr. Richardson. ,stationmaster at Newton, uho retired a few days ago, owing to the Company's age limit, Mr. Darroch, assistant at Lime- street, Liverpool, has been appointed. lie was formerly an inspector at Win, and is very highly spoken of by travellers on the line.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, February 13, 1909,</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, February 13, 1909,</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>tne satin in a particularly pleasing fast-lien. Inside comes a dainty full bodice of finest ivory tulle, the square-cut neck being finished by a band of sparkling jewelled trimming. Over the arms comes a loco drapery of the satin in lieu of sleeves. The skirt is perfectly plain, very long all round, and trained at the back.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL ctiLesrseesw7orer cviluryrcfheawnr cupeetiitcoiriso,irticiLlarwlsssouirne the promotors would rejoice in seeing keener competition FESTIVAL. in those classes. He could only say that viewed educa- tionally, the festival was one of the most valuable ' forms of work which was being done Joe illy. It was done without any hope of reward except that of seeing INTERESTING CONTESTS AT THE a general raising of the standard of musical taste and also an increase of ability in those who competed for PARR HALL. the prizes. He hoped the movement would increase in strength, and that the nromotors would be gratified by seeing that not only had their Jabours been successful in a musical direction, but that they had suffered no loss financially. (Applause.) AFTERNOON MEETING. PIANOFORTE SOLO COMPETITION. (AGE NOT TO!'EXCEED TWELVE YEMIS). Fifteen children entered for this contest, the test piece being "Rondo " (Ludwig Herman Fisher). The playing of the finalists—Miss Woolley, of Runcorn, Miss D. Kenyon, 9f Darwen and Master Hill, of Sutton Oak—was highly commented upon by Mr. Coleridge- Taylor, the keenness of the competition being evident from the fact that Miss Woolley was placed first with 18 marks (out of a possible 20), while Miss Kenyon and• Master Hill were bracketed equal second with 17 marks. The features of Miss Woolley's placing which called forth praise were the clearness and crispness of tone and touch, her interpretation being most interesting throughout.</p>
        <p>A feast of music, which would have done credit to any town, was provided at the Warrington Musical Festival held in the Parr Hall on Friday and Saturday last. Competitive festivals of this kind have made great headway throughout the country of late years, and Lancashire is playing a worthy part in a movement feaught with great possibilities to musical culture. Warrington can fairly claim to be a musical centre, and it is indeed gratifying that the "good old town " now boasts a festival of c_-,ood standing amongst the competitive gatherings in the North. This consumma- tion has not been achieved without considerable effort, and too much praise cannot be accorded to the Apollo Prize Choir, who were the pioneers of the movement in Warrington, and worked most unselfishly to pro- mote a successful annual eisteddfod. They, however, rightly felt that the matter was one for the towns- people generally, and the eisteddfod has given place to the Warrington Musical Festival/managed by a large and representative committee. Whatever the scoffers may say as to " pot hunting" and the like, there can be no question that these festivals encourage a love of elevating music, particularly amongst the working classes, and inspire those with talents to make the best use of them. It did cue good last week-end to see such large bands of earnest competitors, drawn from a wide area, al keen on victory, yet fully prepared to yield the palm to superior merit. From the musical standpoint the festival was a pronounced success. We hope the accounts, when completed, will be such as to relieve the promoters of anxiety on the score of finance. The subscription list, it was pleasing to note, was a considerable improvement on last year's, but the measure of support accorded cannot yet be 'considered by any means adevate for a town like Warrington. A new departure was adopted this year in setting apart Friday evening for local competitions, thus showing that the festival, while aiming at the encouragement of mnsic generally, seeks in a special degree to give Warrington aspirants an opportunity af gaining laurels on the platform. The contests were entirely creditable to the town and showed that Warrington can hold its own in the musical world. There was only one cause for disappointment,and that was that more church and chapel choirs did not enter, for one would hive thought that the second test piece —" The Radiant Morn" —at any rate, would not have been beyond the capacity of the average choir. We hope to see this section taken up with greater enthu- siasm in the , future. The crowuing event of the evening was the competition between Bold-street Wesleyan Choir (the holders) and Bewsey-roa 1 Wesleyan Choir, for possession of the challenge cup presented by Mr. James Dodds. Each choir sang , that fine descriptive piece "The Storm" with wonder- fully good effect,but Bewsey-road undoubtedly excelled and are to congratulated upon a well-deserved triumph. When Mr W. flipkiss,the pains taking con- ductor, came forward to receive the trophy, he was warmly cheered. An exceptionally interesting series of open events made up the programme for Saturday. The piece de resistance was the contest for male voice choirs which attracted the large entry of eleven. Enthusiasm was heightened by the fact that the two local choirs were among_the contestants, and each was certain to make a determined eff /rt to reach the top position. " Martyrs of the Arena," which hid been chosen for the test piece, is a highly dramatic composition, con- taining a prayer of great beauty, but to hear eleven 'choirs sing it in turn certainly borders on monotony. However, the maj /rity of the audience sat in patience , until the end and local pride was satisfied by the victory of the Warrington Male Voice Choir who gave a careful and most expressive interpretation. The Apollo Choir were not up to their usual good form and had to be content with fourth place. The evening meeting, for which the hall was crowded, witnessed the chief choral competition (mixed choirs) for the challenge shield presented by 1 Mrs. A. rr. Crosfield. Six choirs sang the massive chorus" Thanks be to God " (Mendelssohn) and the dainty and ear haunting part song" 0 happy Eyes" (Elgar) in praiseworthy style, but on the whole the competition did not reach the high standard of last year when several of the most famous choirs of the north competed. The festival is, however, fulfilling a very commend- able object when it encourages younger and less experienced choirs to develop their powers to the fullest extent. Stockton Heath Charal Union made their first public appearance and although they were not successful, the fact that they gained the third place showed that much useful work has already been done and that a bright future is evidently in store for the organization. Stretford Glee and Madrigal Society were awarded the first prize, and the decision met with general approval. The individual contests throughout the festival, both vocal and instrumental, were remarksbly interesting. A popular double victcry was that of Miss Josephine Saunders a blind young lady, of Manchester, who was successful in the challege solo as well as in the contralto solo competition. Her singing was characterised by soulfulness and emotion which touched everyone and when the Deputy-Mayor handed her the awards and spoke a few words of congratula- tion, the audience applauded to the echo. The committee were particularly happy in their choice of adjudicator, Mr. S. Coleridge-Taylor, a composer of world-wide renown, and Mr. Harry Evans. the gifted conductor of the Liverpool Welsh Choral Union, discharging their onerous ditties most admir- ably. They possess the happy knack of combining sound- ness of criticism with geniality of manner and they did their full share to ensure that everything should pass off without a hitch. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., and Mr. E. Wilkes acted as aceompanists. The Mayor (Alderman W. J. Porshaw) was unavoid- ably absent but his place was taken by , his worthy deputy (Alderman S:nethurst.) Mr. E. Lloyd Jones (chairman of the committee) and Mr. John Upson were capable conductors of Fridays and Saturdays meetings respectively. The arrangements had been well carried out by the following committee :—NIr. E. Lloyd Jones Clairman ; Mr. Nathan Cook, Vice-Chairman ; Messrs. H. Band, F. Pendlebnry, E. Iddon, W. Clay, P. L. Richardson, W. Knowles, A. Greenough, W. Woolsack F. E. Penketh. S. Hassan, E. Wilkes, IL 0. Hughes, J. E. Hannan, W. Critchley, G. Ratcliffe, J. W. Jowett, C. H. Dawson, A. Spruce, J. Lee, %V. Hipkiss, J. W. Willett, and W. H. Payton ; treasurer, Mr. Frank Hickman ; assistant secretary, Mr. Thomas Brough ; general secretary,llr.R.W. Cook. Special acknowledg- ment must be made of the secretarial work of Mr. R. W. C )ok which was cheerfully and most capably per- formed.</p>
        <p>SOPR X.NO SOLO.</p>
        <p>The victory of Miss Carrie Hastings, of Warrington, in this competition, proved most popular, the adjudi- cators awarding her 78 marks out of 80. Mr. Evans said it was not often they had such a treat on the com- petition platform. Miss Hastings had a charming and delightful voice, well suited to the piece, and her rendering of " Bid me discourse" (Bishop) was most artistic and clear. Hers was by fir the best perform- ance. The other finalists were Miss Jessie Brook, of Tyldesley, who obtained 73 marks, and Miss Holmes, of Widnes, 71 marks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION HOME.</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION HOME.</p>
        <p>THE OLD •AD THE NEW WAY.</p>
        <p>Old-time women, When marriage or love affairs turned out badly, eought refuge in a convent. The twentieth-century woman, says a writer in Woman's Life, soothes her heart-aches by making a militant much ado about nothing in public place&amp;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CHARMING LITTLB FROCK.</head>
        <p>A CHARMING LITTLB FROCK.</p>
        <p>Even the children's frocks are influenced by the all-pervading passion for Empire, effects, and the majority of the best new models are made either with a very short waist or have the bodice replaced by a deep yoke from which hangs the Empire skirt. The little frock in our illustration is a particularly pretty Empire design of somewhat--modified type. The original was carried out in soft, woollen-backed satin of a very pretty Nattiei- blue shade. Across the top of the frock came</p>
        <p>a wide ,straight band of the satin, very simply embroidered in silks to match, and bands of similarly embroidered satin formed the arm- holes. From the band across the front (and back) the skirt hung in an immensely wide box-pleat, flanked an either side by two ,nar- rower flat ploats. The front pleat was adorned with embroidered buttons finished with satin loops. Beneath *as worn a dainty, sleeved guimpe, entirely made of tucked Bre- ton net,' which was made detachable for washing purposes.</p>
        <p>, - , • ej ( . '7 - ) • cf/P • ' t p A !,\ p 111 • •„.„. , ir _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CLASSIC GOWNS AND THEIR WEARERS.</head>
        <p>CLASSIC GOWNS AND THEIR WEARERS.</p>
        <p>We become more and more classic as the time goes on, and the loveliness of the even- ing gown increases with every. month, says a fashion writer in the Globe. The best skill of Paris, London, and Vienna is devoted to evolving beautiful Combinations from old pic- tures, Greek statues, and Egyptian figures on ancient monuments. ' Given a good figure, and the effect of these is super-excellent, if a little startling in a modern drawing-room. The clingin,, embroideries and long skirt be- neath, I. ogeler with the absence of petti- coats, shows all the grace of the figure, and also, on the other hand, all the defects of im- perfection, emaciation, or adiposity. As none of us see ourselves as others see us, it. is diffi- cult to guess whether this very revealing fashion will suit us or not. Therefore, it behoves us to be careful in our choice. Awful examples were far from being few during last season, when the advent of the Directoire in- spired so many to choose a kind cf costume which made them .ridiculous. These present 0.1 a certain occasion last summer are un- likely to have forgotten two women who ma,de a sensation by the exaggerat:on of their attire, and who peaeocked up and down in a most remarkable manner with long trains trailing after them on the gross. One of the most charming of our pretty Countesses smiled as they passed her, end said to her deughter'a significant little word: "Barnyard."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL EVENING GOWN.</head>
        <p>A BEAUTIFUL EVENING GOWN.</p>
        <p>Pictured in our illustration is a particularly pretty and becoming evening gown of the very latest and most approved type. This charming frock is extremely simple in style, depending entirely for its .effect upon its bcpauty of material and cut. The material em- ployed is a handsome and beautifully soft fAtin of a particularly lovely tone of ivory, warm and rich without being in the least de- gree yellow. The bodice, of the Empire type, is " quite original in dc ign, and shews very</p>
        <p>little of the satin in either back or front, the satin being arranged in a sort of simple folded drapery over either shoulder and taken into the waistband on each side. This drapery is edged with a sl:aped piece of beau- tiful beavn Trinh cronbpt thmt is anDliaugi imon</p>
        <p>SCSI 4 0:11101,;1 47 ......rt, S. t t _,4,‘. - 104.17. ,-- ILL •': , ' „...4.75.13;.,,,....,.....„...... 1 1/ il i - 1 r.,.. -7-.1 ll / , 1 ...., Ni! r iiighli,;l /, ,I plod •' / It.. -- ipol,A I 0 ilk , I ,_1 • -1iA.,..,. —IT i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>TRUE 'WOMAN.</head>
        <p>THE TRUE 'WOMAN.</p>
        <p>The suffragette talks loudly of a woman's duty to the State.. But a woman who bears and trains healthy, moral, and sensible sons and daughters in a happy home is doing the noblest pcA•aible work for the State, declares Woman's Life. She is more of an Empire builder than if she were a militant M.P. TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>EGGS AND BREAD SAUCE All GRATIN.—BoiI four eggs for ten- minutes, and place in cold water. Then remove the shells and cut the eggs in thick slices. Take 2oz. of fine bread. crumbs and place them in a saucepan with a breakfastcupful and a-half of milk, an onion F-seled and cut in' quarters, and a little pepper and salt. Let the milk boil up, and then simmer for twelve to fifteen minutes, stirring continuously the whole time. Then lift out the onion and return the saucepan to the fire, adding half a gill of cream, a piece of butter the size of a walnut, and a little more salt and pepper if neeessary, together with a squeeze of lemon-juice. Grease a fire- proof dish, pour in a little of the bread sauce, lay the slices of ega on the top. and cover with the rest of the sauce. bprinkle fine bread-crumbs over the top, lay small pieces of butter here and there, and brown in tho oven before serving.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>BI.RONET RECOMMENDS ZAM-BUK.</head>
        <p>BI.RONET RECOMMENDS ZAM-BUK.</p>
        <p>Slr Canine Cave-Brown-Cave Proves the Balm Excellent for Hurts &amp; Sores.</p>
        <p>A remarkable tribute to the unquestionable superiority and world-wide fame of Zam-Buk has been received from Sir Geniile Cave-Brown-Cave, Bart., of Stretton Hall, Ashby-de-la-Zouch, LeiceAershire. Before coming info the family title and estate, some two years ago, Sir Genille bad spent the greater part of his life in search of adventure abroad. Australia, Egypt, America, India, Burmah and China, each in turn was explored by the young aristccrat. It naturally follows that during a career so crammed with adventure and danger to life and limb, the need for a perfect and reliable healing balm for the skin was ever present. Sir Geniile, after trying various things, always found this need admixabfy filled by Zam-Buk. Breaking through the reserve of his class, Sir Genille does not hesitate to give credit where credit is due, ar.d his spontaneous letter shows bow keenly Zam-Buk's wonderful healing power is appreciated in England's stateliest Mansions as well as in the humblest cottage. The letter reads : "Gentlemen, —I feel it is only my duty to let you know what great benefit I have derived by keeping Zam-Buk bandy and having it in constant use. For the sprains and bruises which I have incurred in my horsemanship and in my cowboy life generally, I have found it a reliable and speedy healer. Some of the best riders in the West that I know use Zam-Buk regularly with the greatest benefit. I may mention that while performing at the London Hippodrome on cne cc3asion, my horse" Bob" came dowa with me at rehearsal, through catching his feet in the arena mat. He rolled over my limbs, bruising me genetally and tearing pieces of skin off my arms. By use of Zatn- Bilk immediately the wounds and bruises were cleanly and quickly healed, and the raw Tarts covered with new healthy skin. I use Zam-Buk daily for lubricat- ing my arms and muscles, as it keeps my limbs free and my skin supple. "As far away as the Mexican plains, I have used Zam-Buk for many poisonous insect-bites, a trouble inseparable from hot climates. _ _ _</p>
        <p>" .Zarn-Buk has yielded ,me far more remarkable results and generally proved more reliable than any- thing could have done in the way of mere ointments or ernbrocations, and I heartily give you permission to publish these fov words. " Yours faithfully, " GENILLE C. B. CAVE, Bart."</p>
        <p>No clearer evidence than the above from this well- known Baronet could be needed to prove the unique merit of Zam-Buk, and to show the wisdom of always keering handy a box of this ivonderful balm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>TRADES AND LA3OUR COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TRADES AND LA3OUR COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above C.tuncil was held itf the I.L.P. Rooms, Friars' Green, on Tnesday evening. Mr. Owen presided, Mr. Plinston (secretary) was also present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>NEW TRADE HALL.</head>
        <p>A NEW TRADE HALL.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY read a resolution of the last meeting of the Executive Committee asking the opinion of the adiated branches in tbe town as to the advisability of erecting a new trade hall or renting a large room in which to hold their meetings. Mr. SUMMERFIELD in supporting the resolution, said that had heard of old church or chapel buildings being used for such a purpose, in which all the members were shareholders. The resolution was confirmed. UNEMPLOYMENT.</p>
        <p>'rho SECRETARY read a resolution with regard to the unemployment in the town. It ran : "That this Council strongly recommend to the Town Council the commencement of works of utility in the town in order to alleviate the unemployment in the town."</p>
        <p>IThe PRESIDENT Baia he wished the Council to con- sider the question of sending a deputation to the Town Council, the Distress Committee and the Board of Guardiana. Mr. Owen referred to the Town Council's slowness in putting work into execution. He mentioned the proposed new school in Wash-lane, Latchford and said the matter had been under consideration since last May. Before long the grant would be withdrawn. Referring to ths new League of Help Mr. Owen stated that they had already got one in the form of the Distress Committee. He had noticed in the papers that a central office was to he found and probably a paid secretary, to superintend the work. The town had already got a central office also a paid secretary in the person of Mr. Fargher, of the Llbour Bureau. Mr. MouNFIELD also referring to the proposed Civic League of Help said that men did not want charity they wanted work. (Hear, hear.) The scheme of the widening of Warrington Bridge had teen under dis- cussion for 20 years and it did not seem as if it was sett led yet. It was prosed to send the following members of the Council as a deputation to the Town Council Sites and Building Committee and Board of Guardians.—Messrs. Plinston, Owen, Haley, Summerfield, Sinclair, Graham. and Parker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>FF.EDING OF SCHOOL CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>FF.EDING OF SCHOOL CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Mr. PODMORE asked how the feeding of school children was progressing. Mr. PLINSToN stated that it was progressing very favourably. There was one fault—that persons in receipt of-relief had not the privilege of sending their children for free meals. It was a great mistake and he would see if it could not be altered before very Jong.</p>
        <p>Competition No. 2. FOR SCHOOL CHILDREN. 48 Prizes Competitors must compose three verses. Each verse must be eight lines long. Every verse must have in it some reference to WOODS' GREAT PEPPERMINT CURE. chooses. (1 ) onencetevderwseiltio,nßaritiusWeEcmt Empire. (2) One Subject. Irse on a Humorous I (3) fir: Verse o Competitor</p>
        <p>48 CASH PRIZES VALUE £7O. FIRST PRIZE £25 I THIRD PRIZE... Z 5 I THIRTY PRIZES OF 10s. each. SECOND PRIZE ... £lO FIFTEEN PRIZES 0F... ... £1 each. This Competition is open only to bona fide Pupils of either sex attending Schools or Colleges throughout the North of England. Scholars must supply name of School they attend, also that of the Head Master or Mistress. KEEP THIS ADVERTISEMENT FOR REFERENCE. int Cure Company, Limited, Sheffield Telegraph Building, Fetter Lane, London. E.C., not later than March 31, 1909.</p>
        <p>onsented to act as Judges of the Competition; their verdict will be final. Competitors must enter on that understanding. land, and PRIZE MONEY PAID immediately the decision of the Judges is received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>FESTIVAL'S EDITATIONALVALCE.</head>
        <p>THE FESTIVAL'S EDITATIONALVALCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOHN I3esos prefaced the afternoon's proceedings with a few well chosen remarks full of encouragement to the committee. He touched upon the humanizing influence of singing, even as taught in the elementary schools, and emphasised the educational value of the festival, in as much as it raised the musical standard in the locality, created a taste for higher and better works and produced better executants, both vocally and instrumentally. While they must congratulate the pro- moters upon the general success of the festival there was, pethaps, a little regret in their minds that more did not take advantage of it. In some of the classes</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>INSTRUMENTAL COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>INSTRUMENTAL COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Master Ernest Millington, of Warrington, who tied with Master Geary for the prize in. the local violin competition on Friday evening, proved the winner of this contest. Mr.Coleridge-Taylor, in awarding him 17 marks out of 20, commented upon his unaEfected style, and expressed great satisfaction with the whole per- formance,remarking that the intonation was practically perfect from beginning to end and the fast pasages were extremely well played.</p>
        <p>thfILDR.EN'''S CHOIR CONTEST.</p>
        <p>Only two choirs had entered for this event—War- rington Band of Hope Select Choir (conductor, Mr. A. Spruce) and Sutton Unitnd Juvanile Caoir (condactor, Mr. W. Knapper). The test piece was " Song and flowers "(E. de Lloyd), and the marks were awarded as follows : Sutton United Juvenile Choir 45 Warrington Band of Hope Union Select Choir 42 Mr. Coleridge-Taylor said the Sutton Choir gave an excellent rendering, their performance being dainty and in keeping with the words. It was perfect in its light fancifulness. The Warrington choir showed very charming tone in all the voices, but the altos were not always satisfactory on the lower notes. The words were extremely well pronounced, but the piece should have been taken a shade faster.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>TENOR SOLO.</head>
        <p>TENOR SOLO.</p>
        <p>There were nineteen entries for this competition, the test piece being "The Message " (Blumenthal) Tne finalists were Mr. T. Morris, of Wrexham, and Messrs. Calvert and Goostrey, of Leigh. Mr. Morris gained the prize with 72 marks, the adjudicator remark- ing that be possessed a sympathetic and well-controlled voine. The expression was more convincing and the performance was more interesting than in the case of the other competitors. Messrs. Calvert and Goostrey gained 08 and 70 marks respectively.</p>
        <p>MIXED VOICE QUARTETTE COMPETITION. Sterndale Bennett's beautiful composition" God is a Spirit " was tin, test piece and eight parties entered. Widnes Harmonic were awarded the prize, the adjudi- cator observing that they had a very musical tone, and the singing of the soprano was very nice. The notes were verN clear. He gave them 16 marks out of a possible 20. The other competitors warn the Celtic Quartette, who obtained 15 marks, and Eclipse, who were awarded 14 marks.</p>
        <p>SECOND CHORAL COMPETITION. (MALE CHOIRS). This proved the most popular competition of the festival, eleven leading choirs singing " Martyrs of the Arena " (De Rille). Before the adjudication was delivered, Mr. EVANS said " Martyrs of the Arena" was a dramatic chorus and, as such, must be made dramatic from beginning to end. Some of the choirs had not been successful in the placing of the voices, the singers being too closely packed. Consequently, they suffered in tone, and in one point particularly the basses were not quite as audible as they might have been. If they asked him his candid opinion about the thing, it was that he did not want to hear that chorus again for a very long time.(Laughter.) Some of the singing had been very good, but most of it was only moderately good. The adjudicators had no hesitation in deciding upon the winners. The intona- tion, generally speaking, had been good, but the pitch varied slightly in some cases. Mr. COLERIDGE-TAYLoR announced that the marks bad been awarded as follows :</p>
        <p>Warrington Male Voice Choir 72 Southport Vocal Union 6B Denton Male Voice Choir Warrington Apollo Choir 54 Sale Apollo Wee Society 5:3 Wigan Harmonic 52 Ashton-under-Lyne 52 Birkenhead Brunswick Brotherhood 5l Gorton Co-operatiqe Glee Society 5O Liverpool Cecilia Male Choir 49 Goodshaw Glee Union ____ 4B</p>
        <p>In his comment on the Warrington Mile Voice Choir-, (conductor Mr. W. S. Nesbitt), Mr. Coleridge-Taylor said their singing was remirkably clear at the beginning and they gave by far the best recitative. The part to he sung with closed lips was excellent. The pitch sharpened considerably at one point but it caused no uupleasantness and might well be pardoned, conidering how well the choir sang and how much more striking they made the music thin the other choirs. With regard to the SJuthport Choir (conductor Mr. .1. C. Clarke), they gave a most finished r9ndering, but the first tenors were sometimes lacking in quality. The tempo was good and the quartette was well sung. The tone pro- duced by the Denton Choir was excellent but the quartette was glossed over so that the prayer sounded almost over-doue. The intonation was excellent hut the performance lacked shape in many places. Dealing with the Warrington Apollo Choir (conductor ,Mr. H. Berry), it was pointed out that 'the first basses missed a lead and the quartette was not altogether good, although the diminuendo bars were excellent. The intonation was not by any means perfect and there were several inaccuracies.</p>
        <p>• EVENING MEETING. PIANOFORTE SOLO COM PF.TITION. (12 TO 18 YEARS OF AGE).</p>
        <p>Out of eigldcompetitors the following were selected to appear in the final :—Dera Grosse, Silo ;Nellie Williams, Liverpool ; Florrie Bennett, Warrington. "Pits des Echarpes " (Chaminade) was the test piece. Mr. Harry Evans awarded the prize to Miss Bennett, with 70 marks. Her playing, he said, was more mature and fluent than that of the others. Her touch ?lways brought out the melody distinctly and made the instru- ment sing, and although there were a few slips in notes, her slyle was altogether more poetio and convincing than that of the other players. Miss Dora Grosse, of Sale, who obtained 67 marks, showed good technique but she was inclined to he a little too timid in touch in the loud parts. The expression was good and dainty and the general effect was pleasing, but the slight timidity made her playing not quite convincing. Miss Williams to whom he gave 62 marks, wai not careful enough in using the pedal and as a result one chord ran into another. Her touch was good and her style more robust than delicate. Her playing revealed many good points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>CONTR ALTO SOLO COMP.ETITION.</head>
        <p>CONTR ALTO SOLO COMP.ETITION.</p>
        <p>The finalists in this competition, who sang Gonnod's "The Worker" were :—Miss Josephine Saunders, a blind young lady, of Manchester, Miss Frost, of Long. sight, and Miss M. L. Risley, of Widnes. Tixe victory of Miss Saunders, who was awarded 70 points, was bailed with enthusiasm by the audience. Mr. Coleridge- Taylor said she had a real contralto voice, and she sang pathetically and expressively. Her pronunciation was praiseworthy. Thom were lapses in quality about the high notes occasionally, and one or two little errors in time, but from an expressive point of view there was little to be desired. Miss Frost had a pleasing voice but it lacked depth and body in the lower notes. There was a feeling all through that the true contralto quality wan missing; it seemed like a mezzo-soprano voice. Miss Risley sang in a rather monotonous and unsym- pathetic tone and she had an unfortunate tendency to dip down to the lower from the higher notes. There were several inaccuracies as to time and enunciation. CHILDREN'S ACTION sosa.</p>
        <p>1 This was a picturesque feature an] the interest was enhanced by the fact that all the entries were local, viz.:—Lorne House School (conductor, Miss A. Williams) ; Stockton Heath United Band of Hope (conductor, Miss M. Isherwood) ; Fdirfield Higher Grade School (conductor, Miss N. Gindy). Mr. Coleridge-Taylor humorously observed that when he was asked to come to the festival be thought it was to be musical adjudicating and he did not know anything of these action songs, otherwise he might not have come. He awarded the prize to Lorne House School, with 70 marks. These girls were attired in Chinese costumes which Mr. Coleridge-Taylor described as really charming. "And I think I am admitted to be an excellent authority as far as that is concerned," he added. Their voices were superior, and their actions quite picturesque and striking. Their pronunciation of the Chinese words was excellent. "At least I suppose they were," he continued, amidst laughter. Fairfield girls appeared as housemaids, and this item, said the adjudicator, was the, most popular of the three, but from an artistic point of view, which ought to come first at a musical festival, he had no hesitation in placing Lorne House School first. He awarded Fairfield 61 marks. Stockton Heath gave "The sweeping brush brigade," and Mr. Taylor placed the brushes before the dresses (which are charming enough) because they made so much dust. (Laughter.) The voices were not</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>very good, being blatant in quality. The intonation' was good,and pronunciation excellent. He gave them' 63 marks.</p>
        <p>BASS SOLO COMPETITION. The bass solo competition, in which the test piece was " Is not His word like a fire " (Mendetssolot), was won by Mr. Ramsay Clarke, Blackley, with 6.3 points. Mr. Tranter and Mr. Weedsll, St. Helens, received 62 marks each. CHIEF CHORAL COMPETITION. This was the crowning event of the festival and the, singing of the test pieces "Thanks be to God' (3fenceelsaohn) and "0 happy eyes " (Elgar) wag followed with keen interest and enjoyment. :the adjudicator awarded the marks as follows : Stretford Glee and Madrigal Society 7O Blackley Co-operative Choral Society 6B Stockton Heath Choral Union Droylesden Co-operative Society 6O Sale and District Musical Society Ashton Select Prize Choir I Mr. Evans said the competition had been very interesting in many ways. With regard to the Mendelsselan chorus,small choirs like they had had that night should not try to get effects which could only be got by big choirs accompanied by big orchestras. With a small choir the treatment must be more in keeping with the resources at their command. With regard to the part song the chief eletrent was daintiness. There should be no strenuous singing, not even in the forte passages. The Stretford Choir (conductor, Hr. T. Corlett) in the part song produced a pod musical resonant tone, which made up for some inelastic sing- • ing. There was good expression and the general effect. I was very pleasing. In the chorus the male voices were scarclly powerful enough, but there mil good straight- forward singing in the right spirit. The Blackley choir (conductor, Mr. F. Sheriff) opened the part song very' effectively, their treatment being smart and artistic, but the tone sometimes bulged too much in the crescendo. There was more elasticity and poetry in. this rendering than in several of the choirs. in. the chorus there was good firm singing and bright tone, but the intonation was not satisfactory.. With regard to Stockton Heath (a new choir, conducted by Mr. H. Berrey), Mr. Evans said in the part song -- they produced a nice, even tone, and their expression was commendable on the whole, but a little more elasticity would have been an improvement. The intonation was good and generally it was a very pleas- ing perfermance. In the chorus they lacked grip at the- maset and the attack in the middle of the piece was umdecided while the intonation left much to be desired. The gathering up of the forces towards the climax was not exciting enough. Still, on the whole, it was a good performance, but was not as good as the part song. Droylesden (conductor, Mr. A. Baxter), in the chorus showed a lack of rhythmic grip and their tone was immature but clear. The intonation was the best there had been. There was rather an unfortunate mislead in the middle. The part song was too slow and gave one - the effect of an anthem. There were some very beauti- ful effects in the soft singing, but there were portions that were exaggerated. The interpretation was not convincing in spite of pretty tonal effects. That was mainly due to the slow pace. Sale (conductor, Mr. A. Higson) in the chorus soon got off the pitch and the difficult middle section was made decidedly unpleasant. The tone was clear and full, hut there was too much vigour. It WPS good chorus-like singing, but this was discounted by the flaws mentioned. The treatment of the part-song was too strenuous, but the words were nicely spoken. The time was unnecessarily hurried in places, but there was some effective phrasing here and there. The singing of Aston (conductor, Mr. R. W. Walker) in the chorus fell far short of the other choirs. The tone was not sufficiently full and resonant, and was some inaccurate singing. The music was not closely knit. They did better in the part-song,although their style was somewhat stilted and immature,and this should give them encouragement to goon. The shield was presented to the conductor of Stret- ford by the Mayores3 (Mrs. Forshavv.) CHA LLENGE SOLO COMPETITION. This was one of the most interesting and successful features of the festival, the result being :—Miss Josephine Saunders, Manchester, 74 • Mr. Ramsay Clarke, Blackley, 71; and Miss Nellie Gibaon,Liverpool 68. The winner sang" Secrecy," which Mr. Evans said was one of the most beautiful and artistic songs ever written. The words and music were touching and the singing was equally touching. Miss Saunders got the spirit of the words and was always highly emotional. The song made great demands on emotional power, restraint and artistic expression. Mr. Clarke had an exceptionally fine bass voice—a rather rare thing—and his performance was very good hut there was some exaggeration. Mis 3 Gibson sang with a nice tone, but it was a little bit thin and not quite free.</p>
        <p>MALE VOICE QUA RTETT-E COMPETITIM The male voice quartette competition was won by Southport Apollo, the test piece being "The long day closes" (Sullivan.) The adjudicator said this party was well balanced, and the singing was equally good in loud and soft passages. The weakness of the performance was in the pronunciation, but the expres- sion and general effect were good. The marks gained were 70 by the winders; 67 Ly Mona Quartette, South- port ; and 61 by the peltic, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>DUET COMPETITION. The duet competition ("When the wind blows in from the sea ") was won by Miss Silcock and Mr. Dutton, Warrington, with 67 marks. Other marks worn : —Miss Charnock and Mr. Goostroy, Leigh, 66; Miss Esther Parr and Mr. Weeds)!, St. Helens, 65 ; Mr. and Miss Corlett, aretford, 6!; and Mr. Bert Wallace, Chester, and Mr. John Gordon, Practntyn, CI. During the evening tbe Warrington Male Voice Choir sang "Beleaguered " and " 0 Peaceful night " with exquisite effect, Mr. Nesbitt conducting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>Bold-street Wesleyan Men's Bible Class.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Men's Bible Class.</p>
        <p>The members of this class promoted al interesting concert and cinematograph entertainment who took place in Bold-street Wesleyan Schoolroom on Monday evening. Mr. Holland, superintendant of Bible class, presided in the unavoidable absence of tha Roy. T. Millar aad delivered an address commending the good work of the class. Some clear and varied pictures were shown illustrating life in Canada the films being kindly lent by Mr. Leonard Bennett, also views of North Wales. Many humourous pictures were also shown, the lantern being manipulated by Mr. J. Parkinson. Solos were con— tributed in a very pleasing manner during the evening by /Miss Silcock and Mr. Levi Richardson. Jr. E. A. Sanders and Mr. A. Waterworth scored a- great success with their humorous st..lections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>CHRONIC ASTHMA, W;th j-immorrhage of Lungs. DEGLARED HOPELESS.</head>
        <p>CHRONIC ASTHMA, W;th j-immorrhage of Lungs. DEGLARED HOPELESS.</p>
        <p>Extraordinary results from VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
