f that kind. The constable said he went in ugly rumours about. Witness knew at the time that and saw Humphries there and that the man was sober. Marshall had been out with Rushworth at nights. In that, he (Mr. Madden) said P.C. Newey was an Witness understood there was going to be some charge unmitigated liar. He (Mr. Madden) never minced his against him and he felt he was innocent but thought he . terms in this matter and he said the officer would get particulars against Rushworth for a counter told a deliberate untruth, Singularly enough, case. Marshall said he could not go against Rushworth when he saw Hiimphries the following night and witness asked him to consider the matter. He did he was drunk and Hayes told him to take not produce a written document but asked Marshall notice of that as "it might be wanted." if he would make a declaration. It was totally Humphries swore that Peacock told him to sign untrue that he offered him £5OO to sign' a paper to the effect that Rushworth had offered witeess had not got £5OO altogether before that c him £l5 if he would swear that Peacock had bribed him case statqd. Witness saw Marshall on another occasion with a shilling. If a man were mean and low enough and he told him that he had been thinking it over and ',hit*" le Id hies - " Wit,</p>
        <p>he did not thini ae wou._ give him away:- . never threatened to oust him from his position in the Corporation. What he told the witness Marshall was that they were trying to make up a case by means of perjury against him for what he hid said after the declaration of the poll, but that his election had been the cleanest and driest election ever known in Warring- ton. The brewers were trying to ruin him and place him in the same position as his brother Arthur. Marshall said he was very sorry, and witness pointed out that many an innocent man had been ruined by a conspiracy. Subsequeutly witness was told that Marshall had signed some paper, but when he saw Marshall he said he had left the paper at Mr. Jenkins' office. Marshall told him what ho had put in the document, about witness having threatened to get him the sack, and said he wanted to put it right. As he said be did not care to go to Mr. Jenkins' office again witness,at his request,drafted him a letter which Marshall copied out and sent to Mr. Jenkins. A few years ago the witness W.L.Williams was an applicant for the position of road foreman under the Corporation, and the Com- mittee gave him the appointment, although his experi- ence did not comply with the terms of the advertise- ment. The witness protested against this piece of political jobbery, and moved a resolution in the Council, which he succeeded in carrying, that the appointment be not confirmed. He never told Williams that he would win the election if it cost him 250. With the witness Frank Wilkinson, he and his brother had had a business dispute, and that morning he had received the following letter :—" 3, Beech Grove, Liscard. February sth, 1908.—Sir,—Reviewing all matters appertaining to your dealings, along with your brother, regarding brewery transaction, I now purpose, as advised, te deal with same in all its bearings without delay.—F. WILKINSON." Mr. Dickens: This is a "threatening letter." Witness said his brother Arthur,Miss Elsie Wilkinson, and Mrs. Silcock were not his agents. Mr. Dickens : Did the declaration Hamphriessigned contain anything about the £l5 ?—Yes. Did you instruct your Counsel so ?—Yes. That is strange, because your counsel in opening the case said you would deny altogether the story as to there being any mention of the £l5 in the paper. The Commissioner pointed out that what counsel had said did not affect witness's evidence. Mr. Dickens: Where is that paper ?—So far as I remember I left everything with Mr. Roberts. You agree that it is a very important paper ?—lt is very important. And would go very far to show whether you or Humphries is telling the truth ?—Yes. and I am sorry It is lost. Mr. Roberts has told us beyond all question that he told you this was an original document and gave it back to you. Do you swear that is not true?-1 cannot remember him saying so. Will you swear that is not true sir ?—I cannot remember him saying that. I don't think Mr. Roberts swore he gave it back to me. He did swear it in the clearest possible language and he identified it and was purporting to give its contents as being a declaration of Humphries. Did he hand you back any papers ?—I don't think he did. You gave him all •the papers you had then and got none of them back ?—Yes to the best of my recollection. Well you give me the pencil copy of Marshall's letter ?—That was not supposed to be in the case at all. Marshall has given what I suggest is very strong evidence against you of trying to quash this petition. Do you mean to say you have selected what papers you would give to your solicitor and did not give them all? —I gave all the papers I thought related to the petition, If 1%1 r. Roberts was telling the truth, that declaration got back into your possession and has disappeared since ?—I don't think it ever has been in my possession since that night. Mr. Dickens: Mr. Roberts said he looked at it awl found it was an original document and handed it back to you.—May I explain the matter? If you can, most certainly.-1 am not a lawyer and I understood petitions only referred to bribery or treating and I only bad papers with respect to that. I did not think thig was a question that would come into the petition at all. Henry Marshall was one of your own committee ? Yes. A personal friend of yours ?—Yes. A man who had no animosity against you ?—None. Can you suggest any reason under those circumstances why Henry Marshall should have concocted a series of lies ?—Yes, I think he must have been under the influenoe of Rushworth. Your own friend on your committee deliberately perjured himself under the influence of Rustrworth and the others ?—Yes. lam positive. Is that your same explanation of Police Constable Newey's story ?—I cannot understand Newey's conduct at all. It is reprehensible. If Newey's story is right you asked him. deliberately to take proceedings against Rushworth in order to quash the case against you. That is a lie is it ?—I told him to do his duty. lam not talking of what you told him. Tam putting to you Newey's story.—lt is a lie. A deliberate lie ?—A deliberate one, yes. And this man Newey, you suggest also under the influence of Newey and the others, has also committed perjury against you ?—Yes. The witness Williams stated that you said you were</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <p>going to beat them if you spent 250.—There is no truth in that. I have not got 250. He has also been subborned to commit perjury by these people ?—Yee. Did you try to get Airs. Lawrinson to sign a paper? —Yes. Did she decline to do it, ?—Yes. Do you suggest she had been forced to sign Rush- worth's paper ?—Yes. Let us see bow far we are getting. We have got Williams' evidence. Tnat is untrue. Humphries, of course, a deliberate lie from beginning to end ?—Yes. Newey's evidence, so far. as it concerns you, is a deliberate lie ?—Yes. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Henry narshall, frozn beginning to end, a tissue o lies ?—Yes. _ . _ _</p>
        <p>His brother, William Marshall, a tissue of lies ?—He was not there.</p>
        <p>Fisher, as regards the twelve months, a lie ?—Yes.</p>
        <p>And Mrs. Lccwrinson, a lie ?,—Yes. All thew people are in a conspiracy against you ? Evidently they are. Da you agree with your counsel that these people are food for corruption ?—Yes. Just the kind of people to take small drinks and small bribes? -From the appearance of them, yes. It is true you saw lllmphries in L3ngford-street ? Yes.</p>
        <p>_ It is true you were at that time speaking to Coun- cillor Cannington and that Ambrose Wilkinson was with Humphries ?—Yes. Curiously enough the whole story is true, except the episode of giving a shilling ?—That is a lie. As to the facts before and alter, he is telling the truth ?—Yes.</p>
        <p>I Further questioned witness said it was common talk in the neighbourhood and in the forge that Ambrose Wilkinson had given Humphries a shilling and witness thought Humphries was going to make a statement against Wilkinson when he went to Messrs. Greeriall's , office. They never knew what Rashworth and Hayes would do when they got hold of a man like Humphries. Da you mean to suggest that Rusbworth and Hayes deliberately concocted with this man a false story ? Yes, I do. You saw Humphries come out of Greenall's office staggering drunk ?—Yes, staggering drunk. Just after Mr. Jenkins had taken his declaration and said he was sober, he was actually staggering drunk.— Yes, that is so. Who were the other bloodhounds that followed him besides yourself, Wood and Walsh ?—Wood was there alone and then Walsh came up. Did you say to Newey "They are trying to bring a Me against me "?—Yes, we were all together. Did you say "You go in the Horse and Jockey and do Rushworth for permitting drunkenness and the other case will fall through ?"—I did not say "do Rushworth " at all. _ _</p>
        <p>_ You did suggest that Humphries was drunk ?—Yes. And you did suggest that Newey should go into the Horse end Jockey ?-_—Yes._</p>
        <p>To see if he was drunk ?—Yes.</p>
        <p>Da you mcan to suggest, whatever language you used, that you did not say to Newey something about the case against you falling through?—! did not. On your oath ?—On my oath. You know it is a curious story for Noway to invent ? —lt is extraordinary. And no motive to invent it ?—I do not know the man except by sight. You were surprised at his peijury ?—Yes, I was. _ .</p>
        <p>The Court thisn adjourned itntil Monday. Mr. Dickens intimated that he would not be able to attend until after the luncheon adjournment and at his request it was agreed that the crosa-examination of Mr. Peacock should stand over until his return.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>MONDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</head>
        <p>MONDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>The hearing was continued on Monday. Mr. Alexander ,Potter appeared on behalf of the Public Prosecutor in place of Mr. Byrne. Neither Mr. Dickens, K.C., nor Mr. Madden took part in the case until after the luncheon adjournment.</p>
        <p>MR. ARTHUR PEACOCK'S EVIDENCE. THE STORY OF A MOTOR CAR.</p>
        <p>Arthur Peacock, Liscard, stated that for nine years he was a member of the Council for Orford Ward. He was not in the Soldiers' Return on October 29th—in fact he was not in Warrington that day but at 91, Victoria-road, New Brighton. Mr. Rees: Were you in the Soldiers*Return with Davies and Pemberton on any Thursday evening prior to the election ?—Not that I am aware ot.</p>
        <p>Were you on any evening ?—No. Witness continuing said he came from Liscard to Warrington by motor car on the day of the election, leaving home soon after B.3o,accompanied by his two sons and the driver. The car would not hold more than four. He arrived at Warrington close on eleven o'clock. He went to the warehouse in West-street where he remained fora few minutes and then went to the polling booth. After that time be did not go in the car again during the day. He did not canvass for any votes. He remained in the neighbourhood of the booth except at meal times. Witness could not say that he saw the motor car at all during the day until 8 p.m. After the close of the poll he went to the Soldiers' Return. A man spoke to him—he did not know who he was—and asked him for something to give his mate a drink. Witness did not know whether he gave the man any- thing—most likely he would if he had it. Witness returned to Liverpool by the 9.40 train. By Mr. Swift: The contests witness fought for municipal honours were keen ones, and he took great interest in political matters. You know your brother entered into this contest to fight against the brewery interest ?—Probably. I don't say he did not. Did you approve of his object ?.:7I did not. . .</p>
        <p>Not -of his- icing into the Council ?—T did not approve of it at all. I strongly objected to it from the com- mencement.</p>
        <p>As he was going in, I suppose you were anxious he should win ?—Certainly. And as far as anything lay in your power to help him, I suppose you would help him?—No. I did not do that at alt.</p>
        <p>Why not ?—Because I was not altogether interested in the thing. I could have made his majority a lot more if I had canvassed. You did not canvass at all ?—Not at all.</p>
        <p>On the date of the-election yo-n-dia-leiP at tha corn- mittee room ?—Not at all.</p>
        <p>What did you do ?—Just walked in and out and if I saw anything going wrong Ishould probably have put it right. I suppose that is what you were doing when the i dispute arose about the motor car ?—That s so. _</p>
        <p>Further questioned witness said he was not wearing his brother's portrait or red ribbon. The motor car he had hired was not in use to bring voters up and there were no placards on it. Witness had hired the car.</p>
        <p>_ - ' Yon knew it would be wrong for it to be used in the election ?—I knew it would be illegal and it was not used in the election. I hired it to bring me to Warrington. Had you ever hired a car before to bring you to Warrington ?—No. I daresay you thought it so nice that you have often done it since ?—No. I have had a contract. Is this the position? That on the one day that there was an election in the Orford Ward you hired a motor car to bring you over from Liscard, and that is the only time you have ever done so ?—I have hired one from Warrington to Liscard.</p>
        <p>What did the iii-oio-i car cost you ?—I think it was about 50s.</p>
        <p>The railway fare would not cost you anything like that ?—Soruetimes one does not consider the cost of these things. I wanted to give the boys a treat. They had not been by motor before. So you selected the day on which your brother was going to fight Orford Ward to give your boys a treat by bringing them in a motor car to Warrington ?—Yes. Who went back in the motor car ?—No-one. What in the world was , the car kept waiting all day for ?—For me and the boys. When did you make up your mind not to go back in it?—About 8 o'clock.</p>
        <p>1_ want to know V. 437 you purposely stayed away from the meeting-on Pctober 29th ?-7-1 had private reasons, I thought something might be mentioned and I refrained on that account.</p>
        <p>You were afraid something might be said about you? —Yes. , \Vanes's further stated that be saw Davies and Pemberton in the Soldier's Return on the night of the election. He never sat in the public-house with them on any night before the election. The story of Barlow and Stockton must be an invention. -</p>
        <p>This great election was being fought on the question of beer or no beer ?—Not by me. By your brother. Didn'G you hear that speech in</p>
        <p>BROWN &amp; POLSON have arranged for a SERIES OF COOKERY LESSONS by Miss W. STOOKE - VAUGIIXN , Diplom4e, National Training School of Cookery, on—</p>
        <p>Had he said before the election 'what he has been saying here, that he came out merely to prevent a publican being elected never heard him say it in public in my life. Hi in ty luve said it to rue or he may not. _ _ _</p>
        <p>And you on the election day gave a man sixpence for beer ?—What he said has nothing to do with what I do. So that he does the preaching and you do the prac- tice ?—I don't do the practice. Well you gave a man sixpence for beer ?—I am not a teetotaller myself, nor a brewer. In the Soldier's Return did you pay for any drinks?— No, only my own. Further cross-examined, witness said he remembered someone askinc, him to give him and his ma'-e.a drink. Witness was alone at the time. He did not hear anyone say "Cheap ale, Nellie, Cheap ale." He might have told Wilkinson, of biscard. that he wanted the car to bring his boys to Warrington. He would not swear it. Did you say " If my brother gets in, the party would have to pay .£3OO to get him out ?"—I didn't say any such thing. You knew that would be so ?—No. I did not. You mean to say you did not know there would have to hen deposit of .£3OO before there could bea petition? —I did not. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Did you say to anybody connected with the case " What a liar that fellow Wilkinson is. There is no</p>
        <p>rnth in this ?"—No. I did not.</p>
        <p>lave you ever up to this moment denie, _ruL of what Wilkinson said to anybody ?—No, I have not. Is it true ?—No. If you said what Wilkinson states you did, do you think it affects your reputation at all ?—I don't think it does.</p>
        <p>the t</p>
        <p>In answer to the C3mmissioner, witness said he did not remember what he gave to the man who asked him for a drink. It might have been a.sixpence, or two three- penny bits. Mr. Rees: Why did von object to your brother going into this contest ?-1 did not think municipal life was worth bothering about at all. Did you during the day, apart from this un-known man give anything to anybody ?—Nothing whatever. Are you and Prank NVilkinson friendly ?—We were probably up to Saturday week. Witness said the dispute with Wilkinson was his (witness's) matter but as he was away he left it in his brother's hauls. Re-examined by Mr. Swift : He considered Wilkin- son had" twisted" upon them, and witness was angry with him. He had no cause to be spiteful against you ?—None whatever. No cause to say what was not true ?—None whatever. You have taken no action against him ?—None ' %%hatever. You have not threatened him in any way ?—T have not. I believe my brother put it in the solicitor's hands about three months ago. Witness said he instructed his assistant to stop Wilkinson's credit at Christmas but it was not done until witness personally stopped it. on January 30th. Wilkinson always thought he had cause to quarrel with him, but the boot was on the other leg. Albert Chesters, 91a, Victoria-road, New Brighton, stated that on October 29th at 8.30 p.m. he and Arthur Peacock were discussing a business matter at New Brighton. He produced a book recording the appoint- ment. John W. Lees, advertising agect, New Brighton, gave similar evidence. By Mr. Swift: Arthur Peacock told him he had been at Warrington on the two days previous to October 29th in connection with his brother's election. ALFRED WOOD CALLED. Alfred Wood, 23, Longford-street, ironworker, employed at Da Ilam Forge for the past twenty years, stated that George Cooper worked at the same works. They had been great friends for years. Witnesscalled at Cooper's house before the day of the election and saw his wife. It was true he told her he would see C3oper at his work. He did not canvass him on the election day. About 9 o'clock on election morning he went to the Unicorn Inn with Mullen. He visited the place again about ten o'clock, alonglwith Mullen, and saw Cooper there. Witness was an old running man and had never lost a race in his life. At ten o'clock, witness ordered drinks for himself and Mullen, Cooper said " Alf. I am having one?" Witness said "All right, George," and put a shilling down. When witness arrived at the inn, Cooper was already there, with a glass before him. Cooper had whisky. Witness and Cooper had probably had hundreds of drinks together. (Laughter.) They paid in turn. Witness did not ask Cooper to have a drink in _ order to influence his vote. He and</p>
        <p>Muliens left Cooper at the Unicorn and witness did not remember speaking to him again that day. Not a word was said about the electjon while they were in the public-house. Witness first saw Fisher on the election day about noon near the Blue Coat School. John Mullen was with him. A conversation took place, witness saying "This is the man to-day" (indicating me. Peacock's photo in his coat). Fisher said" lie is for a— pint." Witness replied, " I didn't think you were a chap of that sort, working at a brewery." Fisher said," Well, I want one," but added "I am not going to vote now." Witness said " You may as well go and get it done with," but Fisher said "l am not going now," and witnes3 replied" All right," and left him in disgust. Witness never asked him to have a drink. At 7 o'clock in the evening witness was" buzzing "about, trying to bring in stray voters, and Fisher came up to him and said " Can't I have a— pint? I have voted for Peacock." Witness was busy and told him to clear off: Fisher went away hut returned again and said " Now then, aren't I having one." Witness was getting in a temper with him and again sent him away. At 7.30 Jim Rose came up and he and Fisher went into the Unicorn together. Witness did not go and he did not give Rose any money—in fact Rose did not ask for any either on the election day or since. 'Witness was not in the Unicorn Inn between 7 and Bp.m. on the election day. Rose was not a worker in the election. Witness had had lots of drink with Edward Gleave and on the Friday night before the election they had drinks in the Unicorn Inn together, for which Gleave paid. On the morning of the election day about nine o'clock he saw Gleave in the Unicorn Inn nearly "boozed." They had drinks together and Gleave again paid. Witness never stood Gleave a drink or offered him one for the purpose of influencing his vote. "if it would have won the election I would never have</p>
        <p>offered him one" he added. Witness did not give money to Henry Perry to stand drink to anyone. He remembered coifing on Thomas Knight along with John Melia and another man before the election. Melia _ . _ _ _</p>
        <p>asked Knight for his vote but Knight looked at them as if they were bloodhounds. He looked as if he would eat them. (Laugh ter) Not a word was said about a beer bottle while witness was there and he might as well have told them at the Conservative Club as talk to Knight 'about beer. Mr. Rees: Is he a strong Conservative ?—There is not a stronger in the country. (Laughter.) It was no use trying to convince him ?—I was not going to try it on. (Laughter.) Did you give Dumbell any money to stand drinks to Knight ?—i don't think I knew Dumbell, never mind ghing him money. Continuing, witness said that on the Sunday 'night before the election he called at John Melia's and Knight happened to be there. Witness asked Knight what lies he had been telling about him with referewie to some drinks, and added " I have a wife and five children. HOW would it be if I got locked up through your lying ?'' On the night of the election he was in the house of Abraham Smart. He threw a sovereign down when he went in because he was a bit jubilant. The sovereign was the proceeds of a bet on the elec- tion, and Smart also had some money to draw. Mr. Rees : In Warrington do you bet on elections like this ?—No, but they were sure of " intelligent Jim" winning, and everywhere you went they were offering bets. And you wanted to back your opinion ?—Yes. Continuing his evidence witness said James Humphries went by the name of "Bungit." Witness was known as " Cousin Alf "and " Little Alf." One night after the election—he believed it was a Monday —he saw Humphries standing near the Central Station Bridge about 8.30, accompanied by Rushworth. Humphries was unwashed and he was " boozed." Rushworth was bolding him by the shoelder. Witness heard the word" Peacock " mentioned and he saw Rushwarth and Humphries go into Greenall's °nice together. Witness had heard runiouis about Humphries having bad a shilling from Ambrose Wilkinson—it was common talk about the forge—and he went and tele- phoned to Mr. Peacock and he came upon the scene very quickly along with Walsh. They all stood near Mounfield's shop but witness eventually went for a drink. On returning he found Mr. Peacock standing alone and be said that Humphries had come out and Walsh bad followed him along Wiewick-road. Mr. Peacock went home and witness went along to the " Jockey " where he saw Walsh John Melia and Constable Newey standing near the bridge. They had been there about ten minutes when Mr. Peacock came</p>
        <p>up. It bad been stated that Humphries was in the Jockey and Mr. Peacock told Newey that he should go into the Jockey and do his duty, if he was allowed to enter public-houses whilst on special duty.Newey went in but when he returned he said Humphries was not there but witness-afterwards visited the Jockey for a glass of whisky and saw Humphries standing at the bar "as large as life." Towards closing time Humphries camo out accompanied by another man who was assisting him along. They went up lierfoot-street but witness did not follow them. Witness worked for Councillor Peacock at the election because he was such a good friend to him in 1003 when he was out of work. He</p>
        <p>always would work for Mr. Peacock, "as long as he bad got breath." The night after these fproceedings witness saw Humphries and his brother Sam at 6.30. Sam said " Ilungit has been telling me he has been doing wrong and be wants to get clear of it if he can." James Humphries said if be did not get clear he would get six months. They all went to Sam Floyd's house and Councillor Peacock was sent for. James Humphries did not know Coun- cillor Peacock—he said he had never eeen him before.</p>
        <p>Humphries said he had signed a note that he had which he said : "Now chaps, this electiorr Is beer or , received is. and he had been offered £l5 ta sign the no beer ?"—No. document. He added that at the office whore be. signed Did you hear of it ?—No. a lot of men kept telling him to" go on and get the Did you know your brother had been dehouncing the . /5 quid," and also that he was drunk and they had to brewing trade ?—I heard about it afterwards. 1 bold his hand while he signed. Ha said he had signed Didn't you hear about it before the election ?—No. ,' it at an office but he did not know where it was, and Did you know what your brothet's principles were I Itushworth was there at the time. Mr. Peacock had about it ?—I did not. I not a document ready for Humphries to sign but he Did you know what induced him to come out ?--I i wrote one out at the house and it was agreed to go to a heard what he said. That is all. 1 magistrate. Tue document was real over to him by _ _ . _ __ . . .. .</p>
        <p>Mr: Wright before it was signed. H imohries said he never received a shilling.Ou leavkng Mr.Wright's,witness and the brothers Humphries went to the Borough Arms and had some drinks. Witness stood the first round. Mr. Rees: Did you- give a drink to any voter to . .</p>
        <p>influence his vote during this election? IVitness : Not one drink. By Mr. Swift: Jas. Ifurnphries was very drunk when he came out of the Jockey.. Witness did not try to! follow him. In this fight you bora the brunt of the battle didn't you ? You were the prinepar worker for Mr. Peacock ? —I don't know. Who did more than you ?—.l. lot of them. I should think those fellows who s'ore on da,y.; would do more ! work than I. llovv many of Rushworth's voters did you manse to buzz into the pull fur Peacnr.:k ?—None that I am aware of. You were not doing much if you could only bring up your own side. Mr. Madden : That is the chief thin. Further questioned, witness said that fast Thursday week he had a talk with Fisher about his evidence and Fisher said it was a lie to sty that he had had a pint. Mr. Peacock happened to come up at that time and he</p>
        <p>told Fisher to tell the truth. Fisher was not told that if the charge against him (Wood) was provirl Ire (Wood) .id th( 4,1 hi</p>
        <p>might get Ewerve months. P ply to Mr. Swift</p>
        <p>-n reply to Mr. witn s e only go ue had in his possession on the election morning, was his gold guard. (Laughter.) Witness never faceived penny for his work for Mr. Peacock during the election. What were the odds of the betting ?—lilvens. (Laughter.) I should have had some money in my</p>
        <p>leSS s</p>
        <p>;Pocket on the morning of the election but for that. 1 am not short of a shilling or two at the week-end. I (Laughter). Can you explain why you watched the manHurnphries from 7.30 to 10.10 and n ver spoke to him ?-43e.:ause he was away from us. MORE ABOUT THE DECLARATION. Tbe Commissioner said that the last witness had stated that Humphries told him when ho went to Messrs. Greenall's office to sign the declaration on! November 9th he was so drunk that they had to hold his hand to make him sign it. Whether that was wryer° recollection or invention of Humphries or someone else it was not for bin tosay, but it was obviously a. most ' serious statement, and he thought there ought to be a further opportunity given to Mr. Jeakins or someone to say really what happened that night. Mr. F. G. Taylor said he had an intim-1Mo!) that ' Humphries was coming to makes statement, and in con- i sequence he remained at his office on November 9th ' beyond the usual hour. He afro informed Mr. ..Tenkins , and asked him to wait. To the best of his recollectibn Rushworth was with Humphries, and witness asked Humphries to tell' him' what he knew about the case. ' He took the statement from Humphries' own lips. The man was sober. Mr. Dickens :• Cs there any foundation for the suggestion that you had to hold his hand in order that he might sign his name ?!,—Certainly not. S )me• witnesses have said they could not write, and I believe ! he was one, but there is no truth in the suggestion that Mr. Jenkins or I held his hand Was the statement read over to hiin?—Certainly. Did he understand it ?Certainly. You appreciate the monstrous nature of the sugges- tion that you held his hand ?' Mr. Madden What I understand was that he said somebody held his hand. No one suggests that Mr. Taylor would do such a thing. Mr Dickens That is the suggestion made by Wood, obviously. Mr. Taylor : I myself definitely contradict it. IT was present and no-ono held his hand. The Commissioner : And you saw him sign-? Certainly. The statement made in my hearing that Humphries arrived about 7.40 is altogether untrue. 113 came between 6.17.nnd 6.30. Mr. Dickens : Was there the slightest suggestion of' any cffer of £l5 to Humphrie.s ?---Not in my hearing. Mr. Rees cross-examined Air. Taylor as to why a• small 'Oh" had been altered to a capital in one of. Humphries' declarations. Witness said he believed it was pointed out to Humphries that he had signed with. a small "h" and he altered it. The alteration was not. in different ink. The man was sober when he male his signature but he said he was nervous and it was! pointed out to him that ha had missed an " h " out of his name. Witness was a commissioner ftr oaths and! on one occasion be declined to take a stattmeut from a witness who was not sober.. The Commissioner said he did not think there was,</p>
        <p>the slightest foundation for the suggestion, that the capital" H "was in different ink. Mr. W. 11. J. Jenkins said that when his office closed' shortly after six o'clock he went into fessrs. Greenall's- office and Humphries and Rushworth had already arrived. He read the statement carefully over to. Humphries and he signed it. Witness drew his atten— tion to the omssiou of a letter "h." Humphries!' manner was boisterous and rude but he was sober. There was not the slightest foundation for the sugges— tion that anyone bold his hand while he signed.. Nb. mention was made by anyone of £l5. Mr. Madden: Ido not suggest any of these men. held his hand. If Wood said it its the box he simply said that Humphries had told him so. Mr. Dickens submitted that the lines of Mr. Rees' cross-examination implied that Woods' statement was- true. He did not like blowing hot and cold, one counsel suggesting that a statement was true and the other that it was not. Mr. J. A. Rushworth, the petitioner, said he met Humphries in Winwick-road and went with him to Messrs. Greenall's office. He was perfectly sober and witness had not to assist him. Witness remained until the document had been signed and then left Humphries to go home by himself. By Mr. Roes: Witness had no appointment to meet Humphries and had not told Mr. Taylor to expect him. He did not go to the Rodney Arms to fetch Ilumphries. The latter had never spoken to him about the matter up to that night. IVitness told him they had heard he bad received a shilling from Ambrose Wilkin- son and asked Humphries if he was prepared to swear it. He replied that he was and witness took him to the solicitors' office. At the office he heard Humphries say that Peter Peacock bad given him a shilling. Did you tell !dr. Taylor that he bad just told you . . . . .</p>
        <p>that Ainbrose Wilkinson had given him 11-. shilling f•--1; mean Peter Peacock had given him the shilling, nut Ambrose Wilkinson. I made a mistake. James Rose 25 Hale-street ironworker, said he was _</p>
        <p>working until' six' o'clock on the day of 'the election. He had had drinks with Wood, whom he had known for some years, but not with Fisher. At 7.30 in the even- ing of the election day he went to Wood, who was busy in the polling booth, and asked him to go the Unicorn Inn. Fisher came upon the scene and asked Wood for a pint but Wood told him to get out of the way as he had no time to talk to him.. Witness went to the Unicorn Inn, taking Fisher with him, and they had drinks for which witness paid. Wood did not give him any money for the purpose, nor did he tell witness to stand Fisher a drink. Witness went to the Unicorn to see a friend. Mr. Dickens: Why should you give Fisher a drink on this occasion for the first time in your life? Witness : I saw he wanted one.' (Laughter). Did Wood say " Take him across and get him one " ? --No. You treated him out of kindness:ef heart because the poor man wanted one ?—Yes. John Melia, 175, Winwick-road, ironworker, Thomas Knight's father-in-law, said Wood did not give him any money to buy the beer or ask him to treat Knight. Witness personally paid for the beer, and Knight drank it. The cost was V- • d He had paid for beer for Knight 2 many a score of times, and Knight had treated him. Witness saw James Humphries on November 9th along with James Walsh in Winwick-road at 9.30 p.m. TLey went into the British Tar but the landlord would not serve Humphries. Afterwards they walked to the Jockey Bridge. Humphries said he was offered £l5 " to stick to the one word," but he did not say what that word was. Humphries went into the Horse and Jockey alone. P.C. Newey came up and Mr. Peacock asked him if he was ar.owed to go in public-houses while on special duty. Newey went into the Jockey and on coming out he said he could not find Humphries there. By Mr. Dickens : Witness was a Liberal. They were all three refused drinks at the Star, but witness and Walsh were sober, it waslrue that they asked Knight for his vote, and Knight said he would consider it. Wood pressed him to vote for Peacock. There were high words at his house between Knight and Wood on a Sunday night after the election. Wood said "Don't give me away. Do the best you can forme. Remember I have got a wife and five children." By Mr. Rees: There was some mention of ducks in the dispute. The Court then adjourned until Tuesday. (Continua on Page 8.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>LITTLE AND GOOD.</head>
        <p>LITTLE AND GOOD.</p>
        <p>It must always be remembered that in food, more than in anything else, it is quality and not quantity that tells for good. The man who would eat nothing but fine white bread would soon become anfemic, puffy, flabby of muscle, and lifeless of countenance, while he who elected to subsist on a dietary of driei currants would remain agile, muscular, well-coloured and generally fit. Nevertheless our illustration points out one clear direction in which health, vitality, and muscular strength are to be attained. Let currants be, not the whole of your daily diet, but a very appreciable portion thereof, and your digestive functions will be kept in sound working order, your complexion:will be clear and bright, and your general health will ho permanently benefited by the constant support detiy- able from so wholesome and natural a food.</p>
      </div>
