      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>PBBRUARY 13th. 1909.</p>
        <p>We shall soon have a THE NEW SOUTH Commonwealth of South AFRICA. Africa, as we already have</p>
        <p>an Australian Common- wealth. Already the determination towards union has gone so far as the drawing up of a Draft Constitution. There appears to have been absolute unanimity on the subject of federation in some form, in a body composed of elements so diverse as are represented by General BOTHA. and Dr. JA3IESON. The organiser of the Raid, we cannot help thinking, must have been full of very curious feelings, and sad rather than joyous reflections, as he sat in an assembly composed of representatives of all the South African Colonies, the object being to unite Briton and Boer under one Constitution. Not many years have passed since that unfortunate Raid; but what changes they have seen CECIL RHODES has long been asleep among the Matoppo Hills. Of the men then prominent in Cape Colony almost all have since died or have disappeared from public life. The two Republics have vanishedrand the men with whose names they were identified— Kruger, Stein, Joubert !—their names seem already to belong to a far distant past. Probably it did not strike Dr JAMESON that for his own part he must figure in history as a con- spicuous instance of futility, of the real unimport- ance of those who oppose themselves, often unknowingly, no doubt, to the great, silent, pro- gressive forces of the world. The union that is now being arrived at by agreement between the South African white races Dr. JAMESON would have brought about by the sword. The miscalcula- tion that he made must always continue to be matter of great wonder. He had a reputation for ability that could hardly be distinguished from genius. lie was such a man of business that the supreme control of the affairs of the Chartered Company was placed in his hands. Yet he had so little judgment in really great affairs that he believed it to be possible to capture the Transvaal Republic with a few hundreds of men. He may be considered to have led the most ridiculous expedition that the world as over known. It was prepared in secrecy, yet every detail was known to those against whom it was equipped. Actually every move in a game which was being played by men whose belief in I their own extraordinary cleverness has seldom been excelled was being overlooked by the enemy. When a searchlight was mounted on a high building in Pretoria "to look for JAMESON " the leader of the expedition was in Pretoria Gaol. Futility indeed I But of Dr. JAMESON and his former colleagues it may be said as of the men praised by LONG- FELLOW—" they builded better than they knew." South Africa owes nothing to their wisdom or their foresight, except, to quote another poet, "a rain of blood and mist of tears" ; but it is rather greatly indebted to their blunders. If there had been no Raid, and no bluffing of President KRUGER, and no Boer War; if CECIL RHODES and Dr. JAMESON had been simply con- tent to mind their own business,which,in the main, was that of making money, the white races in South Africa would have become reconciled and united. Practically, in the long run they would have become of one blood ; but the process would have been spread over many years, and probably over two or three generations. Dr. JamEsorr and his unforseeing associates accelerated matters. Boer and Briton have shaken hands across battlefields, over the graves of their dead. They have already become one people, loyally ] united under the same Crown. with so secure a feeling of fellowship that they are drawing up a Constitution which shall bind them together in a single Commonwealth. What a prodigious work of reconcilement haa been effected under only three years of Liberal Government!</p>
        <p>We are glad to see that THE LEAGUE OF the Warrington Citizens'</p>
        <p>HELP. League of Help is taking definite shape. The objects of the League are EO broad and so excellent that it I ought to receive not only the good wishes, but also !the active help of every citizen. It embodies the true spirit of Christianity so far as i it enjoins upon all the duty of sharing the burden of others. The brotherhood of man is a favourite theme with the preacher; the idea is one which finds universal aeceptance among all civilized peoples, but its personal and individual application to life as we find it in this restless and work-a-day world is often faulty and inefficient. Still, there are unmistakable indica- tions that the age of altruism is drawinv slowly nearer, and such an organisation as the League of</p>
        <p>have the warm glow of fratorn4l love allied with practical common-sense then the new " brother- hood" ought to be fruitful in blessings not only to those who stand in need of help, but to those who give it. And, perhaps, after all the latter is the larger good.</p>
        <p>The long suffering ratepayer A LOWER will derive a crumb of corn- EDUCATION RATE, fort from the estimates of the Warrington Education Committee which will be submitted to that authority on Monday. Since education came on the rates, the cost has shown a persistently upward tendency and it is therefore a pleasant surprise to find that this year the committee will be able to make a reductin of one penny in the. 2 in their demand. The deficiency to be met by the ratepayers is £18,039 103. as compared with £19,129 lls. 31 and the difference is about equal to a penny in the 2. The explanation of the reduction is simple. It has hitherto been the practice of the authority to set apart about a penny in the 2 in each year's estimates to build up a working balance and that reserve has now reached such a substantial amount that the committee will be asked to dis- continue the levy. List year the amount allocated to the balance was £lOOO. The rate for educational purposes at present represents is. 6d. in the 2 and though the drop to is. Si. is comparatively small it will be eagerly welcomed. But this is not the full extent of the concession. The instalment of £l,OOO towards the working balance included in last year's estimates will not be required for that purpose and a reolution will be proposed authorising the treasurer to reduce the final call upon the rates for the current year by that sum. This means that an extra £l,OOO will be available for other committees of the Corporation which may have unusually heavy demands upon their resources, and therefore the relief to the rates will practically be equal to 2d. in the 2. The decrease in the education rate is especially gratifying in view of the extra and unavoidable burdens thrown upon the ratepayers in several departments. For instance, £6OO is to be devoted to medical inspection—an increase of £300; £550 to the feeding of school children—on advance of £250 ; while the increases of teachers' salaries in accordance with the scale represent £6OO. Against these amounts, however, we are fortunately able to set a number of substantial additions to the income of the authority. Customs and excise duties are Qxpected to yield £155 more than last year. The Government grant in respect of the Secondary School is put down at £l5O more, owing to the difference in the rats of grant, while the increase of grants to the branch technical schools is calculated at £320, this being due to the new and improved organization of this important branch of education. Elementary school grants will total £lOO more than last year and there will be an additional £2OO for grants with respect to special subjects—cookery and handicraft. The grant which the committee will be required to make to the Warrington Grammar School is estimated at £2OO less than last year. On the year's working, therefore, the require- ments from the rates would show very little difference as compared with 1907-8, and the saving of one penny is entirely attributable to the striking out of the contribution towards the working balance.</p>
        <p>Mr. Haldane' latest con- IMPERIAL DEFENCE. tfibution to the solution of</p>
        <p>the problem of Imperial Defence is decidedly interesting. He announces that the Government is in negotiation with the Dominions overseas with a view to the creation and constitution of an Army of the Empire, and not of this country merely. That has been an idea in the minds of many people ever since the Boer War revealed the weakness and the strength of the Empire. It has been felt that lack of organisation has wasted the best fighting material in the world. But there have been great MD.- culties. The Colonies until lately have not taken kindly to the idea of Imperial military forces. Canada has insisted on taking over her own Militia.. rind organising her defence. Australia Iris acted similarly to some extent, and,although there has been a profession of co-operation, there is obviously no home-geneity in the constitution of such Imperial forces as are here represented. We believe that in time the Colonies will come to realise their obligation to make some proper con- tribution to defence as an integral part of the Empire. They are, undoubtedly, tending in that direction, and if the Government can hasten their decision so much the better. Logically it is absurd that the people of these islands should be called upon to provide the security, both naval and military, for the whole of the British Empire.</p>
        <p>Help has a distinct and proper place in the scheme !'''Capital, though very glibly talked about by those of things terrestrial. The success of eimilkr sapient Socialist economists who did not know the otganisations is Germany, and in the fifty towns difference between a blank cheque and a bill of exchange, is as elusive as a sunbeam and as sensitive which have formed leagues in this country should , inspire Warrington with _a great hope. If we can as the sensitive plant.—Mr. Claude Lowther.</p>
        <p>* * * Another large batch of Chinese left the Rand in January, the account now standing as follows: Total number of Cbinese coolies admitted 63,453 Largest number on any one date (January 31, 1907) 53,856 Number on January 31, 1909 Reduction since January 31, 1907 It will be seen that four-fifths of the Chinese have already been sent back and that only just over 10,000 are left—all to go before the year is out. * * * In another column we publish a report, sent in by an advocate of Women's Suffrage, of an abortive meeting in Manchester called by the anti-suffragists. Our correspondent seems to have enjoyed the dis- comfiture of her opponents, but she lets them down sa gently that we quite willingly grant her request to announce that the National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies is mustering its forces to attend afternoon service in Westminster Abbey on Monday next, the day before the opening of Parliament ; in order to make the service, in effect, one of intercession for the furtherance of the Votes for Women cause during the coming session. We are relieved to find that, even yet, the suffragists do not consider Parliament past praying for.</p>
        <p>* * Sir John Brunner, P,C., M.P. for the Northwich. Division of Cheshire, the son of a Swiss schoolmaster, arrived on Monday at the age of sixty-seven. He is, in a way, a sort of home-made Mr. Carnegie, for he has established free libraries, town halls, and scholar- ships with a lavish hand. A favourite remark of his is that every penny he has made has come from the application of science to commerce. As chairman of Messrs. Brunner, Mond and Co.. he has had ample opportunities, for few firms have made such huge and consistent profits from scientific manufacture. We wish Sir John many more happy returns, and note with interest that he is renewing his youth by member- ship of the Cannes Croquet and Lawn Tennis Club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>"The Liberals have drawn a red herring across the track, but I can see through it," said Colonel Hall Walker, M.P., in a speech at Widnes on Monday night. This beats spiritualism or crystal-gazing. The Colonel is a very superior kind of medium if he can see through a "red herring." But perhaps it had been gutted?</p>
        <p>* A good story is told of a late brother of Mr. Dickens, K.C., which proves that the paternal humour had to some extent been inherited by the son. This Mr. Dickens was a member of the Australian Parlia- ment and frequently crossed swords in debate with another member named Willis. On one occasion, when his opponent had been specially provocative, the late Mr. Dickens paused in his speech and said : "My late father coined the phrase, Tarkis is willing'; I might add now that Willis is barking." This spicy bit of Dickensian humour doubtless had the desired crashing effect on the aforesaid Willis.</p>
        <p>* * * At the public meeting of the recently formed Women's Circle, in the Town Hall on Tuesday there was some excellent speaking on Working Girls Club work. Mrs. Hards, of Manchester, told a little story of her experience at a Working Girls' Club, which will, be appreciated at Silver-street. She had been impressing the girls with the enormity of speaking to a man in the street without first having made his acquaintance in the orthodox manner when one of them chimed in with the disconcerting query: "How would you get a bloke then?" As Mrs. Hards had not then got her own" bloke "she was unable to give such an answer as satisfied the questioner.</p>
        <p>* * * - Most of our young debaters are glad enough if they can make one" point ' in the local Parliament, and we feel sure that they will welcome the chance of Isecuring one hundred really effective fighting points. The mode of the moment in political literature would seem to be the " 100 Points' model. An excellent lexample of the mode comes to as from the Li beral Publication Department (42, Parliament-street, S.W.), in " 100 Points in Liberal Policy and of the Liberal Record." Every page has its own particular point to make, and makes it clearly and convincingly ; we notice with much satisfaction that care is taken to provide the true particulars about such favourite Tory red herrings as Horse- shoes, Woolwich, Army discharges, and what not. The little book is admirably suited for the pocket, and we imagine that every Liberal who sallies out to speak or to canvass will always take care to be armed with all the "100 Points." The little book is very well printed, and only costs a penny, or post free 2d.</p>
        <p>If we fail against the Lords we shall fasten the grip of the Old Man of the Sea tighter than ever round our necks. —Dr. Alaenamara, M.P. We are the greatest manufacturing country in the world, and the qualities that made us so still exist. But we do not equip ourselves properly.—The Earl of Ilalsbury. The Conservative Party say rariff Reform will stop unemployment. There is no assertion more superla- tively ithotic,more flagrantly and palpably absurd.— Mr. Winston Churchill. More good would be done by instilling into the minds of young children that they should avoid alcohol than by taking steps to prevent people obtain- ing drink when they have acquired a taste for it. —The Lord Chief Justice. For Influenza and Bronchial Coughs,Wooda' Great Peppermint Cure. la. lid., 22. 9d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Local Successes. Mr. T. C. Locker, son of Councillor Locker, and Mr. G. 11. Warburton Steel have both been successful in passing the University of Cambridge local exanaina: tions. Mr. Warburton Steel is, we understand, taking his articles for the law with his brother, Councillor I'. S. Steel. LINSEED COMPOUND 'for Coughs and Colds. Of proven efficacy. 9id. &amp;Is. Id. Of Chemists only.—Advt. Cemetery Ball, The 11th annual ball in connection with the Cemetery Bowling Club was held on Thursday night at the Lion Hotel. There were about 100 present. Mr. Starkey's band provided the music, and Messrs. A. T. Jones, H. Willcocks, and J. Willett acted as M.C.'s. Refreshments were provided by Mr. J. Lee. Cookery Lessons. Messrs. Brown and Polson announce that they have arranged for a series of cookery lessons to be given by Miss W. Stoke-Vaughan, diplomde, National Train- ing School of Cookery, at the Hall Caf6, Stockton Heath, from February 15th to 19th. There will be an afternoon lecture daily from 3 to 4 o'clock, and an evening lecture from 7.15 to 8.15 o'clock and useful baking utensils will be on sale at moderate prices. LINSEED COMPOUND with warm water is an excellent gargle for sore throat.—Advt. Mr. Treyeiyan, M.P. to Visit Warrington. A public meeting on education, promoted by the Lancashire County Association of National Union of Teachers, will be held at the Parr Hall, on Friday, February 26th, when Mr. C. P. Trevelyan, (Parliamentary Secretary to the Board of Education) will deliver an address. The speakers will include Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. AN ARTICLS no housewife should be without.— See special offer for six weeks in Miscellaneous Column.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Progressive Labour League. The annual tea, concert and meeting in connection with this League will be held at the Parr Hall on Saturday evening next, and promises to be a highly successful gathering. Mr. A, H. Crosfield, M.P., and Mrs. Crosfield will be present. Tea will be served at 5 p.m., and the concert and meeting will follow at 7.30. The artistes will be :—Miss E. Taylor, soprano; Mr. J. B. Poole, bass; Warrington Apollo Quartette; Mr. Bert Hollingsworth, humorist. Tickets for the tea, price Gd. each, and may be obtained from any member of the committee or from Mr. J. Howes, plan, at the Liberal Club. BARGAINS in Silver-plated Teapots, Crnets, Pre- serve Dishes, etc., during Sale.—J. S. GA.RLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Cricketer's Death. Local sportsmen will hear with deep regret of the death, which took place at the Warrington Infirmary on Wednesday, of Alfred Thewlis, who for many years had been a prominent member of the Warrington Cricket Club. He came to Warrington from York- shire about ten years ago as groundsman and professional to the Warrington club. He soon established himself a favourite amongst the playing members and the supporters of the club. On his retirement as professional a few years ago he joined the team as a playing member. With both bat and . ball he was equally skilful, and he always figured prominently in the averages. His death was due to a ) seizure at work early in the previous week. PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER MN CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS fl LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Councillor J. T. Locker's Long Voyage. , Our readere will be interested to hear that yesterday Councillor J. T. Locker (the respective hon. secretary of the Warrington Liberal Association) set sail for . South Africa on a business visit for the firm of Messrs , T. Locker and Co., Ltd., wire weavers. Mr. Locker , will probably be away for three months at least, and , on Thursday evening a number of members of the Subscription Room at the Liberal Club made him a , small presentation as a token of good-fellowship. Mr. John Worrall, Alderman Tinnion, and Councillor Wilkinson voiced the prevailing sentiment and wished Mr. Locker a pleasant voyage and a safe return. PUBLIC BEITEFIT.—IINDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCA.SHIRE.—Advt. ' Ancient Order of Foresters. Court Warrington No. 430 held their annual tea and social at the Blue Lion Inn, Market Place, on Saturday evening, when about 80 to 100 Foresters and friends sat down to a capital tea catered for by Bro. Lake in his usual good style. Tea being over, P.D.C.R. Bro. W. R. Smith took the chair, and was supported by D.S.C.R. Bro. Grayson, D.S. Bro. L. Davies, C.S. Bro. J. Chrimes, Bros. Clayton, James, Healey and others. The toast of the King was pro- posed by the .Chairman and received with musical honours after which Mr. W. Martin gave a pianoforte solo. Bro. Grayson then gave the toast of the Ancient Order of Foresters which was followed by a song from Mr. G. H. Bennett after which Bro. W. R. Smith rose to reply to the last toast and spoke in glowing terms of the good work of the Order and of its members and prosperity. Another portion of the musical programme was proceeded with and then Bro. L. Davies was asked to make a presentation of a framed Emblem to Bro. W. R. Smith. Bro. Davies in making the presentation referred in well chosen words to the excellent way in which Bro. Smith had served the cause of Forestry both in his Court, in the District and also as a delegate to the High Court at Northampton and on Bro. Smith rising to reply he was received with much enthusiasm and with musical honours. The toast of the Warrington District A.0.F., was in the capable hands of Bro. E. A. A. Clayton and was - suitably responded to by D.T. Bro. P. Peacock. Mr. G. H. Bennett then delighted his audi- ence by giving them comedy sketches representing various leading artistes in the world of comedy. D. A. Bro. G. W. R. Baines undertook the honour of the toast to Court Warrington No. 430 and after a song by Mr. J. T. Stout, C.S. Bro. J. Chimes rose to ! respond and spoke at length as to the history of the Court daring the last 45 years. It was interesting to note that in the period mentioned the membership had , risen from 70 to 382 and the Court funds which in , 1865 amounted to £B6 now stood at over £1,600- , After a duet by Messrs. Bennett and Martin, two pre- , sentations to Bro. J. Fowler also presentations to Bros. J. Gregory and J. Jeans for services rendered to the Court were made by Bro. Grayson and suitable replies were made by the recipients. The toast to the artistes was efficiently proposed by Bro. F. Taylor as also was one by Bro. W. James to the Host and Hostess and replied to bring a very enjoyable evening to a close. Mr. W. Martin was the accompinist. ; CLEARANCB S&amp;Ls of Table Lamps, Hanging Lamps, etc., great bargains.—J. 5, UARLAND, 511, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SALARY INCREASES AT WIDNES. Widnes Town Council met on Tuesday, the Mayoe (Cokencillor T. Smith) presiding. Five of the nin- committees recommended increases of salary. The pror illnsal which aroused the greatest amount of increases of salary. The proposal which aroused the greatest amount of discussion was that of the borough surveyor. for whom an increase from £350 to £375 was suggested Alderman Quinn stated that, although the time for increases of salary was inopportune, this Would be the surveyor's first advance for rteen years, and much work had been done bY the uEpartmeut during the last few years.- -The only serious opponents of the grant were Mr. bilurst and Mr. G. Davies.—The last-mentioned gentle- Iliac said that he did not judge a servant upon the amount of money that passed through his hands, but nPuu the amount saved.—The next item of interest was that which referred to the taking over by the Corpora- tilun of the duties of everseers, granted by the recent ''et ,of Parliament for Widnes, wheieby it was resolved to take the late overseers' assistant and Vac° him in the town Hall at a salary of :150 per annum. As this had set up a grievance ?tuungst the old staff in the Accountants' Department, 11 was recommended also that eleven members in .4,04 department be granted increases totalling 1:100. Pon the recommendation of the Education Committee, It was decided to increase the salary of the head master (Mr. J. A. Cooper) from £3OO to £3so.—The eqenditure last year and the estimates for the coming -Year drew from many of the members much favourable tenittn, ent--Alderman Owens, said the amount required iii;mirch 1910, was £16,126, and they were in such a b,;"rable position that in all probability there would a reduction in the district rate of Id. or 2d. RUNCORN LICENSING SESSIONS. MAGISTRATES AND THE LONG PULL. the annual licensing sessions for the Runcorn Petty :iessional Division were held on Monday. Mr. Fred J. (31.roarl presided over a full bench.—Superlntendent v.nnio uin his annual report said there are 44 licensed Ictuallers, 49 beersellers, and 3 others who sell 01l the rPtetaisea—total 96, giving an average of 234 of the Pulation to each licence. One licensed victualler beeU proceeded against, and not convicted, the as in the preceding year. No beersellers had been n?9eded against. Seventy-nine persons had been pro- lateaed against for drunkenness, and 69 convicted, as `4°LitUPared with 109 proceeded against and 94 convicted s,2ll:ng the preceding year. At the adjourned licensing sof 1908, 5 beerhouses in Runcorn were referred InPensationand one,the Theatre Vaults had closed. "t7lXChairman said the bench considered this a highly a:tory report. The bench said they had decided the ak a strong line with regard to the long pull. In bet_rat place they did not think it fair competition -nriuus thing „ :Yuen one licensed victualler and another. A most Upset, th also was that the long pult was likely to each e calculations of the compensating authority if re a house had to be closed. They would seriously phrsTer the licence of a house that gave the long With th be whole of the licences would be renewed, Dropo,ecl e exception of the following ten, which it was r oR to refer to the compensation authority : POiret Jae, Halton; Bird in Hand, Halton-road ; atreeta.vec,rn, Alcock-street ; Friendship Tavern, Fryer- °loop lan, Lock-street; Blue Bell, Church- street ; Rutter's Vaults, Brunswick-street; Welsh Cooper-street ; Oddfellows' Arms, Wellington- Inaker ItT. L. and Swan Inn, Shaw-street.—Mr. H. , who represented the Licensed tVhitnuoallers' Association, said the association viewed there ng Pull in the same light as the magistrates ; but the e_Was another thing, that was outside the spirit of had b' and thenenme rich's recommendation, that in several houses it the custom to reduce the ad. beer to sd. te The 4d. beer to 3d. This was not fair competition. but Etastd ° sociation was anxious to promote temperance, might eto taliaAr---, and an expression from the bench was of orking tinesvio, ° away with this unfair competition.—Mr. With I,js aaid surely the bench would not interfere men getting cheaper beer so long as it quality--The bench retired, and on return- Chairman said the magistrates were unani- so,,"Y in favour of not interfering with anyone who "4 good beer, no matter how cheap they sold it.</p>
