      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLITE TO THE PRETTY CASHIER, FINED BY THE SHOPWALKER. Mr. Seddon M.P. for Newton, addressed a meeting of eh° , th. P assistants in Aberdeen, and warmly denounced ...-, aYstero of fines and deductions to which shop "'. alstants are often subjected. He referred particularly "° a firm in Liverpool who, he said, have a book of Y over sevent rules; and he defied the Archangel Gabriel to P:work for that firm without being mulcted in fines. ftiplinary punishment was meted out to an assistant or not eating his pudding, to a girl for wearing an engagement-ring, to another for having toothache, to e!i assistant for being too polite to a pretty cashier in Fa: preeence of the shopwalker, and to yet another for 0 log less Tub to an old, grumpy bargain-hunter. The Rev R,,.I.IrArZeD:OK CHURCH DIFFICULTY. S. Stoney, curate-in-charge of the at thP rish of St. nas, etr°rni rk' Haydock, on Sunday referred ng service to the di iiiculty e patronage as to had le which had arisen Liv of the living and the reasons that d him to send his resignation to the Bishop of chuercri, and to suspend the work of building the waitnew patient! lie counselled the congregation to i th hands Y the course of events. The matter was n e jute e °I the Bishop, who had all along taken a foremost s ..or r.st iding in the affairs of the parish, and arrangement Zs. Raydock into two districts•—Mr. Stoney hulmtee that the Church Council at St. Mark's o de •red Practically, as a condition of their support, t f the outset that the patronage of he apninmi suould be vested in trustees, whom they would to 8 the le The vicar of Haydock, the Rev. F. Ireland, , Patron, and so far he appears determined deeildloain.tam gal his rights. The settlement of the necessary church must be effected before the build- Iv- ij "ropleted and consecrated, and for that reason er the c'ha,. k ' been suspended. The seven trustees which ' •,,„°reh Council desire to appoint are the Bishop of i.-..,1),?0, Archdeacon Madden, Lard Newton, Messrs. Uhl• ' alcington, C. R. Pilkington, John Robinson, and es Glover (churchwarden of Lowton).</p>
