      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>&gt;</p>
        <p>COMPULSORY MILITARY TRAINING..</p>
        <p>A Few Criticisms.</p>
        <p>ARTICLE I. " If Europe should ever be ruined, it will be by its warriors."</p>
        <p>Montesquien.</p>
        <p>It was with considerable diffidence I responded to the call of the Editor, to contribute a series of articles criticising Mr. Townroe's statements on "Compulsory Military Training." I felt it a duty to raise my voice in earnest protest against what I have consistently looked upon as an insidious attempt to foist upon the country an autocratic military regime (which is ever unfriendly to democratic aspirations), and consequently I accepted the opportunity. For some of the teasons why we so fundamentally differ I hope to make quite plain. It is impossible to meet Mr. Townroe's state- ments in consecutive order, because the articles lack continuity and frequently become redundant. For all practical purposes, I think it will be sufficient to criticise his main contentions. A careful perusal of his articles shows clearly that they have been specially designed to capture the democracy. Presumably, there is little difficulty in securing the adherence of the aristrocracy and a certain portion of the monied classes but their task begins when the democracy is confronted. Ido not envy him and his friend of their task. By cleverly-devised schemes they may succeed in enlisting the support of a portion of the democracy use the word democracy in the sense in which it is usually accepted -hut that portion will not be the thinking section. I am painfully conscious of the dense ignorance and selfishness of large portions of the people still influenced by prejudice and false teaching of history. Their outlook is narrow and they fail to perceive the real issues of this question. By assiduously working up a panic, it is comparatively easy to call into action those brutal and combative elements still rooted in their nature. But only an unenlightened democracy can possibly respond to the demand of the National Service League. At the outset he tells us the familiar story from Alsop, which could not have been more appropri- ately chosen as a commencement of his articles, for is not war and the dispositions leading to war, much nearer akin to the savagery of the jungle than to the enlightenment of a wisely-ordered society whose foundations rest upon ethical principles. Ur. TOWllroe proceeds on the assumption, which is the underlying and root fallacy of the article " that war has always been the normal relation between nationalities and consequently cannot see how it can be otherwise in the future." Apparently he assumes this position quite unconsciously, but nevertheless it is very strik- ing and certainly typical of the supporters of the League. We are advised to "see to our army before the enemy appears." Whether wars have been neces- sary and beneficial in the past to human progress, we need not stay to discuss. The question is : " Has not civilised humanity arrived at a point in its pro- gress when difficulties arising between so called enlightened peoples should be settled by other means than war? In a later article I will explain at length what I consider to be the great humane movements re-shaping mankind into a conscious and a "higher" unity, which will leave no occasion for war. I conceive war and the dispositions leading to war to be an ugly anachronism in modern time, a relic of barbarism from the dark past. War is wholesale m irder. The Military Moloch attacks our moral sense and blunts all ideas of right and justice. I approach war not as a problem to solve but as a crime to abandon. It abrogates morality. For To make a death which Nature never made, The foulest stain and scandal of our nature, Becomes its boast. One murder makes a villain,</p>
        <p>Millions a hero. And anything that tends to maintain and further establish those conditions should be stoutly opposed. We are advised to " see to our army before the enemy appears." But who is our enemy, and why? • Great Britain and the U.S.A. are close friends, for are not the people of the States largely our own kith and kin? And our relations with France are most cordial. There is absolutely no tangible disagreement between ourselves and any other continental nation. The international horizon almost unclouded. Of course, at the back of the mind of our friend, the one great trouble is Germany. But why Germany? And the retort will come " Look at her great and powerful army and the rapidity with which she is building her navy !" "Read her hostile Press, even move among the Germans, and then see if you do not come to our conclusion, viz, : " She is preparing to deal a deadly blow to this country and the Empire in order to become the greatest p )wer in the world." I wish our friend and his colleagues, would try and catch the view point of our German cousins and be a little more candid as regards the rapid develop- ment of our own Navy these last fifteen years and see whether the Germans are altogether in the wrong. Of course the Germans have made the fatal blunder in setting the pace and we are bound to do likewise in honourable defence of ourselyes. So say the supporters of the League. But do these statements square with the facts? Did not the impetus come from us in 1893-4 on account of the suspicion we enter- tained towards France and Russia, having as we thought secretly combined with the avowed object of crushing us Fortunately, no quarrel took place, the panic was ill-founded and to-day our relation with the former power is a peaceful one. Russia need not be considered. And so the seat of unrest has been gradually moved from France to Germany. When these last 39 years are taken into account, one can partially understand the cause of Germany's plungiug into these great military and naval schemes. Geographically, Germany is much different from ourselves. She is not" an isle set in the silver sea." At the same time, I con- demn these developments in Germany, as I do else- where, as much as I do those at home, but do let us try and be honest with ourselves and confess the blame lies in no small degree, at our own doors. We have certainly led the way in naval rivalry, and to-dav we are being asked to further increase the strength Of our navy, and organise our army on a basis of citizen- ship, by compelling every youth to servo. am seriously concerned, not so much with the numerical increase, although that is very considerable, as with the spirit that prompts the increase. It is not so much war, bat the war spirit that needs attacking. It is quite possible Mr. Townroe will consider all this as irrelevant, but I contend we are now touching the vital parts of this controversy. I want to get to a close examination of the spirit that pervades the whole of the articles. Let us locate the cause of this unrest. Is it not produced by the foolish jealousies of the small, yet noisy and powerful nava'l and military factions in both countries ? But where is the semblance of a quarrel betwixt the people of Germany and the people of our own country ? Mr. Tovvnroe talks about a few patriotie men who are ready to work while others shirk. Patriotism ! What is Patriotism ? It is customary for the supporters of the League to use and monopolise that blessed word, Patriotism. They cannot conceive of any man being patriotic, unless he bears a rifle, learns how to shoot and undergoes military training. Of course, if a man believes in the right of maintain- ing armaments, his only logical course is to join the ranks else he's a coward. This grievous problem will never be satisfactorily solved until each individual begins the reformation in his own heart, by becoming peaceable in spirit. The great stumbling block is owing to our waiting for each other. For loving all men dearly is deferred</p>
        <p>Till all men love each other. The example of George Fox needs emulation "I told them," says Fox, when they were trying to enlist him in the army of the Commonwealth, that I lived in virtue of that life and power which does away with the occasion of nil war.' But more hereafter under this head. May I ask "Are there not other ways a man can give evidence of sincere affection for his country " ? What about the injustices in his own country? What about those unfortunate men around him whose lot is too often near starvation point through no fault of their own? And what about the little children born in slums, whom!, lives are ruined before they have had a reasonable chance to overcome their evil surround- ings? Born in slumdom amid squalor and vice and the curses of the drink, and pitilessly thrown on the scrap help of humanity ! Cannot we be as loyal and patriotic to our country in helping to cleanse it from , these plague spots as any General training an army to fight? Moreover, the democracy by its growing I enlightenment is coming more aid more to see that its real enemies are not the . Germans, but the men who selfishly lock up the land from the people and those who entice the lower passions of men by the allurements of the drink traffic, and the slum landlord who waxes fat on the ill-earned gains of slumdon, etc., etc. I make bold to say that the enemies to social well-being are those within our own gates. The same</p>
        <p>is true of Germany and the German people are grada ally learning these great truths. Are not these the conditions that have given rise to Modern International Socialism ! The Revolutionary Social Democrats, so plentifully scattered throughout the continent of Europe, tell their own tale. True patriotism consists in serving each other and liberating our fellows from those vices that so closely beset them and not in maintaining national hatred. Later, we will look at this fetish of nationalism. Why maintain national hatred and suspicion of that nation, with whose people we have had no quarrel. Is it not time the two democracies shook themselves free from this military incubus ? (1) We refuse to be led as "dumb-driven cattle into their strife " We are human and the just tribute to human dignity we expect. Why should the peoples be treated as " pawns' or "mere mechanical units."</p>
        <p>As Lowell sings : We owe allegiance to the State, but deeper truer, more To the sympathies that God hath set within our spirit's core, Our country claims our fealty, we grant it so, but then, Before man made us citizens, Great Nature made us men.</p>
        <p>Yes, Democracy is learning that it has often been fooled in the past by kings and nobles and not much longer is it going to be hoodwinked by financial magnates ambitious soldiers and by astute politicians.. ......_ ..</p>
        <p>Cowper in" The Task" says "War's a game, were their subjects wise, Ring's should not play at." Would Mr. Townroe be good enough to tell us of the good that would accrue if this country fought and beat Germany? Of what use would it be to the people? And, moreover, would it not simply extend and strengthen our own military regime? It would certainly bring about a severe economic loss to both countries and to the world at large and worse still, moral degradation. It would produce more and greater evils than it sought to, destroy, both to this country and to Germany. He states that the League will secure " sound physical development for every British boy in every British school.' As the question of physical development is merely subsidiary to the military issue I do not propose to say more than a few words about it, as it is desirable that we should discuss our fundamental differences and not deal at length with matters of secondary import- ance but in the above statement that he makes he forgets or overlooks the fact that in all real einca- tional schemes which are essentially different from militarism, the physical well-being of our children and youths is a prime consideration and. that it is not compulsory military training which will serve that end but education itself. The difference is radical. And to assert that militarism develops the morale of a youth, in my opinion is immoral. Generally speaking —for I am fully aware of many notable exceptions —the men who are engineering this and similar movements, and so anxious to improve the physique and morale of our youths are the self- same men who oppose the social reformers in their endeavours to-re-construct society on a sounder and more ethical basis.</p>
        <p>Next week we will consider that sublime formula To secure peace, we must prepare for war." Warrington, Ii AROLD GRAY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <p>Examiner Saturday, February 13, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>ANTI-SUFFRAGE.</head>
        <p>ANTI-SUFFRAGE.</p>
        <p>An Abortive Meeting in Manchester.</p>
        <p>(CONTRIBUTED.)</p>
        <p>The Anti-Suffragists bad their first meeting in Manchester on February 3rd at the Rusholme Publio Hall. Mr. Saxon Mills presided. He said that Anti- Suffragists did not intend to adopt militant tactics. Those who claimed the suffrage said they represented the women of the country, but the experience of Anti- Suffragists was that they had on their side the sensible, normal women of the nation. He said that if the amount of suffrage that is being demanded were given, only middle and upper class women could get the vote, Very few married women even of that class would, get it, because to qualifv,they would have to become their husbands' lodgers. This would be deplorable, Mr. Mills seemed to think. No Government, he said, would con- sent to reinforce the property vete in this way. But it would not end there. Women would go on demand- ing votes till women of the lower classes and married women got the vote; they would demand womanhood suffrage. Then the country would be under the control of a class that did not contribute any organised forces to the country. Miss Fothergill was the second speaker. She said women claimed the vote because of their old instinct of wanting what they could not have. They could not obey even the laws that were in existence. During Miss Fothergill's speech a man suffragist in the audience behaved so rebelliously that it took a police- man to calm him. Miss Carey, the organizing anti- suffragist secretary,then spoke on Imperial grounds, in spite of the fact that women are supposed to be unsound when they got on to such matters- Mr. Charles Hough then spoke and was treated with a certain amount of rudeness by some of the audience. He too spoke of Imperial affairs. A voice from the body of the hall asked him to speak of municipal affairs Unfortunately he had not time to give due considera- tion to his next remark, which took the form of a rhetorical question, not meant to be answered, " What woman," he asked, "knows anything about municipal affairs ?" That was unfortunate because it is one of the tenets of the Anti-Suffrage League that women can do and do do, grand work in municipal affairs. To make matters worse, the question was answered with shouts of "Miss Ashton," unmistakably enthusiastic. Mr. Hough was obliged to admit that she was an exception, and did know something about municipal affairs. The audience a fter that became unruly and asked a wearisome lot of questions. They weren't content with handing in on paper, they insisted on asking them verbally, but man said he hadn't only one question to ask, he had two hundred and fifty. Miss Fothergill was asked why she adopted a dog-in- the-manger policy. She denied such a policy. The Anti-Suffragists had made a statement that they were non-political and did not canvass. Some curious person wanted to know why they had gone from house to house on Anti-Suffragist business in Victoria Park. A resolution had been formulated by the Anti- Suffragists, but they thought it prudent not to put it to the vote. However a Suffragist in the audience rose and put a motion in favour of Women's Suffrage. It was carried by about two to one. majority.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>LAUNCH OF A WARRINGTON STEAMER.</head>
        <p>LAUNCH OF A WARRINGTON STEAMER.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday last at Maryport the launch took place of a new steamer built to the order of the Bishop's Wharf Carrying Company, which will ply between Liverpool and Warrington upon the Company's business. The vessel has been designed by Messrs. John Esplea and Sons of Liverpool. She Is described as being 80ft. long between perpendiculars, 18ft. Gin, in breadth, built in steel and screw propelled. She has been built by Messrs. William Walker and Sons and it was at their yard at Maryport that the launch and christening cere- mony took phiea last Tuesday. Great interest was manifested by the inhabitants of the town who assembled in large numbers upon the bill overlooking the wharf and crowded to every point of vantage. The builders had invited a number of the leading inhabi- tants, and these attended and watched the ceremony with much interest. The designers of the vessel were represented by Mr. Shirley Esplen. The vessel was gaily decorated, and at 2 o'clock, all being in readiness, Miss Amy Jones, the daughter of the , manager of the company, gracefully performed the ceremony of christening. Taking the bottle of wine, she said "1 christen thee Nonie.' Good luck, and God bless all who sail in thee." The vessel immedi- ately began to move, the bottle of wire suspended by I ribbons from the bow being at once broken by the con- tact. She took the water with a great splash, to the usual cry of" Now she's of!" from the workmen, and the hurrahs of the numerous spectators. On the invitation of the builders the party were entertainted to refreshments and several toasts heartily given. Mr. William Walker proposed the toast of Messrs. Bishop Wharf Co..wishing them every prosperity and lasting success. lie expressed regret that the launch had not taken place on the previous Saturday, as originally arranged, as it would have given them great honour to have had the presence of the Chairman of the Shipping Company, Mr. W. Peter Rylands, of War- rington with his wife to perform the christening cere- mony. Every effort had been made to get the work completed but the weather had caused delay. Mr. E. Jones responded on behalf of Bishops Wharf Co., and also proposed the toast to the builders of the vessel wishing them all success. Mr. Trail then proposed the health of Miss Amy Jones, the six-year-old who had so happily performed the ceremony of the day. The " Nonie "is being fitted with engines by Messrs Gauldie, Gillespie and Co., of Glasgow. She will be towed to the Clyde and when completed will bring her first load from thence to Warrington.' She is expected to arrive on Monday, March Ist and will doubtless be , received with much interest. And as the advent of a 1 new steamer is an indication of the development of our shipping industry we may predict that she will receive a hearty welcome.</p>
        <p>I know a boon to all the nation, Which scarcely needs much demonstration, Considering six folks out of nine Declare its virtues are divine! But some poor folks there may remain Who do not know this cure for pain; Know all men, therefore, that for aura, Colds fly before Woods' Peppermint Cure.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>TARIFF REFORM DEBATE</p>
        <p>CONTINUED.</p>
        <p>Our local Parliament resumed its debate on Alderman Bennett's Bill for the Federation of the Empire in the Conservative Club Assembly Room on Tuesday evening. The speeches, as usual, turned largely on the question of tariff reform, which is the most important, section of the measure. Next. week a division will be taken and the Government will apparently have to muster all the forces at their command to escape defeat. Mr. F. A. JENKINS (C., Scotland Division of Liver- pool), Postmaster General, supported the Bill in its entirety. The present so-called Free Trade was possibly all right for us in the days of Cobden but to-day it would not do at any price. Whv were the Corporation making such a noise about Warrington Bridge ?—lt did for Warrington fifty years ago and why would it not do to-day? For the simple reason taat we were living in an enlightened age. Tariff Reform meant that every mad who used our market should pay a fair price for that right and that the burden upon British industry,this bonus given to the foreigner should be withdrawn. The Conservative policy was " A fair field and no favour." Tariff Reform meant mote work for the worker of this couptry and that the home market should bo conserved for our own industries. Mr. H. G. ROBERTS (b. Montgomeryshire) supported the rejection of the Bill not because he was a Radical but because he was a convinced Free Trader. If the foreigner was going to be taxed for the purpose of finding more employment for the British worker, it would not mean the large increase of revenue for our exchequer which tariff reformers claimed for their scheme. They could not have both. Tne speaker quoted from the speech of Mr. Nair Hardie, al.P.,at the recent Labour conference in which he stated that unemploy- ment was of world-wide extent and applied ecinally to Protectionist and Free Trade countries. Uuemploy- meat was not the result of Free Trade or Protection, nor was either of these a remedy. lie root cause was lack of organization in our industrial system. Would the Colonial Secretary guarantee that the duty he pro- posed would remain at 5 per cent? (The Colonial Secretary : "No.") Mr. Roberts quoted figures show— ing how the duty in foreign countries had leapt up. enormously over a period of years. Further he main- tained that the working-classes, and of those classes the very poorest, were going to pay for and away out of proportion the4mount of this duty, whether it was 5, per cent. or 100 per cent. He characterised as an. insult the suggestion that Canada's loyalty to the Empire depended upon the taxation of the food of the people of the home country.. Councillor W. 13, ROBERTS (Sacretary for War) sup- ported the Bill on the ground that the proposed 5 per cent. duty —he wished it were 10—was not prohibitive.. The intention wa% not to levy a prohibitive tax, but to raise a fund for imperial defence by purposes-of a toll, the foreigner should pay for the use of our markets. It ' was acknowledged by all economists that it was not the consumer who paid. the import duties, but that 75 per cent, was paid by the importer. it &amp;duty was to be put upon food, it would only be upon that food which this country could produce, and he hoped and antici- pated that the Colonial Secretary would remove exist- ing food duties, and especially the tea duty for which the workingman was so highly taxed. 111 r. E. litany (N., E. Tipperary) said when trade improved did the 18s. a week man get a rise in. wages, or did thes.veated women and children gets fairer price for their goods? No. It was not tariff reform that was wanted, but the protection of Christian labour laws. The battle of Protection versus Free Trade stank in the nostrils of the working classes, because they knew. they had little amelioration to expect from the exponents of either cause. Mr. F. G. TAYLOR (Attorney General) said that if i ever there was a mistake made n dealing with a Bill of serious intention it was in the effort of the Opposition, to divert the attention of the House from its far- reaching purposes and drag in the shibboleth of Tariff Reform without which they could discuss nothing at the present time. Mr. W. FURNESS (L., N. Bristol) : That is the key- stone of the Bill. _</p>
        <p>Mr. TAYLOR: I call the keystone of the Bill Imperial Federation and I have never heard one real speech made in this House upon anything connected with the Bill but Tariff Reform. 1 say distinctly that in my opinion there is no such thing as Tariff Reform within the meaning of this Bill—(opposition laughter)— in any shape or make. Continuing he said the Bill was ajmeans for providing amongst other things, a remedy forthe cost of the defence of the Ernpire, ao.l it was common ground that the Empire was at present defended by the Mother country, almost alone. ('Shame.") They wanted to remove that shame in an equitable way. Mr. C. H. JOHNST'ONE (L., Dumfries), said the Bill was badly drafted,it showed confusion of conception and thoughtand it was a measure which could not see even as far as the parish pump which the Colonial Secretary said was the limit of the Liberal party's vision. (Laughter.) He defied anybody, to say what the Bill meant and to elucidate the extraordinary clauses of the Tariff Reform section. The Colonial Secretary pro- fessed to belong to the Constitutional party but io this Bill there were provisions so unpatriotic and so uncon. stitutional that they were beyond the wildest dreams of the most advanced Socialist. (Laughtar.) Mr. Johnstone wound up with a reference to the House of Lords. When the question came up, he said,they were going to smash the House of Lards—(Ministerial laughter)—and not only that, but next week when they took a division they were going to smash the Govern- ment. (Opposition cheers.) Mr. G. WthsoN (C., Hythe) said the combined efforts of the Opposition had produced a mountain mouse, it few smatterings of political economy and a strong recommendation to try foreign tomatoes. (Laughter.) He contended that Germany and America had made great advances in prosperity since the introduction of tariffs whereas our exports were diminishing month after month. The French considered that their tariffs were not high enough yet and were preparing to raise them and Japan was arranging tariffs on scientific lines. Ho asked the Opposition seriously to consider the extreme gravity of the position into which we as a nation were drifting and he earnestly solicited their co-operation in passing a Bill which would be Use means of cementing the different units of the Empire. Mr. F. NIAGINN (N., W. Clare) said that from what he had heard that night he could only cry "Oh, the insin- cerity of politicians !" A subject of stupendous import- ance seemed to be treated like a question of log-rolling between two parties. Tne debate so far 11. d been conducted under false pretences. They had been invited to debate imperial federation but they were simply dis- cuseing Tariff Reform. As a profound believer in the the wisdom and indeed the necessity of imperial federation he deplored that fact. He thought the Colonial Secretary was responsible for having turned the debate into that channel. He would be doing a grievous wrong if he attempted to bring an Imperial Council into existence with its hands tied. The proper course would be to bring together the collective wisdom of the Empire and leave it to that assembly to decide whether to impose an imperial tax and what steps they would take for the defence of the Empire. He did not think so meanly of his fellow citizens as to believe that they really wanted to prop up and defend the Empire at the expense of the foreigner. If the Empire was worth defending, we ought to defend it by moans of our own loyalty, co-operation and self-sacrifice, Mr. DARDYSHIRE Davonport) expressed regret that so much ability as that possessed by the Colonial Secretary should run to waste on a pieco of political corruption like Tariff Reform. Liberals must flatly decline to erect a tariff wall against the world, simply that the colonies might benefit at our expense. He believed the German nation at this moment, given an opportunity of expressing itself effectually, would discard Protection. Mr. NIGHTINGALE (S., Normanton) moved the adjournment of the debate until Tuesday evening next when the division will be taken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>AT CANNES.</head>
        <p>AT CANNES.</p>
        <p>The Society papers continue to devote a goad deal of attention to the social events at Cannes, and Mr. and Mrs Crosaeld appear to have been busy with entertaining, golf and tennis. The" World " says: Countess Torby was a constant visitor at the Cannes Croquet and Lawn Tennis Club last week, joining one day in a doubles with Sir John Dickson-Poynder, against Mrs. A. H. Crosfield and Mr. J. A.. Pease, the last-named having the misfortune to sprain his right ankle severely. Countess Zia, Countess Nada and Count Torby have also been playing tennis. Although the Grand Duke does not now keep up his tennis, he is an interested spectator. Sir John and Lady Brunner have become members. On the golf links there has been some attractive play. The Grand Duke Michael,partnered by Mr. A. E. Hedstrom,against Count Limburg-Stirm and`Mr..A. H. Crosfield, found himself beaten by the latter by two and three to play. The Grand Duke Michael and Countess Torby enter- tained Princess of Pleas, Prince and Princess John Ghika, .and Mr. and Mrs. A. 11. Crosileld at Villa Kazbeck. The Casino is looking like " mid-season" during the afternoon and evening. Among other dinner parties given there was one by Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield, whose guests were the Grand Duke and Grand Duchess Cyril, the Grand Duke Michml and Countesa Torby, Lady Cheylestnore, Mr. Henry Chaplin, Sir John Dickson-Poynder,Vicomte Jancourt, Mr. and Mrs. J. A. Pease, Mrs. Frith, and Captain Robert Marshall. On Friday Mr. and Mrs. Von Andr4 invited their friends, mostly those already mentioned, to Isola Bella.</p>
        <p>Take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Coughs and Colds; it never fails. is. lid„ 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>Buttermarket-street Improverffeilts.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Cannot the Corporation have the propety in Buttermarket-street pulled down? The shop at, the corner of Bank-street—no one takes it. It would be' a great help to the unemployed,especially the bricklayeri labourers. The Building trade was never so bad as it is new, it would mean the employment of a number of hands while the cold weather is on.—l am, yours truly S. SEDGE WICK. Mersey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Musical Festival.</head>
        <p>The Musical Festival.</p>
        <p>Sir,--One cannot help but congratulate the Warring- ton Musical Festival Committee upon another of their attempts to eater for the higher tastes of music in Warrington. It is a great uudettaking, entailing great responsibility to carry it through so successfully. The arrangements for competitors 4nd the public worked admirably, and the festival afforded the greatest enjoyment. The, items were all good, mid some excep- tionally so, the various choral contests bring a treat in themselves. Ow was also delighted with the fine vocal and instrumental solos, and I was deeply moved by the beartiful singing of the blind young lady from Man- chestere who was successful in winning two prizes. The Committee, too, are to beam:amended upon their choice of adjudicators. Warrington must indeed be rceilebetter and healthier by these- uplifting festivals, and it was pleasing to see the Mayoress and the e5-Miyor taking such a lively interest lathe proceedings. We il,Te greatly in&amp;Abted •to all who have worked &amp;a, hard to, make the fentival a success, and one can only hope that the financial result will bwsatisfactory.—Yours A WILL WISaEI4-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Free Trade V. Tariff &amp;form.</head>
        <p>Free Trade V. Tariff &amp;form.</p>
        <p>Sir.---My humorous frieud %fr.G VL Et,oel,like °the. , advocates of Tiriff?R;iforin, fights sky of facts on the' I main issue in this controversy. Be is unreble to face, I the music and combat the statement tha• this tight little islaud of oars stands first and forerzost in the world in the volume of trade—and also, that this has chiefly been atte,ifled.by her Vree-TradErpolicy. He, however, raises-the question of unemployment. But surely he is aware that no authority on economics seriously advocates Tariff, Reformets.a.retnedlyc for the fundamental reason that to erect on, artificial, barrier cannot possibly overcome natural law.. In addition many advocates of Tariff 'Reform frankly acknowledge that Tariff Reform wilt not solve the problem. either do we who are Free Traders state that Fiee.Traie will entirely solve it. What we do say, however, ancll'erove, fleets being indisputable, is that Free frede assists, the solution and also that under Free Trade the conditions of the people have vastly improved—vide Beftish History. To come to recent events: What was it that took place at the recent Labour Congress at Portsmouth? I presume Mr. Steel keeps himself informed on these events. Despite Mr. Grayeon's-trip•in a motorcar, the question of unemployment ves discussed. Now if an one should know anything of this sub.:ea he must admit that the Labour representatives and the leaders- of the Trades Unionists do Well did they not declare, and pass a resolution stating • their firm adherence to• the principle of Free Trade and also added that Thrift Reform would aggravate and increase the evils-attached to unemployment ? However, Mr. Steel states -- "Look at the unploy- ment in our midst. See how It has increased.l" True it has. But surely, he is not prepared to make-political party ce,Pital out of that, although no doubt, he will admit that the late war in Seuth. Africai both aggravated and increased unemployment. The evil of unemployment is too serious a matter to become the shuttlecock of any party and no true humanitarian or patriot will lend himself to such means: It is the duty of Governments to assist in the solution of this problem and they can in a great measure assist, but even they cannot finally solve it without the assistance of each and every individual.. net us by all means avoid political humbug on this point V'. Fortunately the evil is being grappled with, although we must admit slowly. Large sums of money have been grunted to local authorities in fact several millions in loans were last year granted by the Local Government Bard for the special object of finding employment. But more will require to be done and under the establishment of Small Holdings, Afforestation Scheme and Labour Colony, great advance towards its final solution will ho reached. Now with regard to comparisons with. other nations, I We are bound to look at other nations to see how they fare. To hear our Tariff Reformers debate on the subject of unemployment, one would imagine, if we did not know otherwise, that other lands were fihwing with milk and honey, and that emoloyment never ceased,and all who dwelt therein were cheerful and happy. Distance lends enchantment to the view !' But what are the facts. Discontent, riots, and unemployment worse than here. In Germany in the early part of 19d3. unemployment more than doubled and in the months of November trebled and in December quadrupled. In the United States (New York State Returns of Trade Unionists) there were actually in the first six months of , the year over 31 per cent unemployed and in fact in the I first three months of 1909 one in four of the Trade Unionists of the New York State out of work continu- ously from January Ist to March 31st. To come to percentages comparing England with other places,they are as follows ::--- England (unemployed) 9, per cent. France lO per cent. Berlin (Germany) l4 per cent. New York (State) ll per cent. how can therefore Tariff. Reformers in the face of facts, attempt to delude the people by stating that Tariff Reform will solve unemployment ? May I-add in conclusion that Tariff Reform admittedly means increased taxation. Fl•ee Ttade means less taxation. The burdens of the people need lightening and more equitably adjusting. And under Free Trade this is gradually being done for, Free Trade means the removal of all restrictions on labour and barriers of taxation and duties on the foods and fruits of the people so that all shall be able to partake freely of what is ' pcovided by nature's great storehouse of which there is a super-abundance. To tax the people's food and labour is a crime.—Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>E. ENGLAND. Mr. Ramsay Macdonald's Criticisms</p>
        <p>- Ram-say. Macdonald when speaking at Warrington the other night used language with refer- ence to Lord Roberts and his league which will do him and his party little good. A week full of N.S.L. meetings prevents me from replying in detail to his remarks—this I shall hope to do next week—but I cannot let his reference to liberty pass unanswered. Mr. Macdonald says to quote his own words" that they still valued their English liberties." It is precisely because we do value them that the National Service League is striving to preserve them by making this country strong to resist attack from without.. Whore will be our much vaunted liberty if our country is invaded, and for the time being Might become Right. It is surely worth while for each young man to give up a small measure of his liberty to prevent such disaster, especially too seeing that by so doing he will be taught lessons which will enable him to make far better use of his freedom for the rest of his life.—l am, etc. OEORbE CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Sir,—There is obviously little or no excuse for the sins of a certain section of our British aristocracy, which the flash-light of the TAW Courts shows op at frequent periods in all thoir gruesome and hideous reality, and _it is probable that of evils less deny revealed there are none greater than those of evtrava- gone() in dress, in luxurious living and in pleasure of various kinds. But when Mr. Ramsay Macdonald, taking in his hands the sword of vengeance, proceeded to "hew Agag in pieces "on the Parr Hall platform,he seemed to forget that every section of the community has its sins and follies.</p>
        <p>Ho informed his hearers that at the present time the "smart set " was dividing its attention between " the Edinburgh Divorce Court" and "the New Play in London." He defines the "smart set" as "that set" in society which has all the airs and graces of distinc- tion, but all the odours of a very badly kept stable. The "smart set," and soldiers like Lord Roberts—about whose soldierlike qualities one had to go to South Africa as he (the speaker) did and find the opinion of the Boer leaders—might issue their manifestoes in order to mislead and misdirect the trend of politioal thought and conviction, but if the Labour Party and the wage- earners were worth their salt they would teach Lord Roberts and his "smart set" that they had not forgotten their political history, that they still valued their English liberties, that they still believed in British democracy and that they were not going to allow a military system to ride over them either with spurs or without. (Applause.) Yes " Applause "without thought, perhaps for the most part, at words which one would have supposed no son of Britain would be coarse enough and ungrateful enough to utter. Ido not think that 20 men of the crowd that while under the subtle influence of political excitement applauded Mr. Macdonald's miserable slur on the name of that distinguished soldier, the grand old man of our modern military history, would repeat the applause if the words were read to them in their quiet moments. Mr. Macdonald's idea of a military system riding over the democracy is obviously absurd. Strictly speaking, there can be no such thing as conscription or even compulsory military service in this conntry, for the simple reason that before any law can be made which would deal in a drastio manner with this question the people would have to be consulted, and the National Service League recognising this is endeavouring to enlighten the public with the ultimate object of persuading them to adopt what it considers to be the best method, that is, paradoxical as it may seam, to volunteer to compel themselves to be trained so as to be capable of defend- their home and country if required. We think it should be plain enough for Mr. Macdonald to see that Lord Roberts and his co-wdrkers believe in the British democracy and have no intention of" riding over them either with spurs or without." G. DALE.</p>
        <p>Warrington; February 10b, 1909.</p>
        <p>:t:›l3s</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln the admirable address given by Mr. Ramsay Macdonald in the Parr Hall the other evening there wai one important point in which his argument :mimed incomplete and inconclusive. It arose when he dealt with the question of unemployment and the effect upon it of inefficiency, intemperance, and the like. The argument was something like this: under present conditions when slackness of trade comes the intemperate and the inefficient are the first to be dis- missed. Suppose that all were temperate and all efficient and there was no difference between the work-</p>
        <p>men, the same number woold have to be discharged bteause there was not work for all. Is not this reason- ing based upon a questionable assumption ? Hive effidency and temperance no bearing upon the call for employment ? Mr. Macdonald's argument appears to assume-that the demand for labour is beyond any con- trol and uninfluenced by the quantity and value of the work produced by efficiency, PARI the waste caused by intemperance. Given greater efficiency arising from general competence, and the preservation of wealth due to the elimination of 'Waste consequent on intemperance, yotr have as a result greater wealth pro- duction and less caste. By the increase and saving of wealth you inevitat4y produce a greater call upon labour. The more capital, or stored ?abour, which is created the larger ne fund employing labour. Idle capital scarcely existev One man may say "'I can see no profitable employment fel, my savings; I will store it by putting it in the bank,"crea workman may say*The money ray mate spends in drink% will save in our Co-uperative society." But the bank or society does not bold it, it is on offer to all the cuslomer traders and manufac- turers of the bank who ciur give satisfactory evidence that the money can he safely lent to them and the society uses it or iII47OSLS it. It will be seen therefore that the measute of the call upon labour is no filed quantity but increttles or decreases accordi ing to the power of previouee labour to enlarge the fund for future employment. We ought not, without further proof, to accept as conclbsive the assumptiba. that unemployment is inevitable, that periods of acute,' depression, or even a proportion of permanent unem- ployment is the natural and inevitable result of present conditions. it is quite probable that under effective 'production, due to individual calwacity and wise 'escrenditure, conditions would arise wNcti would be two for one man instead of, as is olten the case now, two men for one job. am well aware the question is a very large one, and Itiese are a number of complicating fact•Trs which limit tlia.operation of the above principle, but isolating the tar-tors indicated, I contend that efficiency, and the el-mination of waste due to intemperance?-other things being equal, would increase the call for employment ancPtepd to render unnecessary the disrcinsal, which Mr,ll.cdonald consklers inevitable, of efficient and teraerate men. Pecketb,.Feb. sth, 1900.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. FARKER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Parliaanntary Tariff Rearm Debate.</head>
        <p>The Parliaanntary Tariff Rearm Debate.</p>
        <p>Sir,—There is every reason to congratulate the mem- bers of our local Parliament on the character of the Tariff Reform Debate. The speeches on both sides have beeo et a high character, and the opposing views have had ao able setting forth. The Tariff Reform section of the Colon:al Se-3retary's bill has over- shadowed the other clauses in this omnibus measure. It is not probable that the debate has influenced many votes; one reason for this may be that, while excellent points were made on both sides, the debate can hardly be said to have got to the real heart of the economic aspect of the question. I may be pardoned if I attempt to follow out the argument and illustration which, as a member from my native town of Dundee, I introduced to the house. My contention was that any industry not advantageous on an unprotected basis, if made advantageous by being put in a privileged position, would involve the country in a loss proportionate to the rise in price of the articles produced caused by, and dependent upon the proposed tariff. Let us return to the illustration of the tomatoes. We receive from the Spanish Canaries tomatoes to the value of half a million,. It may be supposed by the introduction of the five per cent. tariff it would be possible by the help of glass, to grow a given nu alas- rof tomatoes for 21s. to displace an equal number we now buy from the Canaries, for 20s. The argument of the Tariff Reformers , as put forward by one of the members,was that although the price of tomatoes would be higher by five , per cent. or it shilling in the pounl, the gain to the country would be much greater because we keep in the country and distribute to labour 20s. One of the members asked what benefit was it to this country if the 20s. vent to the Canaries and was distributed in wages in those Spanish posses- sions ? Ido not wish to dogmatise and am quite ready to be corrected,, but it seems to me that this argument is based upon more than one erroneous supposition and deduction- The 20s. for the tomatoes from the C merles does not go to the Canaries at all. Let us examine the facts. We import from the Canaries mostly in tomatoes and bananas, one and a half a million in value. Does this million and a half go from here to pay wages &amp;c., as the member seemed to think? Last year Spain and the CAnaries (the Board of Trade Returns do not separate them) sent us in coin £93,254 in gold and silver, and we sent them £2,131 leaving a balance coming to this country of £96,120. How theft do we pay the Canaries for their minima and a half of produce? Mainly in goods we send in exchange. Our exports may be taken as £900,000 of which £BOO,OOO represent things rnanufac- tured or grown in this country. Practically the whole of the trade both outward and inward is carried in British skips, so that it might be roughly estimated that the shipping trade gave in freights and other charges, not included in the above figures, service to the value of £'loo,ooo, which together with the exports show that we partly liquidated our obligation to the Canaries by £l,loo,ooo,leeving a balance in their favour of .21.00,000. It would be absurd to think that they give us a present of this difference. The explanation. is that the balance is made good through those countries to whom we export in goods, or give in service, more than we receive. But, contends the Tariff Reformer,surely we are bound to pay to the C snarler the excess of value we receive from them.Tlaat is so,but we pay them indirectly. The merchants who buy tomatoes do not send out sovereigns, but pay for what they buy generally by a draft on London. This draft is pat into a bank in the Canaries, the banker or broker in these islands may pay over the draft to a third country in payment of goods received from them, and this third country may use the draft to help to liquidate its debt to England, and not a sovereign in coin passes, confirming what I stated that coin is useful mainly as a measure of value, and this is the principal office in international commerce. May we not take it as conclusive that the idea of money going out of the country to be spent abroad is a fallacy. In point of fact we receive in bullion and coin more than we pay away by about four millions annually. I Let us now come back and trace, if we can, the effect on the national wealth of the call upon labour of trades brought into being by the help of the five per cent. tariff. We see clearly from what has been stated that keeping out foreign goods must necessarily keep in the goods we export in exchange. We export to the Canaries in part payment of tomatoes, etc., about £30,000 value in soap. If we grew our tomatoes here we should necessarily check the export of soap. The manner in which, by the difference of monetary exchange, the check would come about is an aspect of the question that would take more space than I can in this letter claim, but it is an important and interesting plittre of the subject which can be discussed later f necessary. .</p>
        <p>We get at the heart of the question when we turn our attention to the additional shilling, the extra cost of growing tomatoes instead of importing thorn. We have seen that the increase of labour caused by growing tomatoes here is balanced by the diminution of labour on the articles we exchange. But the two do not in product value balance; it means the exchange of 21s. for 20s.—not a profitable transaction. The difference is realized and felt in the spending power of the country, as well as in the waste caused by misdirected labour. If 21s. has to be expended in tomatoes which under present conditions can be got for 208., surely it is clear that the man with 21s. has nothing left when be has bought his English grown tomatoes.lf on the other hand ke can buy similar tomatoes for 205., he has a shilling left to spend in something else. This extra shilling (amounting in the case of the tomatoes from the Canaries to .C 15,000 is the measure of the national loss on this item alone.. If therefore we have £15,000 less to spend we have diminished the fund which calls for,and employs labour by this amount; which would mean if we take a man's weekly wage at 255. we should displace 231 men by this single item. If we included the whole of the vast amount of imports, or if we conclude ,that half of our imports can be excluded, we should find the displacement of labour to amount to a very large sum, and to a very considerable number of men. The number of unemployed is not only a burden on the employed, but it is one of the main faotors in depressing the wages of those in work. If I am right 'in this (and if tarn not I will be pleased to hear from the Tariff Reformers wherein my reasoning is wrong) then this attempt to foster industries which are not advantageous on a Free Trade basis means increasing the sad amount of unemployment in our country and I adding considerably to the misery which this unemploy- ; ment entails.</p>
        <p>Let it be clearly understood that as Free Traders we do not suggest that of itself it is a cure-all of the many evils which exist under present conditions, but we do contend that other things being equal the system pro- duces the most wealth and well-being, and that to hamper industry by tariffs will only aggravate conditions we all deplore, and involve the country in a wasteful use of its activities.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER. Penketb, February Bth, 1909.</p>
        <p>For all Chronic Chest Complaints, take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. is. 14, 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
