        <p>St. Barnabas' Orchestral Society. This society, which is making gratifying headway. gave a successful c mcert in St. Baraabas' schools, Bostock-street, on Thursday evening. A. large audi- ence listened with great appreciation to • a most interesting programme of vocal and instrumental music. The orchestra, under the conductorship of Mr. H. J. Iddon, gave highly creditable renderings of the following items: - Overture "Le Diadame" (Herman), march " Tannhauser " (TVagncr) overture "Bohemian Girl " (Balfe), and overture " dtradella (F/otote), and the members are to be congratulated upon the good progress which has been made. Miss G. Povey, who possesses a pleasing soprano voice sang "The Swallows" and "The magic month of May very tastefully, and was heartily encored for the latter song. Miss J. Armstrong (contralto) delighted the audience with her expressive interpretations cf "Kathleen Mavourneen and "Angus Macdonald„' and was twice recalled. Ur. L. Aukland sang "The Bandolero" in his usual spirited style, and Was also heard to advantage in" Asleep in the deep." Mr. E. Walker sang "The Sailor's Grave" with good effect. Mr. James Watts played two violin solos Romance et Bolero " and " IL Trovatore " with taste and skill, and responded to an encore with Raff's " Cavatina." The humorous part of the programme was entrusted. to Mr. G. H. Smith, who was eminently successful ill his efforts to amuse. Mrs. 13. Forman was the acoorn.- , panist.</p>
        <p>OUGHT I TO USE GE ASSES.—In order to answer this question it is necessary to test the sight. This is done and all information- given willingly. JOHNSTON WATSON, Eyesight Testing Rooms, 84, Bridge-street, Warrington. Hours of Business: 9.30 to 7.30. Johnston Watson attends on Monday and Tuesday from 11 to 430, Wednesday 11 to 5.30. Thurdsy 11 to 1, and Friday and Saturday 11 to s.3o.—Advt.</p>
