direct rock but God. To Christians it was Christ or nothing; action on time Blood that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are they would not. accept any other belief. In conclusion daily achieving their wonderful successes. Obtain- be asked them to build upon the rock, which was Christ. able of dealers, or direct from Dr. Williams' Medicine LECTURE ON "GERMANY AND THE GERMANS." Cmnpany,46, Holborn Viaduct, London,post free 2s. 01. On the Monday evening the Rey G. Harbottle gave for one b )x,or 13s. 9d. for six boxes. Substitutes do not . a lecture on " Germane and the Germans." He bad cure ; that is why you must insist at Aiwa on having for hischairman Mr. R..Ceirbett, who was supported by the genuine pills. wive!' bear the full mime, Dr. the Rev. E. Illansbard Keeling and Mr. W. Healey Williams' Pink Pills for Pete People. Say nothing ( secretary of the Bowsey-road Wesley Guild). else will do. The CIIAIRMLN spoke appreciatively of the work Mr. Harbottle did in starting the Guild at Bewsey- road. He announced that at the Council meeting of the Circuit Guild it had been decided to offer a shield to the Guild which had the largest average attendance at the devotional meeting. In the course of his lecture the Rev. GRORGE HARBOTTLIe said that when the King !Aided at Berlin the next day he would be met at the station by a magnificent regiment of soldiers and would also he accompanied by great men into the capital of Prussia. When the speaker made his humble entry there was no ceremonial like that. The Brandenburg Gate was surrnounded by a triumphal "guard,"which in itself was remarkable. It, was worthy of notice for its history. That "guard" of victory was carried away by Napoleon after a battle with Prussia. At that time the Germans slrunk the cup of dread to the very dregs. After the long series of battles the "guard" was carried back and placed in its former position. Under that gate King Edwftrd and the Kaiser, the son and grandson of our late Queen Victoria, would pass and tbe long desired, long expected and long delayed visit to the German capital wonhi take placeit was a momentous event.lt was very certain that at that moment the two greatest men in all Europe, the two most important personages in Europe, were our King and the Kaiser. And this was after the announcement of the iscrease in the German and British fleets, after the publication in the " Tele- graph "of the interview with the Kaiser. It made one tremble to think of the immense power in the hands of one man. A chance word or mistake would upset the unstable equilibrium of nations and would set the soldiers marching to war. One was glad to think that in King Edward we seemed to have a king of con- summate wisdom. We have a king who as on the side of peace, and for his own part, he hoped and confidently believed that Wilhelm of Germany, was not the unpeacefnl man some thought. When the lecturer arrived in Berlin twelve months ago Berlin was suffering from a mild form of invasion by the Baptists of England. He heard from the lips of some , of the Baptist ministers in an inteiestiniz and fascinating way, of the unexpected discovery of Baptists through. out Germany and on the borders of Russia. The representatives of the congregations of those far-off lands came to Berlin to take part in the conference. As be was standing on the balcony of the hotel watching the military pageant he was struck with the deep interest that the Germans took in Englishmen. He met with kindnesaes,—kindnesses from first to last, which were not even bestowed upon their own countrymen. Describing the Cathedral in Berlin, be observed that the pulpit was right in the centre and not pint away in n corner as it was in some of our Anglican Churches. In Germany it was customary to smoke cigars, and if anyone was noticed smoking a pipe he was termed "An Englander." The condition of religion, ganged by attendance on public worship, was far worse in Germany than it Wig in England. If they spoke to a German about his attendance on public worship they were apt to get a reply hors him, that the churches were intended for the women and children. Sunday was over practically by noon and the rest of the day was a great fete day. Potsdam. 18 miles from Berlin, was a place of many palaces and full ot historic interest It was the great city of Frederick the Great. He was the roan who established the supremacy of Prussia among the German states and added wonderfully to its territories in his life. He was a great soldier, a voluminous writer, and a great strong man,—perhaps the strongest that Germany had produced. and, by the way, he was almost half an Englishman. His mother was the daughter of George Ist. tee had the most carsed.ill-natured man for father, that perhaps anybody possessed. Witten. burg was one of the most important places in the entire world. Two men had lived there, Melanchton the great echoler of the Reformation, and Luther. Luther lay buried at the Castle Church at Wittenburg. It was at this place that Luther burnt the" Papal Bull." For the speaker's part, no incense went up to heaven more acceptable than that Papal Ball. it was the bravest deed in history. (Applause.) The lecturer had talked with all sorts and conditions of men in Germany and he had never met with anything but friendly feelings towards Englishmen. The Press had told them that there was a danger of invasion, but if they mentioned it to a German, he laughed at it. If</p>
