      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>TWO MORE BURNING FATALITIES.</head>
        <p>TWO MORE BURNING FATALITIES.</p>
        <p>The Coroner and the Absence of Fire- Guards.</p>
        <p>Coroner Brighouse conducted an inqiiiry at the Blrough Police Station on Monday evening, into the circumstancs attending the death of Mary Alice Ashworth, and five, who was admitted into the War- rington Infirmary on Friday afternoon stifferirtg from extensive burns and died two hours later. Mary Ashworth, mother of the deceased, said she resided at 33, Al!card-street. She was getting the dinner ready on Friday last about ten minutes to one o'clock. She went into the back kitchen to water a plant and while there the deceased came rushing to the kitchen door in flames crying" Mamma! Mamma ! " The flames were at once extinguished and the deceased was conveyed to the Infirmary. The Coroner : Have you a fireguard. Witness : No, sir. The Coroner: Well after the Ist of April people who are found without fireguards will be fined. On the Ist of April the Children's Bill will come into force. Ile Coroner remarked that he had just come from an inquest, where a child was burnt to death in exactly the same circumstances.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict of" Accidental death." The second inquest wes then proceeded with, on the body of Helen Pidgeon, aged 10 years, who was admitted to the Infirmary on Toursdly afternoon, and who died a few hours later from the effects of burns. The mother of the deceased said she had six more children, ages ranging from 3 to 12 years, and resided at 33, Aiken-street. On Thursday last the grate in the kitchen was being repairei, and in consequence she mode a fire in the front room. About a quarter past four o'clock she went with a bucket of rubbish to some waste land,and on her return found the front door open and a large crowd of people had gathered round. She found that deceased had been burned. She was afterwards told by one of the children that deceased was reaching over the fire in order to get a halfpenny that was on the mantel-piece in order to give it to the youngest child to pacify it. In doing so her clothes ignited. Deceased endeavoured to extinguish the flames herself, but being unable rushed into the street.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: You had no fire-guard? Witness : No.</p>
        <p>Why did you not buy one? Witness : Because I have not the means. My husband is only a labourer, and I have six children to look after.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: I am getting callous hnd indifferent with regard to these fate lities.but at the same time I feel very sorry for any child burning. After the Ist of April he would consider that the police in the South-West Lanca- shire district (Coroner's district) were not doing their duty if they did not take proceedings against any person found neglecting their children by not providing a fire- guard. A verdict of " Accidental death " was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Young People's Society. Mr. A. Millar presided at the weekly meeting held on Monday evening in the Memorial Hall. Miss M. Swindells read a paper entitled " English novels past and present." An interesting discussion followed, in which many members took part. Progressive Labour League.</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall will on Saturday evening next be the scene of a tea, meeting and concert in connection with the Progressive Lablur [Algae. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, UT. and Mrs. Crosfield will be present and a highly successful gathering is anticipated.</p>
        <p>Warrington Women's Suffrage Society. The members of this society held an " At Home" last evening week when there was a good attendance at the " Aca,detny."--Ifrs. H. M. Ashton, wit) had been appointed delegate to the Annual Council Nfeet- ings of the N.U.W.S. Societies held in Brighton, January 28th and 27th, gave a very interesting report of the meetings.</p>
        <p>LINSEED CO NIPOUND' for Coughs &amp;Colds. Relieves Asthma and difficult breathing. 90., is. Women's Liberal Association. We would remind our readers that a soiree, under the auspices of the above association will be held at the Lion Hotel, on Friday evening next. There will be a reception at seven, whist at 7.30, supper at 10.30 and dancing from 11 to 1.30. Nfr. R. H. Pearson's band will play for dancing and the function promises to _be_most successful and enjoyable.</p>
        <p>Choir Social. _ A pleasant gathering took place in the schools, Kendrick street, on Tuesday evening when the adult members of St. Paul's Choir assembled for a potato- pie supper and social. An excellent repAst provided by the members, was served, and was followed by games, dances and competitions, a very enjoyable function being brought to a close about midnight. Evangelistic Mission. _ -</p>
        <p>An evangelistic service was held in Bold-street Wesleyan Church immediately following the ordinary service on Sunday evening. Th. 3 Rev. T. Gladding presided and an address on "The power of GA to save to the uttermost," aR instanced by his own cm- version, was given by Mr. James Hamblett. Mr. E. J. Guest also took part in the service.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENI FIT.--UNDBR TRH CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Concert at the Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>As will be seen from an advertisement in another column the Perfection Soap Works Band will give a sacred concert at the Palace and Hippodrome on Sunday evening next. A most attractive programme has been arranged and the high reputation of the band should be sufficient to ensure a large attendance. In addition to the instrumental selections, there will be items by Madam Lena Mitchell, contralto,who ill sing " The Enchantress (J. L. Hatton.) Seats may be boAed at the Hippodrome. ANNUAL SALE of Tea Services, Dinner Services, etc., great bargains.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge street, Warrington.—Advt. Penny Popular Concerts.</p>
        <p>Week by week there is a crowded attendance at these concerts held in Bold-street school, and Satur- day was an exception to the rule. The Rev. T. Miller presided and a varied and excellent programme was submitted. Songs were pleasingly given by Misses Clara Platt and Hilda Yates, and Messrs. E. Worrall and Leonard Platt, whilst a quartette by the above was much appreciated. The humorous part of the programme 11%14 entrusted to Messrs. W. Nicholson and Bert Cross, who greatly amused the audience. Pictures at the Parr Hall. The people of Warringtan have again been afforded an excellent opportunity of witnessing a grand set of animated pictures by the London Animated Picture Company. On the opening night the hall was packed with patrons, which spoke well for the remainder of the week. The pictures shewn were both humorous and sentimental, and of a quality which should satisfy the most fastidious. The most interesting pictures were of the great earthquake scenes in Italy, the disaster of which cost 190,030 lives. Another fine picture was the groat wrestling bout between Hackensmidt and Rogers. Field Club.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of this Club on Friday Mr • W. Webster lectured on " Ants." The subject was treated on non-technical lines, The life stages, of egg, larva, pupa and imago or perfect adult form, and the general structure of ants were concisely described, whilst the habits of most of the 34 British species were pleasantly explained, the explanation being the outcome of the long and close personal study of these interesting little insects by the lecturer. In the dis- cussion which ensued Nit.. Wabeter was warmly con- gratulated on his able and instructive discourse. COAGULINE," ICLINX," TENASITINt.' Cements for mending all things.—Advt. St. Paul's Church Tea Party. _ _</p>
        <p>- A highly successful gathering was the annnal tea and entertainment in connection with St. Paul's Church which took place in the schools on Monday evening. Abmit 250 sat down to tea and at the subsequent proceedings the Vicar (Rev. W. Bra•cecarup) presided. Encouraging speeches on the work of the parish were made, it being noted that the debt on the church restoration fund had been reduced from £lOO to £lOO in two years, and that the endowment of the living now stood at £l4O. An excellent entertainment was</p>
        <p>submitted,including songs by Miss E. Holmes, Miss Lacey, Miss Gladys Tuft, Rev. J. IL Bankes, selections by the St. Paul's Quartette Party, recitations by Miss Pale, and humorous items by Mr. Cross. Mr. E. Wilkes was the accompanist. 'Latchford Band of Hop.</p>
        <p>The Latchford Blnd of Hope held their monthly Public meeting on Thursday, and owing to the kindly help of Stockton Heath United Band of Elope it proved to be a very enjoyable meeting. They brought with them a good programme. Recitations were given by E. Phillips, 11. Stringer, E. Hamblett, M. Kirkland ; a dialogue by C. Jones and E. Clark, action song by six boys ; songs by E. Mounfield, Miss Hamblett, M. Moun field and E Whitdeld; a pianoforte solo by Evart Grounds. Friends from the Latchford Society taking part were Miss M. Jones, who gave a pretty song of Scotland, and recitations were given by Percy Morton and John Habberlv, a song by three girls,and a short dialogue by four }joys and one girl. Wycliffe P.S A. Society. There was a most enjoyable meeting of this Society on Sunday last. Mr. D. Owen presided, and spoke upon " Meeting God, face to face." Jacob received counsel and strength to meet Esau, his brother, after _ _</p>
        <p>years of estrangement and unreconciled spirits. God greatly helped him. Mr. A. Jolley, Warrington, gave the address upon " Play the game." He instanced the recreation world and the spirit of fairness and good sportsmanship. The lessons drawn were: play the game, to parents, remembering their sacrifices, their thoughts and counsels for our good,play the game to our country, give our fellows our best services, remembering the bright example given by men in the past who have played the game for us, had been the means of brightening life. We liked to see a good game politically, socially and spiritually. Therefore we must play the game to God, who in all flis riches, all His mercies has played the game for us, given us of Himself, made us noble creatures and has a perfect right to our best game. Mr. C. Cotton also took part in the meeting. Mr. Payton, Mus. Bac. presided at the organ. LINSEED COMPOUND ' for Coughs and Colds. Of</p>
        <p>proven efficacy. Of Chemists only. 91Ci.,15.1ic1.,25.9d.-Adv St..__Barnabas' Orchestral_ Society._ _</p>
        <p>This society, which is making gratifying headway, gave a successful concert in St. Barnabas' schools, Bostock-street, on Thursday evening. A. large audi- ence listened with great appreciation to a most interesting programme of vocal and instrumental music The orchestra, under the conductorship of Mr. H. .J. Iddon, gave highly creditable renderings of the following items : Overture "Le Diadetne " (Herman), march " Tannhauser " (Wagner), overture "Bohemian Girl (Balla), and ovetture " Stradella " (F/otow), and the members are to be congratulated upon the good progress which has been made. Miss G. Povey, who possesses a pleasing soprano voice sang "The Swallows" and "The magic month of May very tastefully, and was heartily encored for the latter song. Miss J. Armstrong (contralto) delighted the audience with her expressive interpretations cf "Kathleen Mavourneen ' and "Angus Macdonald," and was twice recalled. Mr. L. Aukland sang " The _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Bandolero" in his usual spirited style, and Nias also heard to advantage in" Asleep in the deep!' Mr. E. Walker sang " The Sailor's Grave "- with good effect. Mr. James Watts played two violin solos" Romance et Bolero " and "II Trovatore " with taste and skill, and responded to an encore with Raff's " Cavatina." The humorous part of the programme was entrusted to Mr. G. H. Smith, who vvas eminently successful in his efforts to amuse. Mrs. 13. Forman was the accom- panist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>"THE LADY OF OSTEND."</head>
        <p>"THE LADY OF OSTEND."</p>
        <p>Dramatic Society's Annual Performances. _</p>
        <p>We do not know whether the general public is obsessed by the fixed belief that the dramatic work of amateurs is necessarily bound to be amateurish, but for this or some other unexplained reason the Warrington Dramatic Society does not secure the patronage which b3th its work and its objects fully mirit. As a matter of fact there are few productions at the local theatre from cue year's end to another which surpass in interest and excellence the annual performances of the Dramatic Society. And in addi- tion it has to b 3 remembered that the Soeiety devotes its net proceeds to deserving chirities. Yet the public represented here by the usual theatre habitues does not respond at all adequately to the efforts put forth for its entertainment by the enthusi- astic and talented members of the Society. : Even the choice of the material cannot be , blamed, for the Society alternates from grave to gay • with frequency and never fails to produce something , of merit. Yet, this week for instance, the audiences at the Court Theatre did not assume even respectable proportions until the third night's productien of " The Lady of Ostend," a farcical comedy by the late editor of " Punch," Sir Francis Burnand, which could be guaranteed to make an Egyptian Sphinx indulge in farcial contortions indicative of mirth. within. Fortunately the Society's artistes refuse to be discouraged, and cheerfully make the best of whatever support is accorded them. They have succeeded, in the course of years, in raising over £2,000 for local charities, bat the sum ought to have been twice as big if the Warringtou public had not been so unresponsive and indifferent to the good things provided. "The Lady. of Ostend" should have easily filled all parts of the house every evening, but the pittites and the " gods " seem to have fought shy of the lady, when by making her acquaintauee they could have assured themselves of a very hilarious evening. Unfortunately in this case, their loss is no one's gain. The only conclusion one can come to is that the public resembles the law in being "a bass." It is not incumbent upon us to devote much time to retaining the plot of Sir Francis Burnaad's comedy, because there is little than can be defined. The lady of Ostend is a real personage whose charms cause one or two respectable gentlemen to forget the proprieties when on a visit to the gay continental town. Whoa you go to Ostend you must do as Ostend does, but it is more than a I:tt!e unfortunate if your escapades happen to take place before the recording eye of a cinematograph. This is what happened to Dick Whortles (who had, of course, gone to the continent on business) and his friend Baron de Longueville. Their harmless rendezvous with "The Lady of Ostend" looked very different in the merciless glare of a London music-hall, and especially when he the unevninathetic eyes of a wife and a mother-in-law. Dick's little affaCre (tumour cost him considerable tribulation and a pot of money, but then as he says, he always was a Jonah for ill-luck. We , have here the substratum of the farce, the action of which all takes place at Mr. Whortles' usually quiet suburban home in Bayswater. The ill-fated Dick attempts to appease the wifely anger as the result of a late night at the Cl üb,by an escort to the cinematograph, when, lo ! the deluge ! Complications ensue with alarming rapidity, and get particularly lively when Toby Krockitt, the pugilistic husband of " The Lady of Ostend "also casts an eye over the fateful pictures and forthwith seeks out our friend Dick and the Baron to wreak a fearful revenge. Before any tragedy occurs the lady herself appears on the scene, and explains that she had been employed by the Cinemat o- graph Company to decoy silly-looking and susceptible males into amusing situations. This explanation appeases all but the victims, who find even their pleasant recollections of Ostend shattered, and not to be counted as worth the ccst. The cast of the comedy is as follows : Pick Whort4es, General Manufacturing Agent, London and Continental .. .. Mr. HG. Mosley Joseph Carbury, his Father-in-law .. Mr. Sydney Wallhead Baron de Longueville, his Friend .. .. Mr. H. M. Ashton Toby Krockitt, a Prize Fighter .. Mr. W. H. Brown John Humbert,Manager of the Cinematograph Mr. R. P. Jolihson Thompson, Clerk in Whortle's Office .. .. Mr. J. Craik Dorothy NVhortles, Dick's Wife .. Miss May Morrison Matilda Carbury. his Mother-in-lam Mrs. Jennings Millie Grace .. Miss Dotothy Browne Jane .. Miss Bessie Forshaw The Lady of Ostend .. Miss Helen G. Wright</p>
        <p>The farce is excellently done, and moves with the life, rapidity and gaiety almost of the far-famed " Charley's Aunt." There is, of course, not much scope for scrim's acting, but the light comedy shows some of the Society's best known members at their best. Mr. 11. G. Mosley is capital as Dick -Whortles and gets through a tremendous amount of work in accomplished style, though we have seen him in roles which give his special talents better scope. Mr. Sidney Wallhead is always at home in the part of a middle- aged or elderly man, and has certainly a special gift for looking paternal. lie c3uld hardly be improved upon as Dick's festive father-in-law and contributes much to the jollity of the piece. The role of the Baron makes rather more serious demands on the</p>
        <p>artiste's capacity for characterisation, and the Society is fortunate in having within its ranks a gentleman like Ur. Ashton, to whom the assumption of the air and speech of an excitable and emotional foreigner comes quite easily. A. more Frenchy Baron could not be desired, and Mr. Ashton's appearance was always the signal for a roar of laughter. High praise is the fitting reward, too, for Miss hay Morrison on her debut in the ranks of the Warring- ton Society. She had an uncommonly awkward role to sustain as the injured wife. While in the midst of comedy she has to preserve a dignified severity of expression and manner not easy to assume, and the great charm of her acting was its ease and naturalness. Mrs. Jennings was delightfully amusing as the mother-in-law, and another successful debut was that of Miss Dorothy Browne, who looked the typical charming English girl as Millie Grace, the object of the Baron's serious attentions. Miss Helen G. Wright and Ur. W. Et. Brown also proved them- selves to be acquisitions to the Society as " The Lady of Ostend" and Toby Krockitt respectively. Miss Wright has a distinguished stage presence which accorded well with her role. There was much merit in</p>
        <p>Mr. Brown's study of the prize-fighter husband of the dashing Ostend belle. Miss Bessie Forshaw mide a very bright and saucy maid, Mr. R P. Johnson looked distinctly professional as the cinematograph manager, and Mr. J. Craik was a typical clerk in \Mortles' office. One of the best features of the production is the uniform strength and proficiency of the cast. The Society's company in " The Lady of Ostend " is quite good enough to tour with success, if such were demanded of them. - _ -</p>
        <p>The comedy was very han,lsomely staged, and the results proved that Nfr.C.Lll.Wood's unusually obscure part (for him) was, as usual, well done. As a "curtain-raiser " the comedy in one act " A Marriage has been arranged" was performed by Miss Dorothy Browne and Mr. H. G. Mosley. As a dialogue it was interesting, but there was a stiffness about the comedy which we hardly think was written into it by Sutro.</p>
        <p>An acknowledgment i due to Mr. Arthur Browne (treasurer) and Mr. J. W, Bullen (hon. secretary) for their indefatigable labours, continued from year to year, in the arrangement of these most enjoyable dramatic performances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>LEAGUE OF HELP FORAM</head>
        <p>LEAGUE OF HELP FORAM</p>
        <p>A Promising Organisation.</p>
        <p>A subject which has caused much discussion in Warrington during the last few months took practical shape on Tuesday evening last in the form of a meeting held at the Town Hall, by kind permission of the vrnves when it was decided to form a Civic League v.f Help for Warrington. The Rev. W. Bracecanop presided and the good attendance included the Revs. H. A. Lester, T. W. Barber B. E. Peters, J. Morgan Price, C. Harvey-Cook, and it. E. Letheren, Councillor L•zshford, Captain Createld, Captain Waring, and Messrs. C. E. Parker, T. W. Monks, J. Moore Murray (Director of Education), W. Farnworth (Bolton general secretary of "The eounty borough of Bilton Guild of Help"), W. Mason, J. H. Smethurst, Alfred Perris, E. A. Sanders' A. Bottomley (clerk to the Board of Guardians), E. Osborne, J. A. Ransome, J. Fargher, (Labour Bureau), 4. S. Hill, Inspector Co; (N.S.P.C.C.), and A. W. Perris (hon. secretary). The CHAIRMAN expressed regret at the Mayor (Alder- man Forshaw) not being able to take the chair. Letters had been received from the Rev. E. Vale Owen, Alder. Smethurst, the Rev. F. Vaughan, Miss Dutton, and the secretary of the Manchester League of Halp,expressing regret at their non attendance. Continuing, he said they needed a league in Warrington. They wanted to establish a league similar to those in other towns. There were already 53 leagues and they were helping on the great cause they had at heart. He knew something of work among the poor in Warrington, and in other towns, and he was convinced that if they could all work together in one common cause they would be able to do some good. It was a grand thing to have something, where they could sink their little differences, and all get into a new atmosphere, where they could realise that they were brothers and sisters. It would help those who worked in it, and it would do a great deal of good. (Applause.) Mr. W. FARNWORTH said he took it that there were a number of people in Warrington who were willing to help a poorer neighbour. It was better that they should be banded together rather than work separately. The idea of League of Help was to make people think somebody cared for them and was willing to help them There were a number of charity organisations in War- rington, but the Loagues of Help will not interfere with them. Triey did not want to start a League of Help In Warringtoa with the idea that they were going to play the good samaritan entirely. In Bolton for their £350 a year, which was subscribed by their friends, they found work for 450 helpers— those people who were willing to come and help where there was distress. During last year they dealt with 701 new cases, which affected close upon 3,000 people. The total number of cases dealt with during the year was 1,142. 171 persons were found employment; nine persons received legal aid; 10G were sent to the Con- valescent Homes, and five children gained admission to Orphanages. The provision of a friend for everyone who was in need of Lelp would overcome a good deal of differences, particularly if the friend was willing to give personal service. When they started work in Balton they had 89 helpers, and at the present time they had 450. (Applause). Questions were asked of Mr. FARNWORTH, and in answer to the Chairman he said that it was necessary if they wished to do the work as it ought to be done in hay e a central office with a properly equipped staff. The town should be divided into districts and sub- districts, and the helpers should be organised. It was neoessary for someone to be available to assist the helpers and act as ago between. The administration in Bolton cost about £350 annually. He had three assistants, and it was probable another would have to be appointed owing_ to pressure of work. Answering Mr. NI.,ASON, Mr. FARNTWaRTEI said that he should say, there were about 25 towns in which there were paid secretaries, who devoted the whole of their time to the work. He himself generally.worked from 9 in the morning till about 10 at night. In reply to a question by the Rev. HARVEY Coo, he said that in Sunderland, which was about the same size as Warrington, it took £3OO to commence.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. A. LEsTEa spoke as one who did not agree with the scheme at first, but now he was con- verted to it. It would be good for the souls of those who were going to help and be a great blessing to those who were helped. After considerable discussion it was decided unani- mously that the league should be called " The Warring- ton Citizens' League of Help." The CHAIRMAN announced that two heads of cele- brated firms in Warrington were so keen about the movement that they had kindly promised him £lOO each. (Applause.) If they all gave that, they would get enough to keep them secure for the first twelve months. A committee was then formed which included all who were willing to join.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>HOW TO STOP RHEUMATIC PAINS QUICKLY.</head>
        <p>HOW TO STOP RHEUMATIC PAINS QUICKLY.</p>
        <p>The greatest desire of everyone who suffers from Rheumatism is to get rid of the pain quickly, as soon as it appears. Little use it is to know that something is wrong constitutionally, or that prolonged dietary treatment is necessary, when agonising pangs are shooting through one's limbs. Something must be done instanter</p>
        <p>-71;ali-o—g-ientest blessing ever producod for sufferers from muscular rheumatism is Sloan's Liniment. It penetrates through the skin, through the muscles, right to the bone, and instantly kills the pain. Sloan's Liniment requires no rubbing in—it is the most penetrating of liniments, and every sufferer who knows what it is to have the affected part even lightly touched will appreciate what comfort this means. Head what Mr. J. Adams, Monsieur's Hall Cottage Broomgrove, says :—" I am pleased to inform you that oue bottle of Sloan's Liniment has done my wife and me more good than any other preparation we have ever tried. We have suffered with rheumatism and goat for forty years and are very thankful to say we are now quite cured.' If you are subject to rheumatism, sciatica, lumbago, or any muscular affection, get Dr. Sloan's Liniment and use it at once- Dr. Sloan's emergency book is a valuable compendium that tells you how to treat these and other ailments with assured success. A copy will be sent you free if you write to the Distributing Depot, 32, Snow Hill, London, B.C. Dr. Sloan's Liniment may be had of all Chemists is. lid. and 2s. 3d. a bottle.</p>
        <p>Oddfellowship The "Prince of Wales" Lodge, of the Independent Order of Oddfellows, Manchester Unity, held a smok- ing concert and dance at the Conservative Club on Thursday evening, to celebrate the occasion of obtain- ing a membership of 500. In opening the proceedings I the Chairman P.P.G.M. T. P. Bradshaw, touched upon ! the good work done by friendly societies in the I Warrington District and all over the country. He !emphasised the fact that over 40 had been paid out in sick pay during the past year, and that after all payments the total worth of the Lodge stood at over 5:3,600. He hoped before long they would meet again to celebrate the acbivement of 600 members. (Applause.)The Chairman was supported by the follow- ing, P.G.M. G. F. Eireeman, P.G.M. Forbs, I.P.P.G.M • Lot. Brooks, P.P.GM. Drink water (C.S.), P.G.T. and Wadsworth (secretary), and L.G.M. E. Clayton (secretary entertaining committee.) During the con- cert various songs were given, including the follow- ing :—Pianoforie selections, Mr. F. Webster ; • song, "A Summer night," Miss Chalmers ; song, " The sailor's grave," Mr. J. Willett ; quartette, "Little Tommy," Warrington Orpheus A HUMBER DISPLAY AT MANCHESTER. Quartette Party ; song, "The Trumpeter,' Mr. G. Gough ; humorous song, Mr. J. Emson ; During the run of the Belle Vue Motor Show at , Manchester, from February 19th to 27th, inclusive, Song, Kathleen Mavourneen, Miss Boyle ; duet, W. I Messrs. Humber, Ltd., have arranged for a striking dis- " Watchman of the Nigh," IlessreJ w. Willett and • I play of 1909 models at their local depot, 33 Blackfriars- Clay ; song, "If I should plant a tiny seed of love„' street. Besides the 8 h.p. chassis and 16 h.p. chassis, Mr. A. Boyle. Numerous encores were given. After I which drew forth such favourable comment at the the " Smoker," dancing was indulged in, and -a most ' Edinburgh Exhibition, a representative range of other successful function was brought to a close about Humber models will be on view,an inspection of which 2 a.m. The M.C.'s were P. G. Fred Webster, L.G.M. will well repay the potential buyer,for Humber quality : combined with reasonable prices and striking novelties E. Clayton, Bro. W. Pemberton acted as pianist. The catering was in the hand of Atkinson's Café Compan in construction characterise all the productions of this famous firm. , Limited. •</p>
        <p>Have you remembered what I asked you to send me a fortnight ago? Replies are cotning in, but I want a good many more: My members were, asked to send me their ideas of "How to Make Others Happy." I think all of you could very easily tell me the way to make yourselves happy, so I want you just to write it down and post it to me. I will send to the sender of the best letter a P.O. for 2s. 6d.— that will repay you for any little trouble to which you may be put. I daresay many of you take up the paper and think that it is easy enough to write me a few lines, and fully intend to do so, but then something comes in the way and you forget all about it. Then perhaps you think of it when the next week's paper comes, and finally you come to the conclusion that it is too late. Well, perhaps this is natural on your part, as you are busy with lessons and play, but I don't want you to forget me. Why, some of you I have known for years past, although I have never seen you, nor have you seen me, but so many of you are like old friends that I do very much like to hear from you, either by let ter or contribution.</p>
        <p>I hope my young friends will bear this in mip4 and not quite forget poor old UNCLE IN ED.</p>
        <p>MEG, THE MATCH-GIRL.</p>
        <p>Meg Brown was an orphan, she was about ten years old, and she was a homeless waif. Meg's mother had died three weeks ago, and since then Meg had slept, not in bed, but in an old stable amongst the straw. Meg's mother had taken washing in, but when her health began to fail, and she was no longer able to stand at the wash-tub, Meg had to sell matches in order to get money to buy food with. But Meg's mother had died, and after the funeral, the landlord, to whom they owed money, turned Meg out into the street, where she was at this present minute. She stood at the corner of a street, leaning against a gas-lamp, and gazing into a shop window where chocolates and confectionery were displayed. How she longed for one of those delicious looking buns, but she turned away with a sigh, for she had no money to buy one with. Sho was €1 fOrlorn 'oohing. object: with her dress torn in several places, and her bare fetA 'all covered with mud, but she had a pretty face, with big blue eyes, and although her hair had not been brushed and combed it hung in ringlets around her face. As she stood trying to decide which would be the likeliest way to get rid of her matches (at two boxes a penny), a lady wheeling a lovely mailcart, in which sat a pretty child, and a little 'girl running by the side, passed her. Meg looked at the little girl, and wished she too had nice clothes and a little brother to play with. They stopped outside the shop window into which Meg had looked with longing eyes, and when the baby saw the chocolates, he cried:. " Chocky, mummy, chocky, me want some chocky, please, mummy." " Alt right, baby, mother will fetch some. Madge," she said, turning to the little girl, "take care of Eric, until I come back," and she went into the shop. _ _</p>
        <p>"Meg saw the little girl stoop to pet a dog, which until then she had not noticed. The baby was playing with a ball and he kept Lhrowing it a little way, and the dog ran after it, and carried it back in its mouth. Then he stood up, and instead of throwing it along the causeway, as he had done before, he threw it into the middle of the road. Off like a shot darted the dog, just as a hansom turned the corner. The little girl became alarmed, for she thought that it would be run over. "Jack I Jack I come back! " she cried. But Jack did not, so nothing daunted she rushed into the road and grasped the dog's collar, but just then she slipped and fell. The hansom was almost upon her. Her mother coming out of the shop saw the danger she was In, and started wringing her hands in despair; "Oh! save my child, ohi someone, do save my child," she sobbed: Meg could not bear to see the lady crying, so to the rescue she ran, and taking hold of the little girl dragged her to her feet. Meg pushed her on to the causeway, and then all her strength seemed to leave her, her head swam, and she sank upon her knees. She conld feel the horse's hot breath but could not move, then some- thing struck her head and she knew nothing more. . . . When she recovered concious- ness she was • lying in a snow-white bed, all icr dirty, ragged clothes were gome, and in place a pretty nightdress. She gazed round the room, there were lots of pretty Ilictures on the walls, and flowers everywhere, but it was quite strange to Meg, and she wondered if it was all a dream. The door pdened and a lady came in. Meg recognised ‘;er at once, it was the mother of the little girl and baby. She sat up in bed, then put tier hand to her head, for she felt a sharp pain, and to her surprise found a bandage around it. The lady hurried to the bed-side. "Lie down, dear," she said. " It is all right," she answered, as she saw Meg's look of enquiry. " You saved Madge's life, and now you are in my house." (To be continued.) _</p>
        <p>Mamma (at seven o'clock in the evening): "Come, Ethel, it is bedtime. All the little birds have gone to sleep in their little nests." • Ethel (next morning at five o'clock): "Conic, mamma, it is time to get up. All the little birds are up, and the mamma birds, too."</p>
        <p>When is it best to lose your temper ?—When it is a bad one.</p>
        <p>• " How is Gullem getting along now?" " Splendidly, for him." "Flow do you know '1" "He has just succeeded in borrowing a sovereign from me."</p>
