        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. By " Ovalist."</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>-- The Warrington-Hull match has long had a reputa- tion for Providing one of the brighest games of the season, and expectations were more than realised on tardaY• There was a great display provided for be Wilderspool uatrons, who had the pleasme of see- 'g Warrington run up their highest score of the season by means of the most brilliant football under Perfect conditinne, Hull have the reputation of Picssessing tho strongest side in the history cf the Tot% and there is no doubt the team is a good one. 'l.tf home they have so far proved invincible, but on 9reign soil the Third Porters have generally per- formed like tame rabbits and only on one occasion have they returned home victorious. Why there should be this disparity between the perform- a.nosi of the team at home and away is a mystery, and tt is doubtless one the Hull directors would give a lot tro solve. Hull beat Warrington at the Boulevaid by tvao_ints.to nil, and were the better team on the day's Bid" On Saturday they brought practically the same , and lust by 41 points to 10. Such a reversal of t°_fro in the course of a month is hardly credible, but trr it is he Hull men got the bigge:4 drubbing har leY have had for years, aud the score might easily , e been larger.</p>
        <p>-- Of course, Warrington gave a far different display frO m the one at Dull, and would unquestionably hart beaten any team by a good score on the day's play. ,Eren they are not free from tho taunt of occasionally o 1 aving their hearts in their dressing-room when sway fr°re home, but at W ilderspool they are veritable giant. Oldham felt the might of their hands a tb! h,en Y Met ith their first reverse—a little matter of -77 Nults to nil and on Saturday Hull were even more ceverely handled. Warrington had their full cup team oat for a preliminary canter, and the crack thirteen certainly one of the -best in the history of the Club :iinPlY toyed with the visitors. For a quarter of an 'Jour they held their own, and then the scoring corn- -1:113„etecl• By half-time Warrington had compiled 10 tc' nil, and in the second half they added another Nhile Hull got ten. Thus in the second 40 L'lliftee, 33 p)ints were scored -surely something like a record in first league football. Tbe,- -- to main team's big victory was due, in ill, n naaguificent combination coupled with strong, lioPeedy running. Better handling has never been seen junstildie ground, and the play generally of the four !nen never once fell below International 118.--ctArd. Jenkins was as good as Brooks,and Taylor 7` was _4lled Dickenson, who is now enjoying his captaincy. 0 rather a curious thing that Brooks, who made ,814,rnY openings, was the cnly member of the back \excepting Tilley) who did not score. He et'aanriny°dt.anynsw he charged with the selfishness of his ,and the writer who said he could not swerve °tight vto have seen him on Saturday. The wings l_.ere also up to to concert pitch, and the forwards, .treei3thened by the presence of the burly Shugars, were In au irresistible mood. -- tea qn? could have said that Hull were a poor /urn. '`'LleY could to have beaten many a side,but thwere big enough, clever enough, and n enough 9t core with the brilliant home attack.lt appeared to 44e that the team has elaborated its methods of attack so there much that defence is neglected. Certainly li„t was no attempt at spotting, and the home backs IlrallY had a day out. Hull might take a lesson cironte Halifax or Runcorn on " how to keep the scare twil°_-", T.hey were fully entitled to their ten points ;e el"lng star/es of the game, though there was ,snericion of slackness about the home defence Dectart ran through with his two tries. b: big Scotchinan only just redeemed himself from eng vvritteu down a failure. Anderson played a leakY gime dazzlingly clever one moment, and Ie criminally careless the next. Ile was always an tibtl, 0411" n quantity, but on his day there is no finer 7c' '1 playing. The Brothers Roger s are an acquisition ` the Hull team, and it was a pity that so fine a wing in t. Rogers had to take the rear position and get hurt the bargain. The forwards seemed anxious to v7IE) in the open, and neglected honest scrummaging the reEula that they seldom saw the ball at close giluvaor!ers. If i the Hull men did not learn a thing or t was not for want of opportunity. So far, --- Warrington have not reached the " sixties in !he Leagt;,, table ,na Visits to Oldham '.` with this month closing with poned a little and Halifax, progress may be post- are ,already 1 ost, but "ger- Not that I think these two games _,tiLke and aro e have more a the home teams ha t .18n te entitled to the odds. I believe War- __ 8_ n can heat Oldham again, and thus smash w"nootnber of the Spinners' proud no rrds. No team has Watersheddings for two years lam not sure _outettiber Warrington ever have—so that they could c 'pose a better time for beginning. The League e u tab' pto date is as follows: .</p>
