      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Warrington Ovarwalm Hull.</head>
        <p>Warrington Ovarwalm Hull.</p>
        <p>The Warrington men gave a dazzling display on Satur- day in their return engagement with Hull, and before 5,000 of their own supporters ran up the record score of I the season, viz., 41 points, against 10 points gained by I Hull. Spring-like weather prevailed, and the fast I going seemed to suit both teams admirably, some very fine play being witnessed. The home side had abso- lutely their best team out, the forwards being strengthened by the return of Shugars. Hull suffered a serious loss in the absence of their full back, 11. Taylor, for whom E. Rogers appeared, holder going on the wing. Teams : Warrington : Tilley, back; Fish, Dickenson, Taylor, and Treharne, three-quarter backs ; Brooks and Jenkins, half backs; Dowell, Neill, Thomas, A. Board- man, Belton, and Shugars, forwards. Hull—E. Rogers, back ; Holder, G. Rogers, Cotterill, and Dechan, three-quarter backs ; Anderson and Jones, half backs ; Herridge, Boyien, Walton, Major, Fulton, and Stevenson, forwards. Referee, Mr. F. Renton, Hunslet ; touch-judges, Messrs. J. Oakes (St. Helens), and W. Crandiey (Runcorn). Play opened at once in lively fashion, and Anderson was the first to get his side going. Smart handling troubled the Warrington defence at both sides, but neither Holder nor Dechan were allowed to travel far. A long punt by Tilley changed the venue to centre, where the home halves broke away and served Dickenson. The lengthy centre gave a sample of his international form of drawing Hull on to Fish and then doubling in near the posts, Fish missing the goal. Warrington kept up the pressure and after Taylor had served Treharne badly on the right, Jenkins dodged over cleverly and Thomas added a goal with a long shot. Hull had a very warm time, and in quick succession Fish scored a try, and kicked a penalty goal. The visitors persevered and Anderson took play to near the home line, but the defence was very sound. ‘Varrington quickly ran the ball back to the Hull quarter, and another exhibition of quick,clean passing sent TrAarne over on the right. Half-time : Warrington 2 goals, 4 tries, (16 points) ; hull, nil. Though Hull had the advantage of the breeze on turn- ing round they were only aggressive in spasmodic fashion. Cottrell missed a fine chance after going nearly through the home defence, while Jones was applauded for a long run and put into touch near the line. A brilliant response Wai made by Fish and Dickenson who ran to the Hull quarter before being checked. Dicken- son then passed inside to Shugars who burst over the line with a try which drew cheers all round the ground. From this point onwards Warrington completely over- ran the Hull men. Taylor got in behind the posts, Thomas improving, and then after a brief visit to this end, Brooks and Jenkins ran and passed and repassed nearly the length of the ground, the Welshman finally touching down under the bar, Thomas again adding a goal. About this time Hull had the misfortune to lose their full back, E. Rogers with a sprained ankle. From some loose play Fish scored, also goaling. Hull responded splendidly and Dechan put on the finishing touches to a good round of passing, Anderson goaling. Treharne responded with another try for ‘Varrington, but Hull were by no means daunted and Decban again crossed for Anderson to improve. In the closing stages Thomas dashed over from a scrum near the line. Result :—Warrington 7 goals 10 tries (44 points); Hull, 2 goals 2 tries (10 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Sr. HELENS MAKING HEADWAY.</head>
        <p>Sr. HELENS MAKING HEADWAY.</p>
        <p>Swinton were the visitors to St. Helens on S.aturday and there was a capital gate of 4,000. Simister started the game for the visitors. Off-side against St. Helens gave Swinton a chance, Barton kicking dead from Young. Following this Hill opened the game out, and Harold Greenwood cross-kicked to Flanaz,an, who scored. J. Greenwood failed at goal. Resuming. Wallwork kicked to Vernon Hampson, who dribbled to the line, and eluding Barton, scored. R. Jones con- verted. St. Helens retaliated, and from passing Barton scored, but Hill failed to improve. Hill and Trenwith set J. Greenwood going, mid with a clear run scored under the posts. Barton succeeded in placing a goal, and on the resumption Hill dropped a goal from half- way. Half-time score :—St. Helens 2 goals, 3 tries (13 points), Swinton 1 goal, 1 try (5 points). St. Helens had to defend for some time, and ultimately Townson scored, Jones failing to land a goal. Barton, Trenwith nut! J. Greenwood passed, and the latter scored, Barton failing to convert. Near the close Simister scored near the posts. Jones again converted. Result :—St. Helens 2 goals 4 tries (16 points), Swinton 2 goals 2 tries (10 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>OLDHAM v. WIDNES.</head>
        <p>OLDHAM v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Widnes wore at full strength, and in the first half they played a strong game. After thirteen .minutes' even play a round of passing was initiated by Avery in mid-field, and a strong run by Llewellyn gave Oldershaw an opening to score. Jardine failed at goal, but before the interval he kicked a penalty goal. In the third quarter Widnes had the better of the play, but failed to pierce the home defence, and in the last quarter Oldham boat them hollow, the home end not being visited except from re-starting after a score. Ferguson scored at the head of a forward rush, and Oldeshawr added another try after good passing work, and Jardine kicked the goal. Score :—Oldham, 2 goals 3 tries (13 points); Widnes, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>RUNCORN AVENGED ON BATLEY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AVENGED ON BATLEY.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, before 2,000 spectators. Oakland, Bevan, and Senior got away, but Runcorn were soon on the attack again, and from a forward rush Walker scrambled over, Padbury failing at goal. Batley soon had Runcorn defending through a capital effort by Davies and Thomas. Davies then got possession, and dropped a goal. Half-time score :—Runcorn, 1 try (3 points) ; Batley, 1 goal (2 points). After the interval Runcorn were quickly on the attack' and forced the game, Padbury and Butterworth each having hard luck on the line. Kennedy at length scored for Jolley to adds goal. Immediately on resumption Jolley secured and dropped a goal. Davies, Senior and Thomas transferred play to the Runcorn end, where Ward made a fine effort to score. Result—Runcorn, 2 goal 2 tries (10 points) ; Batley, 1 goal (2 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>SATUnDIY'S RESULTS.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. Ince Warrington 3 2 12 Wigan Hightield 1 0 2 Widnes 2 2 10 Oldham 0 0 0 Pemberton Rochdale 11.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WARRINGTON " A "CLEAR ANOTHER OBSTACLE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A "CLEAR ANOTHER OBSTACLE.</p>
        <p>The Warrington "A "took a powerful team with them on Saturday to avenge the only defeat sustained this season, and Wigan Highfield were compelled to bow the knee before a record crowd of their own supporters. Right from the start Warrington showed superiority,both forwards and backs being too good for the Highfleld men. liockenhull opened the score with a smart dropped goal. Maintaining the pressure the Warringtonians again drove home a hot attack, and this time Jordan put, on the finishing touch to a round of passing, "Mclntyre adding the extra points, the visitors leading at half- time by seven points to nil. Turning round the game took an even turn for some time. From a penalty Webster notched a goal for Wigan. Warrington effected a splendid rally in the closing stages, and their victory was made more pronounced when llockenhall raced over after flue passing end McTntrye augmented the point. Final : Warrington, three goals two tries (12 points) ; Wigzin Highfleld, one goal (two points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>WARRINGTON CLUB FINED.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CLUB FINED.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Lelguo Committee on Tuesday, Warrington, for late start at St. Helens were fined 9s. ; Kitson (Bitley) WO suspended to and including February 20th for striking an opponent, versus Runcorn; White (Oldham) misconduct at Widnes, to and including February 20th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>THIRD TEST MATCH TEAM.</head>
        <p>THIRD TEST MATCH TEAM.</p>
        <p>NOTITEIERN.UNION V. AusTrtaidA..--:At a meeting of the Australian Sub-C )mmittee of the Northern Union at Huddersfield on Tuesday, the following team was chosen to represent the Northern Union in the third test match with the Australians at Birmingham on Monday next :—F. Young (Leeds), back; J. Tyson (Oldham), B. Jenkins (Wigan), J. Lomas (Salford), and W. Batten (Hunslet), three-quarter backs ; E. Brooks (Warrineton) and J. Thomas (Wigan), half backs; W. Jukes (Hunslet), Arthur Smith and W. Longworth (Oldham), A. Mann (Bradford), R. Padbury (Runcorn), and F. Boylen (Hull), forwards. Reserves :—Gifford (Barrow), full back ; Miller (Wigan), wing three- quarter; Dickinson (Warrington), centre three-quarter; Lally (Widnes), half back; Morley (Halifax), and Silcock (Wigan) forwards.</p>
