      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>(Continued front page 3.) TUESDAY'S PROCEEDINGS. Mis3ing Document Discovered.</head>
        <p>(Continued front page 3.) TUESDAY'S PROCEEDINGS. Mis3ing Document Discovered.</p>
        <p>Ambrose Wilkinson, 28, Jockey-street, labourer at Messrs. Crosfields, said be was no relation to Elsie Wilkinson. He saw James Humphries near the Jockey Bridge about 7.35 on election morning. Witness was wearing a red ribbon and when he saw Humphries wearing a blue one he said "Hello, Bungit, have they bought you over ?" Humphries said he had been in the The inquiry was resumed on Tuesday when Mr. Byrne Jockey with a" bloke" who had spent is. 9d. and that ' be had a share of it. Witness asked him to come and was present on behalf of the Public Prosecutor. Before vote for the right man, and he pulled the blue ribbon out of Humphries' coat and replaced it with a red one. any evidence was taken, Mr. W. Madden, leading counsel for the respondent, Witness had two pieces of red ribbon in his pocket. made an announcement with regard to the missing They went together to Mr. nudge's house in Long- document about which there has been so much corn- ford-street and afterwards along Orford-lane towards enent. He said that when the document was stated by the polling booth. When they reached the Soldiers' him to be missing there was a great deal of jubilant Return Humphries said " Come and treat me " but cross-examination by his friend Mr. Dickens. In regard witness said "No, 1. am having no drink this morning. to the document, he (Mr. Madden)lf said at the time he I am not having any till the election is over." would be very glad to produce it if he could. Mr. F. Humphries went in alone and when he came out they R. Roberts went into the witness-box and gave a both went to the polling booth. Witness never gave history of the document as far as he could remember it. Humphries ls. o treated him to drink. They passed He could not account for its disappearance beyond the Ceuncillor Peacock in Longford-street on the opposite fact that to the best of his recollection he had handed side of the street, and witness shouted " Good morn- the document back to Mr. Peter Peacock. Whe