        <p>When the hearing was resumed on Wednesday, Mrs. • Mary Ann Silcock, 267, Lovely-lane, said that on the day of the election she called on Sarah Ellen Hadcroft to take her to vote, but witness never gave her any- thing. By Mr. Dickens: ram just a little cnrious to know ' what took place between you and Hadcroft in the witness room.—She simply said she had to swaar against me about the sixpence. Was anything said about the sixpence being for lost time ?—Nothing was mentioned of that kind. Mrs. Frederick Peacock said there was no money passed between Mrs. Silcock and the woman Hadcroft in the governess car on the return from the polling booth. MORE ABOUT THE JOCKEY INCIDENT. James Walsh, carrying on business in Tanners-lane, said that on the Monday night after the election he met Humphries in Winwick-street and asked him what he was doing at that time of the night without being washed, to which Humphries replied, "I have just been in yonder office signing a lot of papers" that Jim Rush-</p>
        <p>PRINCIPALS IN THE CASE. Front row Mr. G. C. Rees, barriAter, Air. F. R Roberts, Solicitor, and Mr. Wm. Madden, barrister. Back row: Mr. A. Peacock, Councillor P. Peacock (the Roapondent), and Nit. N. Cook, jun.</p>
        <p>'worth was going to give him £l5 to swear that Peter Peacock had given him is. to vote for him. Humphries was in It drunken condition. Witness denied that he said to Newey, "If you go iu there, there is a job for you." It was pure invention. What made you and Mr. Peacock take up your positi- tion outside Mr. Greenall's office ?—Because we bad got word that Humphries bad-gone in drunk. Before this evening it was common knowledge that Mr. Peter Peacock bad given Humphries a Is. ?—lt was 8 rumour.</p>
        <p>Whan you went into the British Tar did they refuse to serve the three of you ?—No. He did not serve you ?—He refused Humphries and we all came out.</p>
        <p>Why didn't you and Melia have a drink ?—Because it is a custom when one is refused for the others to have none.</p>
        <p>Did you think Humphries was very drunk ?--T did. You, an old publican, going into a public house with a man who was drunk and one of your own party to ask for drinks ;. isn't that fueny ?—fdon't know that it is.</p>
        <p>How was it Wood and Petcock also found themselves at the Jockey afterwards ?—They live down there. Do you mean to tell the Commissioner that five of you were outside the Jockey at an identical time by accident ?—Wood lives down that road.</p>
        <p>I suggest it was to get hold of Humphries if you could ?—No answer.</p>
        <p>By the Commissioner: When Humphries went into the Horse and Jockey drunk why didn't you try to follow him and get him out ?—I did not think it was my business. The Commissioner: It is a pity you did not, I must say.—lf a man gets drunk he gets stupid and I had been lugging him along and was getting sick of it.</p>
        <p>SOME OF THE RESPONDENT'S WITNESSES. Front Row : Mrs. P. Peacock, Mr. J. Ho'wes, jun. (Liberal Agent), and Elsie Wilkinson. Back row: James Walsh, Alf. Wood, and Arnbros.) Wilkinson.</p>
