      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>THURSDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>The closing stage of the case was entered upon on Thursday when counsel addressed the Court. MR. MADDEN'S CLOSING SPEECH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Madden, for the respondent, after acknowledging Mr. Dicken' eminence in election law said fourteen charges of bribery and fourteen of treating were origin- ally brought but several had been dropped. He submitted that the charges of the alleged payment of sixpence to Mrs. Lawrinson, sixpence to S trail Helen Hadcroft and a shilling to James Humphries were the only cases of bribery which it had really been at-erapted•to prove. They were the only charges that could be said from the legal point of view to be charges of bribery, because all the others properly came under the head of treating. Bribery in itself was corrupt hat treating was not necegslrily corrupt; it might be quite an innocent thing according to the spirit in which it was done. As to bribery, the law was absolutely clear that the bribe must be given with the idea of in lacing a person to vote or to refrain from voting. According to the evidence no attempt was ever made to induce Mrs. Lawrinson or Miss Hadcroft to vote at all. According to Mr. Justice Intvrance "The money must be given to the other side in order to draw a vote." Dealing with the question of agency, Mr. Madden said that agency to raake the respondent liable must be agency to get votes. In cases of a small amount, when there was no systematic bribery, there must be the strongest proof to justify the Commissioner taking the responsibility of hmeating a man who held the seat by the favour of a large majority and a hundred nominators. To bring</p>
        <p>borne agency, they had to prove that the person bad authority from the respondent or that the respondent accepted or adopted what he did. So far as Frank Hughes was concerned he had no authority from the respondent, who did not even know what he was doing. The three charges be had mentioned were the only ones that had the slightest substance. With regard to treat- ing, whether Alf Wood, Abraham Smart, George Henry Perry, Herbert Davies, Ambrose Wilkinson and Robert Pemberton were agents or not, it did not matter a single straw because there was the positive evidence of those men that they did not not treat thc:u upon the occasions alleged. In the course of the case there had been a great many written statements or declarations made. A man's testimony did not gain any more value from the fact that it was put in writing and made before three or four people. They were merely witnesses to the state- ment and net to the fact. Seca statements were always to be received with a certain amount of suspicion, and he would quote the words of Nfr. Justice Grantham in a case where a man had made a statement for the other side:— _</p>
        <p>I cannot help thinking that this evidence was obtained in a way in which it ought not to have been obtained. The method does not commend itself to we and I doubt very much the value of the evidence as offered on behalf of the petitioner. It is easy for a person who wants to get evidence to take a statement down in his own language and give to it an entirely different effect to that intended by the person making it. We find con- stantly that persons sign statements without know- ing what they have signed." Continuing Mr. Madden said it was a pity legislation did not exist making it illegal that such statements shouldbe taken or atlany rate, they should be made before some public official specially appointed for that purpose. The witnesses for the petitioner were drawn, from those who were interested in the drink trade or immediately under its influence. Those who suppartel Mr. Peacock were in favour of sobriety, industry and good government. When James lliimphries said Mr. Peacock gave him a shilling and Mr. Peacock said it was a lie, they must bring into strong contrast the character of the two men. The allegations were borne out by people dragged from the slums of Orford Ward —by the " Bungits," the Cleaves, the Knights, the Fisher, the Baileys, by these products of the public- houses and of education. These were the only kind of witnesses who could be called to prove a crucial charge in this case. All the rest was padding. Were they going to take the word of the unfortunate girl Hadcroft, who contradicted herself in the box, against the word of a respectable, clean, industrious woman like Mrs. Silcock ? He (Mr. Madden) denied all the charges and would not shirk one. The other side had struck out three or four charges because, he supposed, they could not prove them, although they had hazarded the charges in the same way in which they hazarded the petition. The petitioners, continued Mr. Madden, had had three solid months to get up their charges in this petition, and this straightforward man (Peacock) had only live days to meet them. It was not fair or just, even if it was the law, to the respondent, that he should only have five days to find evidence to meet those maglignant, cruel and diobolical charges. Lastly Mr. Madden dealt with the charge against Mr. Peacock of giving a shilling to James Humphries—or Floyd, or" Bungit." He did not know whether it was really necessary seriously to go into this case. He did not know whether, after what had been said in the box by the respectable witnesses be had called—Mr. Peacock, Mr. Wright, J.P. and others—the petitioner's solicitors and advisers did not at the present moment feel that they ought in common decency to drop this charge. They had nothing beyond the evidence of this man—the poor, unhappyvunfortunate creature who was admittedly a drunkard, who was helped home at nights by some of his kind and benevolent friends. Had Mr. Peacock to be held to have been guilty of corruption upon the evidence of a tainted and already corrupted witness? He did not want to make any charge against the brewers in this matter except that as far as the evidence was concerned, it had come out that the banner under which they fought was " Cheap ale, Nellie, cheap ale." Oh, that ho had the eloqusnce of the illustrious father of his learned friend, who had described in the glorious pages of" Boz "what the meaning of gin palaces at the corners of -streets was, and the darkened homes that went along them. He had painted it in a way he (Mr. Madden) could not hope to picture and yet; mark the strange irony of fate, that throup professional interests the other side had to be championed to-day by the son of that illustrious preacher on the subject. If it was only a small bribe or if there was no systematic bribery the Commissioner must hesitate long before he unseated a gentleman of the position of Mr. Peacock. He had won his fight fairly, and his laurels were not to be torn from hi a brow inconsiderately or without thought. When Mr. Madden concluded his speech, which had lasted four-and-a-half hours, there was applause in the gallery, which was immediately suppressed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>MAGISTRATE IN THE BOX.</head>
        <p>MAGISTRATE IN THE BOX.</p>
        <p>Mr. Samuel Wright said he had been a justice of the peace for sixteen years. Witness administered the oath to Humphries and tbld him that it was a serious matter, and that he must tell the truth. Ilumphries replied that he now wanted to tell the truth as he was drunk when he signed the first paper. A few days after the election witness went with Peter Peacock and George Gillen to the house of Mrs. Lawrinson. She made a statement to witness that some men and women came to her house, and, under a threat that they would bring a magistrate and a policeman, made her sign a paper to the effect that she had received sixpence. Mr. Peacock wanted her to sign another paper, but she would not sign, as she was afraid. Arthur Sanders, 275, Lovely-lane,cornmercial traveller, spoke to going to Mr. Wrights'. Humphries was quite sober.</p>
        <p>George Henry Perry, 33, Roome-street, said be was a Conservative but worked for Mr. Peacock. On the morning of the election he root G leave wearing a piece of blue ribbon in his coat. Witness made use of the remark : "What are you doing with that d-thing in your coat. I have turned my colours for Mr. Peacock." lie never said anything about a drink. In the end Gleave took Rushworth's colour out of his coat and tore it up. _ __ _</p>
        <p>Tho offer of a pint wonld have persuaded him to vote wonld indeed. Weave was three sheets in the wind when he met him.</p>
        <p>He was just in the humour to be persuided ?—Yes. Did you say he could have a pint for nothing ?—No.</p>
        <p>DRINKING IN THE SOLDIERS' RETURN.</p>
        <p>Herbert Myles, 29, Wlnwick-road, greengrocer,.stated that he did not treat Billow and Stockton during the fortnight preceding the election. He did not receive money to stand drinks from Mr. Arthur Peacock, Mr. Peacock,Robert Pemberton, or Alf Wooda.On the night of the election witness wont to theSolcliers'Return about 8.10 and Mr. Arthur Peacoock subsequently catne in. Mr. Peacock had a drink and went out.</p>
        <p>By Mr. Dickens : Witness was working for Mr. Peacock a fortnight before the election. He stood drinks in the Soldiers' Return, probably a few nights before the election, to celebrate winning a bet. The money he spent was all his own, and the affair had nothing to do with the election. It was done "out of the pure largeness of his heart." The witness was so cheerful in the box that Mr.' Dickens remarked : "You must be happy Jim. You are the best tempered man I have had in the box yet. I am glad I have met you." Witness created great laughter by stating in answer to NIL Rees, that he considered the most important part of a newspaper was that which dealt with race- horses. Those who won bets were generally large- hearted and stood drinks. (lAughter.) Robert Pemberton, 17, Winwick-road, greengrocer, son-in-law of the last witness, corroborated his state- ments.</p>
        <p>James Lewis, 41, Forster-street, gaswoker, stated that at 2 p.m. on election day he saw a motor car stop opposite Mrs. Lawrinson's house on the same side of the street. Miss Elsie Wilkinson, who was carrying a book, knocked at the door and went inside. Ho did not see anything pass between Miss Wilkinson ani Mrs. Lawrinson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>STORY OF A LOAN.</head>
        <p>STORY OF A LOAN.</p>
        <p>William 11.3,lands Alcock, 80, West-street, labourer, denied offering 13 'Hoy a drink to influence his vote, but said he lent him a shilling. _</p>
        <p>By Mr. Swift : When Bailey asked for the shilling he told witness he was thirsty.</p>
        <p>SWEETS FOR CHILDREN. COMMISSIONER AND UNDESIRABLE ADVERTISING.</p>
        <p>Frank Hughes, master confectioner, said he had various shops in the town. On tho morning of election day he got into a motor car—he presumed it was one of Mr. Peacock's. Ha 111C1 tw3 pockets full of sweets and as the car was going he threw out the sweets to the public. Ho was only in the car a few minutes.</p>
        <p>Mr. Madden: You threw them out indiscriminately? —Certainly. Had you over been asked to do such a thing ? Never.</p>
        <p>Had Mr. Peacock the slightest knowledge that you were doing such a thing ?—Not to my knowledge. Witness, continuing. said he did not see anyone else throwing sweets. He did not mix coins amongst the sweets and he distributed them simply to advertise his business.</p>
        <p>By Mr, Dickens: One of Mr. Peacock's canvassers was in the car from which ho threw the sweets.</p>
        <p>May I take it as a fact that you took the opportunity of advertising your business only in Peacock's cars.— You did not use any of Mr. Rushworth's ?—No I did not.</p>
        <p>Mr. Madden : Ihd "Intelligent Jim" issued a placard or something saying he had got the Catholic vote You thought you would checkmate that ?—Yes You are the Secretary of the Catholic Association ? —Yes.</p>
        <p>The Commissioner: I don't want to say anything more but I am bound to say that I think you did a very foolish thing in associating yourself with one political party in distributing your sweets. I hope that mode of advertising won't happen in any other election in Warrington or elsewhere. This concluded the case for the respondent.</p>
        <p>For Children's tacking Cough at night, Wood's Great Peppsrmint Cure. Is. 11. 3s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>MR. DICKENS' LIST WORD.</head>
        <p>MR. DICKENS' LIST WORD.</p>
        <p>Mr. DICKENS, in his reply, reviewed the whole of the case and dwelt upon the salient points of the evidence in each charge. Ile said he was not going to follow the heated argument of his learned friend. He was not, there to address the gallery, but to address the Com- missioner. They had bad a very eloquent peroration but it entirely begged the question. With reference to those people who had formed an opinion about Mr. Peter Peacock, such as Mr. Madden had tried to put before the Court, all he could say was that they had not read the evidence in this case, and when they had read it, he ventured to think that anything more despicable than the conduct of Mr. Peter Peacock throughout this cue he had never known. He had allowed all</p>
        <p>these wretched witnesses to go into the box and lie, himself a party to what was a gross conspiracy in the case of Humphries, and he (Mr. Dickens) would show that if the Conrt was to believe the witueSSes who had no interest in this matter, Mr. Peter Peacock had lied. }laving explained the reasons i why several cases had been dropped, Mr. Dickens , said he had never heard in all his life such a proposition put forward as that to have bribery there must be a contract. With all deference to Mr. Madden, it was ridiculous. Bribery ' might be done without a single word in connection with the election. It might be done by inference and after the event. Bribery might be after a direct promise but it was equally bribery whether there was any direct understanding between them or not. When his friend talked about money being given after the event, he must surely have lost sight of that very salutary part of the statutory definition, that bribery was not only payment of money for a vote but pay- ment for having voted—about as dangerous a form of bribery as they could have. As to the diffesence between treating and bribery his learned friend was perfectly wrong. They could not have bribery without a corrupt intention—that intention being to win a vote. Treating was exactly the same. Having gone fully into several cases,Mr. Dickens sub- mitted that the agency had been established in the case of Elsie Wilkinson and Frank Hughes. It was fatal to tile respondent's case if agency could be established, because after the dramatic scene with Sarah Ellen Hadcroft, who was got over by the other side in a , shameful and disgraceful way, no one could doubt the , truth of that story. Mr. Pe tcnck prided himself upon fighting beer. He had made a good use of that material in this election. Lot him go on fighting beer, but let , him fight it without using beer as a weapon, if he was to be consistent. A remarkable circumstance was that , in practically every case, all the facts leading up to and , following upon the act were admitted. Mr. Dickens proceeded to deal with the cases seriatim and in touching upon the Humphries case he said P.C. Newey did his duty and his evidence showed that the Commissioner could not depend upon what any one of the four men who contradicted him said. Mr. Dickens characterised the story as to the alleged incident at the Jockey as a double-dyed, concocted, ridiculous staten:ent. He ventured to think that when he (Mr. Dickens) had gone through the whole of the case—and this was the only time he would say anything not directly addressed to the Commissioner—there would be many good Liberals who would be rather glad to get rid of Mr. Peter Peacock. Mr. Dickens had spoken two hours when the Court adjourned until Friday morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>YESTERDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</head>
        <p>YESTERDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dickens, continuing his speech for the petitione dealt at great length with the Hadcroft and Humphrie cases. He said it WAS a grelt shams that Elsie Wilkinson was allowed to go into the witness-box and tell the story she did. The case against Frank Hughes was dropped, owing to his straightforward way of giving evidence, and the Commissiener said he was glad this course had been taken as he might otherwise have had to take a serious view of the nutter.</p>
        <p>Mr. DICKHNS, in concluding, said that since the case began it had assumed proportions which no-one could by any possibility have supposed it would assume, and that was caused entirely by the attitude of the other side. It was a plain and easy case of bribery and treat- ing, which had been complicated by the tactics of the other side, of getting hold of the petitioner's witnesses, of trying to turn the charge against Mr. Peacock into nothing by bullying the man Humphries. In every way they had tried, by using hard language, by putting their counsel up to condemn everybody, to attack everybody, to induce the Court to come to the conclusion that the charges hard failed. He (Mr. Dickens) ventured to think they had wholly failed in their attempt and it would have been much better had Mr. Madden looked at home before he used such language, for it re-acted against himself. He (Mr. Dickens) could honestly sit down by repeating what he said when he opened the case—throughout the whole proceedings the respondent's counsel had not been able to prove—he was not talking about bare assertions to a public gallery—one single thing against the petitioner's side of which any honest or reason- able man could complain. The petitioner had fought the case with proper weapons, fairly and openly, without bribes, promises or inducements. He asked the Commissioner, with the greatest possible confidence, to say that the charges he had pressed had been proved up to the hilt, including the charge against Peter Peacock himself and he ventured to hope that the Commissioner's judgment would be in accordance with what he (Mr. Dickens) had suggested—that these were charges not lightly made, not made without con- sideration, but based upon a real basis, namely the basis of truth.</p>
        <p>At the close of Mr. Dieken's speech, which had occupied altogether five hours, the Commissioner intimated that he would give his judgment at 10.15 on Monday morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>So many of the tabloid comedies and operettas on the music hall stage are thin and devoid of either music or sustained interest that it is a pleasure to come across one like Jake Friedman's " Happy Holland," which is produced at the Warrington Hippodrome this week. The operetta is fully entitled t ) the description, for the singing and dancing are really excellent, and the mounting of the piece most artistic and in keeping with" Happy Holland." There is a strong company of over twenty performers and each and all take a considerable part in the operetta. There is a full chorus and a ballet of quite a:nbitious pretensions. The part of Jakey is well taken by 11-. Friedman himself, and in both singing and acting he is well-backed up by Miss Florence Huntoa who makes a pretty tied vivacious Gretchen. The Yankee, the Frenchman, and the Dutch "Pa-pa " are all good and "Happy Holland " goes with a capital swing to entertaining music. A return visit to Warrington is made by the merry and popular Stble Fern, whose clear singing of catchy songs wins her hearty applause. A. unique item is pro- vided by Belle Davies with " her Pick Chick Actors." Belle is a handsome coloured lady, and the " pick chicks " are a trio of juvenile darkie3 whose capacity for fun is prodigious, and whose dancing has to be seen to be believed. They might be built of piano-wire and watch-springs. Their's is one of the best turns in the bill. Frank H. Fox is above the average as a dude comedian, his songs being full of go and really well sung. Mack °Hive and Daisy Baldry produce a " whistling scena." A capital representation of an electrically illuminated chocelate shop on high class lines forms the " scena," and Mark 011ive's whistling does the rest. He is a sifflear of no mean powers, and is reported to have presented the turn over 1,000 times in the famous comic opera " The Belle of New York." Clarice Clarke, vocalist, and biograph pictures of great merit and originality complete the programme, save and excepting that the orchestra has a good deal to do with the enjoyment of the whole entertainment. There are some very tuneful accompaniments skilfully played.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRA.MXIE.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRA.MXIE.</p>
        <p>Next week's bill is beaded by Ted Waite, the spasmodic comedian, and other attractions will he : Gallimore Trio, comedy artistes ; the Fordyces, singers and dancers ; Ruth Molloy ; Gus Garrick, the celebrated mimic ; Feldman's Beau-Ideal Troupe of Refined Juveniles ; Kinnard, the expert magician ; Lillie Luraine,musical speciality artiste.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>GUARDIANS' NEMO FUND.</head>
        <p>THE GUARDIANS' NEMO FUND.</p>
        <p>Mr. 11. J. Longton, solicitor, appeared on behalf of the Property Owners' Association on Tuesday before the District Auditor of Poor Law Union accounts, and raised the question of the notorious Nemo Fund. The Auditor said the question was a difficult one in law whether he could cm-lapel the Guardians or their Treasurer to bring the accaunt in for him to audit. There was no law compelling the Guardians to keep a banking account at all, and it might be argued that they sere entitled to keep any interest earned upon the banking account. Mr. Longland contended further that the Guardians carried too large a balance and called up too much of the ratepayers' money. The interest was thus excessive. The Auditor said he would consider the point, along with those respecting the 1130 of the interest generally through the Nemo Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>.ROYAL COURT THEATRE</head>
        <p>.ROYAL COURT THEATRE</p>
        <p>Pantomime Next Week.</p>
        <p>A special attraction has been secured for next week in Mr. Edwin C. Jason's grand pantomime "Dick Whittington." The production will be on a gorgeous scale, with many novel turns, and there slyuld be crowded houses. Miss Mande Beaumont will appear as " Dick "and Fred Ward as "Jack." There will be a matinee on Saturday. After the match Warrington v. Australians to-day (Saturday), the members of the Australian team will attend the Warrington Dramatic Society's performance of the "Lady of Ostend "at the Court Theatre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>FREE TRADE.</head>
        <p>FREE TRADE.</p>
        <p>MR. G. D. KELLY, hi P., ADVISES WORKINGMEN TO UPHOLD IT.</p>
        <p>The Free Trade League has organized an active campaign in Warrington in support of Free Trade, and the first of a series of public meetings was held in Cairo-street Schools on Thursday evening. Mr. S. Rigby Armitage presided, and wag supported on the platform by Mr• G. D. Kelly, M.P., Rev. C. Harvey Cook, Messrs. J. Kenworthy, A. H. Rathbone and R. Wyllie, There was a good attendance. The Cif ilftlfA.l nal the following letter from Captain G. It Crosfield :—" Daar Mr. Armitage,—l much regret that I am unable to be present at the Free Trade League meeting either to-day or to-morrow, owing to prior engagements. I regret this, because I should like to hive been present to support a fiscal policy, which I believe is the right and proper one for a country situated as we are. In any case the burden of proof rests with those who advocate a change, and I do not think the Protectionists have by any moans proved their case. My feeling is that if Pro- tection " were introduced into this country it would bolster up a good many industries which are suffering, not from our fiscal system, but from the fact that they are behind the times. Undoubtedly, it is not difficult to point to special cases where a tariff would be helpful, but we know once a tariff is introduced it is not going to stop at special cases, but will become general. There is one argument against the introduction of Pro- tection which is not often used, but is, to my mind, a very important one. It is this : There is little doubt that Free Trade is conducive to peace. ft we intro- duce Protection, war may be precipiatated, and we are not prepared for war." _</p>
        <p>Tile CHAIRMAN, in proof of the argument that a tariff would raise the price to the consumer, said Sir Michael Hicks Beach, the late Conservative Chancellor of the Exchequer, showed that a slight increase of is. on wheat was sufficient to cause a rise in the price of a loaf. Mr. Wyndham at Liverpool told us that one of the first acts of a Tory Government would be to put a tariff on corn, flour and wood in favour of Canada. We would have nothing whatever to gain by such a policy. All the taxes we put on would come out of our own pockets. Mr. Armitage referred to the strong Free Trade feeling amongst the farmers of the Western States of Canada and the United States and said that he had been asked by many of them "Why are you dabbling again with Protection ?" They were the great Protec- tionists of the world but still they were only held to Protection by the great forces of the great cities. There was another pretty theory put before us by the Protectionists of to-day which was not quite in lino even with the ideas advocated in the first instance by Mr. Chamberlain in Glasgow. Mr. Chamberlain spoke firmly but very moderately as to what he would like to have and he did not intend that it should be a vigorous Protectionist tariff on everything. Mr. Chamberlain, whose illness they all regretted, had lost the reins or we should never have had the clamour that we had to-day amongst irresponsible men for a tariff on every- thing, irrespective of the needs of our working-people and of the needs of a large section of the community who were dependent upon fixed incomes. They wanted us to try again the terrible curse that the land groaned under in the forties. Were we all going to be docked in our wages bill up to 25 per cent., as they were in Germany, for the sake of tariffs? These gentlemen told us we were going to have far more work and better wages. Warrington men were not fools; he meant to say that a great many of them were thinkers. The craze of football rather sapped the reeding capacity of many men, while in another class golf sapped it, and there were too many golfers who did not know how to read solid literature consistently. The time of one class was taken up with golf and the other with football, much to the detrimeet of the country. Alr.Armitage showed how the tariff would increase the cost of pro- duction in many industries and hamper manufacturers by raising the price of many things which they used in their works. He was afraid the good feeling that now existed between employers and workmen would be broken down by a Protectionist tariff because the masters must inevitably demand a lower rate of wages to meet the markets and the men would be asking for higher pay to keep the wolf from the door. The Rev. C. HARVEY COOK moved : "That this meeting affirms its uashaken belief in the principles of Free Trade adopted nearly forty years ago and expresses its conviction that Free Trade is now more than ever necessary for the well-being of the United Kingdom." He said that until our business men and manufacturers realised that business was a branch of ethics, we should not understand the dignity of business or the religiousness of commerce. He was absolutely sick of hearing that "business is business and religion is religion." lie wee tired to death of hearing men say that the pulpit and the man in the pulpit must not touch the things of the world. He believed that the man in the pulpit, while not dealing with such issues as Free Trade there, ought to take his part as a man who was interested in human movements and in world movements such as !elle, wEaoh involved great moral Issues, lie ought to come out of the pulpit and take his part in the rough and tumble of life. (Applause.) The world's business relationships struck deep into moral issues. He wanted U 3 to learn how to be big, how to use power, how to carry-corn, but so far as ho read the signs of the times many of those who were opposed to Free Trade principles minted to be bumptious rather than big, aggressive rather than powerful, and to be kicking the traces rather than carrying corn. The fiscal question presented itself in his judgment as a humanitarian and ethical issue. It was not simply a question of a change of policy but of principle. He might be in a minority among his Liberal friends but he believed Mr. Bilfour saw the moral issue and Chat was why he did not give a direct lead towards Protection, because he realized ' that there were spiritual principles underlying the thing they called Free Trade. He (the speaker) traced in the fiscal issue other and auguster things than fiscal politics. Retaliation must retard the intercourse of nations and whatever did that was wrong in principle and wrong in practice. Free Trade brought about that relationship between the nations of the earth which acted as an engine of reform and of Fellowship. Tariff Reform' retaliation or anything j which made men want to hit men back was out or harmony with a world democracy. The principle of retaliation did not make for universal brotherhood, and if he belonged to the Labour party— and he did in a sense—he would advocate what he I thought they were trying strenuously to do, and that was to create a sense of world fellowship of workers. 'Free Trade was more than ever necessary for the moral j well-being as well as the social and political well-being jof the United Kingdom, and for international good- will. Tariff Reform he maintained, wonld make the rich men richer and the workers poorer. (Applause). Ntr. G. D. KELLY, M.P., in seconding, said he was there as a Labour man looking at the subject from the ideas and standpoint of the Labour Party. They were there to defend themselves against a common enemy that was embraced in the title of "tariff reform." They were asked to surrender their present conditions and go 'ack to the dark and dismal time which the instigator of tariff reform was once wont to paint in vivid colours. Were they going back to it? (Cries of "No.") As one who had fought the battle of labour he was going to hold on to Free Trade while there was any breath left in him. (Applause.) If Free Trade was such a failure as it was alleged to be, if it had entailed so much misery through lack of employment, how was it that the trade and commerce of the country had increased to such an extent ? Tn fifty years we had increased from 152 millions to G 46 millions in imports and from 97 millions to 426 millions in exports. Mr. Kelly combatted the theory that imports were paid for in gold, pointing out that they were'paid for by exports, and an excess of imports over exports meant that there was extra profit and extra money in this country. We had no need whatever to be afraid of imports. To classify our imports, 231 millions related to food of men or animals, representing about 42 per cent., 189 millions were raw materials. and 78 millions partly manufactured materials. Of the manufactures sent here ready for popular consumption, 25 per cent, were necessaries of life and 25 per cent. wines and other luxuries. Out of a total importation for home con- sumption of 554 millions, 90 per cent was food for man, animals or factories, and nine per cent. consisted of manufactures, ready for popular con- sumption. What wes the Tariff Reformer going to put the tax upon ? In Germany, which was so often held up as an example by Protectionists, the people worked a greater number of hours per week for smaller wages, and their conditions of life were not equal to those in this country. On the occasion of his recent visit to that country he found a great deal of unemployment. The German workingmen received the peace deputation from England as if they had been angels from above, and eagerly swallowed every word they uttered in favour of peace,and explained the strong desire they had for Free Trade. As a worker and a friend of the workers be (Mr. Kelly) wished to say that one of the greatest mistakes would be made by the people of this country if they allowed themselves to be cajoled into voting for any man who embraced within his political programme Tariff Reform. (Applause.) The workers would be in a much worse position if they accepted Tariff Reform and he did not care whether a man was a Liberal, a Tory or a Socialist, if he consulted his own ieterests and the welfare of his wife and family he could not possibly vote for Tariff Reform but was bound to stick to the principle of Free Trade, (Applause). The resolution was carried unanimously. Mr. KIINWORTHY in moving a vote of thanks to the Chairman and speaker, emphasised the harm which tariff reform would do the Lancashire cotton trade. Mr. BATHBONN seconded. He said they had inherited a great cause and each one could do his share to main- tain it. An elementary knowledge of the economics of trade and commerce 'has sufficient to enable them to expose the fallacies of tariff reform. The vote was heartily accorded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Meeting at Brick-street.</head>
        <p>Meeting at Brick-street.</p>
        <p>Last evening a meeting was held in Brick-street Mission under the presidency of Mr. H. ROBERTS who said that if a man was desirous of the greatest good for the greatest number and would intelligently consider</p>
        <p>com:=69</p>
        <p>the principles upon which Free Trade was based and the facts by which it was supported, be was bound tO become in the long run a Free Trader, whatever his political principles might be. He quite admitted that there was a great deal said which at the first blush would appear to justify a policy of Pro- tection. It did not matter whether the term used was tariff reform, reciprocity or fair trade, the whole thing turned upon the proposal to impose u duty not for revenue purposes—that was one of the red herrings that was drawn across the path—but for Protective purposes pure and simple, and if a man WO engaged in a business which was hit by foreign com- petition, and if his whole interest was in that business and he could not allow his mind to extend beyond the confines of that business, he (Mr. Roberts) could quite understand him becoming a tariff reformer, but the muli who had the general weal of the general public at heart could not be anything but a Free Trader. The cottoe trade under a system of Protection would be squelched. out in a few years, and the soap trade would be seriouslir Injured, if not entirely destroyed. We were a manu- facturing and exporting nation and anything which added to the cost of manufacture reduced the capacitY of the British nation to compete successfully for the trade of the rest of the world. T.iriff Reform was no remedy for unemployed and it would not to the extelA of sixpence improve the position of any workingnaao, except temporarily ft nel only then in one of those favourea industries which would for a time benefit by a systetu of Protection at the expense of the community aS whole. (Applause.) Mr. W. El. SElEfinurrs moved and Mr. G. D. KELLY' M.P., seconded a resolution affirming unshaken belief i 3 Free Trade and it was enthusiastically carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>FORTHCOMING MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>FORTHCOMING MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>Next Thursday evening a meeting in the ThewliS° street school will be presided over by Mr. John Upsori and addressed by Mr. John Whitworth (Bowdon), gr. H. J. Leach (Aanchester.) On Friday evening gr. James Dodds will preside at a meeting in Silver-street schools, and the speakers will include Mr. Edward Wakefield (formerly C)lonial Seortarv, Nev Z3Alan4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>TARIFF REFORM.</head>
        <p>TARIFF REFORM.</p>
        <p>The Challenge to a Debate.</p>
        <p>The following reply has been sent by the lion- secretaries of the "%riff Reform League to the hon.- secretaries of the Progreisive Labour League in regard to the challenge to debate Tariff Reform.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>copy.</head>
        <p>copy.</p>
        <p>Dear Sirs,—We are in r-a-celp-t of your letter and are sorry we misunderstood your previous communication. We do not think that any useful purpose would be served by the importation of outside speakers, probablf at a Leavy tariff, to discuss the question. As believer' in " home industries " we think that the people of Warrington have sufficient ability to fight this matter out amongst themselves. Ws must therefore adhere to. the terms of our original offer and our challenge still holds good.</p>
        <p>- Yours faithfullv, GEORGE E. MACKEY ) Joint G. H. WARBUR TON STEEL j Secretaries.. To Messrs. J. Morton and J. Howes, Hon. Secs. Progressive Labour League, Bewsey-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-02-13_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>LICENSING SESSIONS.—OnIy tbo usual routina busi- ness came forward at the licensing sessions for the Daresbury division held at Stockton Heath on TuesdaY. Superintendent Ennion reported that there were 20 licensed victuallers and three beersellers in the division. There was one licence to 571 inhabitants. Two licensed' victuallers had been proceeded against during the yeaf. and one was convicted. The number of persons pro- ceeded against for drunkenness was 17, as against 23 last year. Seven were residents and ten non-residents. —There was no objection to any of the licence; and they were all renewed.</p>
        <p>SCALDING FATALITY.—An inquest was held at the" Hall Cif6, Stockton Heath, on Monday, by Mr. T. Ridg- way, touching the death under very distressing circarn- stances, of Alfred Jones, aged 4 years and 8 monthsr son of Alfred and Mary Jones, 18, Roman-roady Stockton Heath.—lt appeared that on January 18th, deceased's mother was washing clothes and her son was at the bottom of the yard. lie was playing ball, when he slipped into a bath containing hot water, aucl was' severely scalded about the body.—The jury returned verdict of " Accidental death."</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFS AND STOCKTON HEATH UNITED DANT,. OF HoPE.---Success attended the fourth entertainment of the session which was held in the Pro viience SJhoolroora. on Monday evening. The Bev. J. L. Hughes presideci and was supported on the platform by Mr. Firth, Polk!, Court missionary. A good programme was given, coo;" sisting of recitations by F. I3,swick, G. Nicholls, NS. Bates, C. Saunders, E. Lloyd, E. Phillips, H. Stringer, Grounds, B. Brocklehurst, F. Gamble and Miss E. Hamblett. Master George Saunders delighted all with his coon song " Dinah." The action song, "The Sweeping Brush Brigade," by 12 girls, was well give!) and much enjoyed. Miss Edith Hamblett sang "The' children's home " very sweetly. The dialogues, "OU of date," by Ernest Clark and Clifford Jones, ana "Band of Hope Spectacles," by Mabel Clin, Flo, McDermid and Cissie Ferguson were both exceedingli well given. The event of the evening was the humoronS., dialogue, "Domestic frugality," which was given b 9 the senior members of the S in key Bridges Band of Hope and provoked roars of laughter. An address WO' delivered by Mr. Firth. in which he appealed to the boys and girls to get others to join the Baud of Hope* Mr. G. A. Whitfield was the accompanist.</p>
