        <p>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES. By Veronica. OIL AND BROMOIL PRINTING. The majority of amateurs are very familiar with the sight of oil and bromoil prints at the various exhibi- tions, but comparatively few have any intimate acquaintance with the subject. This method is so adaptable for artistic work that its use is very largely on the increase. An outline of the method of procedure is as follows :—Paper with a coating of gelatine is sensitised in a bichromate of potash or bichromate of ammonia, and is hung up in the dark to dry. When dry it is printed under a negative somewhat in the ordinary way as P.O.P. but the time of printing is very much loss and the image can only be very slightly seen. It is then washed in water for about half an hour, to get rid of all the yellow bichromate stain, and during this time the gelatine will have become considerably swollen. It is then laid on several thicknesses of wet blotting paper, and treated with an oil pigrnont by the aid of specially made brushes. Those parts of the print that have not been acted upon by light, that is, the whites that have been printed underneath the dense parts of the negative, will hold more water in the gelatine film, and the water consequently ' exercises a repellant action to the pigment, whereas it adheres to the other parts that are not con- taining so much water. The ink is applied with a sort of dabbing or hopping movement of the brush, and this is a part of the process that requires much practice, as the resulting picture differs considerably according to the amount of skill displayed in this work. Hitherto, there has been very little information on the subject, except occasional contributions to the photographic journals, and demonstrations before various photo- graphic societies, but there has now been published a most explicit handbook, called "The Oil and Bromoil Processes "by F. J. Mortimer, F.R.P.S. and S. L. Coulthurst, both of whom have for some time past been accepted as first class authorities for dealing with the subject. Explanation is very fully given for all methods of procedure, and anyone who is at all interested in artistic methods of reproduction cannot do better than study this little handbook, as everything is so simply explained. ROOM INTERIORS. Many workers are so impressed with the necessity of taking photographs in a bright light, that, unless they have ever attempted it, the photographing of the interior of a dimly lighted room, appears to them an impossibility, but in the majority .of cases it is only a matter of exposure. Considerable difficulty sometimes arises with regard to focussing owing to the small amount of illumination on the focussing screen, but this can generally be overcome by getting a friend to hold a match or a lighted taper in the various dark corners of the room, and then focussing the light. One thing to guard against is if possible not to point your camera directly towards the window, as otherwise you 'may get great trouble from halation, in spite of taking I the precaution of using backed plates. Exposure, of . course, varies ccnsiderably, according to the illumine- I tion of the room. Some interiors can be taken with an , exposure of about a minute, whilst under other circum- stances the exposure may last several hours, and even in the latter case most oxeallont rtnalta aro often obtained. BORDER PRINTING. Very pleasing effects can be obtained by the masking of the negative in printing, and leaving borders of different shapes and sizes. This is a very easy matter when using printing-out paper, but it you want to print a tint or pattern on your border in using gaslight paper, you then have to be a little more exact in the adjust- ment of your masks, etc. A very useful and interesting little pamphlet, called " Border Printing on Velem Gaslight Paper " has just been issued by Kodak, Ltd. Very explicit directions with illustrations of how to do ' this class of printing correctly is contained therein. Any of those who would like to pursue this subject further send a postcard asking for a free copy, to tke Kodak Company, Clerkenwell, London, E.C. LARGE DEVELOPING DISHES. When the amateur has been to the expense of equipping himself with enlarging apparatus, the question of large dishes is also a somewhat expensive item. For those. who cannot afford the expense of porcelain dishes in I large size, a very economical way of going to work is to get a piece of oil baize, carefully folding it in the corners, so as to make it into the shape of a tray. The bottom and sides can then be strengthened ,eith wood. Of course, this is not quite so convenient for handling as a porcelain tray, hut it is quite practicable, and with care will last quite a good while. REMOVING STOPPERS. To remove a tightly fixed stopper from a glass bottle is often a difficult task, a very common method, of course, being to tap the stopper with a lump of wood. • This is often effectual, but there are times when it is so fixed that other mutes have to be resorted to. One of the best that I have heard of is as follows :—Turn the bottle upside down and insert the top of the stopper only in a solution of ice and water. This change of temperature causes the stopper to bontract, and it can then be easily withdrawn. THE PHOTOGRAPHIC MONTHLY. A MONTHLY MAGAZINE DEVOTED TO BOTH TECHNICAL AND PICTORIAL PHOTOGRAPHY. ARTICLES BY PRACTICAL WORKERS, TECH NI CA L SUI3JECrS SIMPLY EXPLAINED, ADVICE BY POST TO READERS NEEDING ASSISTANCE, PRIZE COMPETITIONS EACH MONTH. PRICE 3D. POST FREE, 4ID. DAWBARN &amp; WARD, LTD., 6, FARRINGDON AVENUE, • LONDON, B.C.</p>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING. WARRINGTON WESLEYAN DEFEAT MANCHESTER Y.M.C.A. Manchester Y.M.C.A.. twenty strong, journeyed to Warrington on Saturday, and had a pleasant run with the Warrington IVesleyans, of whom 21 turned out. Four of the 'Warrington-Club laid strait of about seven miles over a fairly heavy country, about three miles being over:plough and three ruilis road. The Y.M.C.A. showed up very badly in the finish, only two members of their fast pack being present. The results are :-1, W. Thorniley, Warrington ' • 2, J. Massey, Warrington ; 3, H. Gill, Warrington; 4, G. H. Hampson, Manchester; 5, H. Dale, Warrington ; 6, S. Millett, Warrington ; T, W. Grundy, %Carrington; 8, A. Remick, Warrington; 9, 0. Warburton, Warrington ; 10, T. Maskerville, War- rington ; 11, F. Fielding, Manchester ; 12, te. Pluton, Manchester. The points (five to count) were :—War- rington, 17; Manchester Y.M.C,A., 61.</p>
